{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility","id":1149965,"title":"game line compatibility","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-15T11:39:47-07:00","id":1149965,"title":"game line compatibility","content":"I have searched the forums and google for this and couldn't find much,<br /> how inter compatible are the different 20th anniversary game lines, I have vampire, and I'm looking into getting the others, I mean how easy is it to run a game mechanically with both vampire, mage and werewolf PCs? the fluff i can work out but are the games mechanically compatible?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12845-ahather","name":"ahather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12845_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T11:41:55-07:00","id":1149966,"title":"","content":"About as compatible as the games always have been, which is to say not very. You'll still have a mostly compatible base system with the need to go hunting for reasonable crossover mechanic difficulties and such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T14:29:36-07:00","id":1150009,"title":"","content":"If you want crossover, you\u2019re honestly better off with another system entirely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T16:29:20-07:00","id":1150028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150009#post1150009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want crossover, you\u2019re honestly better off with another system entirely.</div> </div> </div> </div>That, or make peace with some games getting hosed in places. This isn't aided by the fact that Vampire hasn't changed its rusty, crumbling crossover guidelines for Werewolf and Mage system topics since about 1993.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T16:33:31-07:00","id":1150030,"title":"","content":"tbh the thing i've heard being the major issue is power levels being so different?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12845-ahather","name":"ahather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12845_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-15T19:33:41-07:00","id":1150050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ahather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150030#post1150030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">tbh the thing i've heard being the major issue is power levels being so different?</div> </div> </div> </div>One of the consequences of Crinos being so daunting to the monsters that Garou fight on a regular basis is that it is also very daunting to most vampires who find themselves going up against werewolves. The themes that either game is pushing also don't help to mollify this, since W:tA is a game of Anyone Can Die where you're expected to risk your neck to maybe untwist the cosmos just a little bit (and thus you'll be risking your life all the time), and V:tM is about holing up and hiding as the years go by and you have to address the question of what you'll stop caring about to persist another night (wherein you try to minimize the times you'll have to risk your unlife)..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-16T19:19:12-07:00","id":1150254,"title":"","content":"Well, the basic rules for each game line, once you get to the 20th anniversary editions are the same. Roll x dice against difficulty y for ability use and damage is similar. The combat capabilities of characters and how the special powers of each group interact are quite different. <br /> <br /> Mages, although very powerful if they can use Spheres effectively and have a strong Arete and Avatar so that they can pull off substantial effects, are normal humans for purposes of damage soaking. Mages need armor or magick to soak lethal damage and rarely have anything that works against aggravated damage. Vampires can be effective at combat, but since a lot of their disciplines require blood points, a vampire isn't going to last long in a fight if they run low on blood. As for Werewolf, some of the werewolves aren't combat monster, but anything in Crinos is a lot more dangerous than the base form. Vampire and Werewolf powers are much more static than Mage magick, so it's a bit easier to see what fight is fair before it happens. A Mage, well that's only limited by their imagination.<br /> <br /> Since each line also has its own lore, in general, the more you mix, the more difficult managing the World of Darkness will be. That being said, one of the best games I was ever in as a player was a Vampire/Mage game. However, everyone in that group was a hardcore gamer who committed to the game and our co-GMs spent hours planning each weekly session. If I were to have any crossover elements, I'd carefully consider which ones work best for the players in question.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-16T20:14:14-07:00","id":1150259,"title":"","content":"Mechanics could be tweaked, but they're functional(mostly). The real problem is like how saur ops explained, how to handle theme. Vampire's gothic horror will just as strange and out of place set next to werewolf's pulse-pounting action, mages subjectivism/trans-humanism, or changelings chaotic wimsy(It goes pretty well with Wraith's nihilistic themes though). You either have to come up with a theme that can accommodate all of that(lovecraftian style oppressive alien horror comes to might), or leave some themes by the wayside to tell your story.<br /> <br /> Not to mention the conflicts in the games different metaplots, as each faction is the center of the world in their own gameline to the point where they're behind every major event that ever happened(was pompeii leveled by an ancient Methuselah, a mad archmage, or a raging incarna?) and have fingers in every consperacy that ever is, was, or will be(yet somehow the books keep trying to tell us that they have little to no knowledge of each other despite each faction having massive magical and mundane resources as well as frequent conflicts/alliances throughout history.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T13:43:09-07:00","id":1150450,"title":"","content":"My experience is that the problems with baseline power levels are more due to the Storyteller not communicating a coherent theme to the players than to the rules. Its not really any worse than when one guy builds a Brujah with physical primary, five dots in brawl and their starting dots in celerity/potence while another one builds a Toreador with social primary (physical tertiary), no dots in any combat abilities at all and their starting dots in auspex/presence.<br /> <br /> Yeah, a combat focused Crinos can rip things up. So can a combat focused vampire with some dots in celerity, fortitude and/or potence (plus some ghouled retainers as backup) or an Akashic with Forces 2, Life 3, Mind 1.<br /> <br /> By contrast, a werewolf theurge with tertiary physical stats and couple dots in brawl might only have Str 5, Dex 4, Stam 5 and a dice pool of 6 for their attacks in Crinos and only 2 rage to take extra actions or heal with. It'd cost a lot of blood, but even a non-combat vampire could hit those stat levels for long enough to escape.<br /> <br /> The problem is when the guy with the Werewolf builds a Get of Fenris Ahroun with physical primary attributes and high Rage, the one with the Vampire builds a Toreador with Auspex, Presence and tertiary physical attributes (because they want primary social and secondary mental) and the Mage builds a Virtual Adept with Correspondence, Entropy and just enough Mind to project into the Digital Web. Not only are they wildly disparate in combat ability, their existences are so removed from each other that just putting them in the same room would be problematic.<br /> <br /> This is mostly a communication problem.<br /> <br /> If the Storyteller let the players know that the planned campaign was going to be dealing with say, dealing with the Unquiet Dead in the wake of the Sixth Great Maelstrom then my group would probably be looking at something like a Silent Strider Theurge, a Giovanni Necromancer and a Euthanatos (w. entropy 3 and spirit 3) so they'd all have an interest in the subject and a means of contributing as well. Similarly, if the Storyteller says they want to run a combat heavy war against the forces of darkness (say a Baali/Nephandi/Black Spiral Dancer team up) then that Ahroun having the company of that aforementioned Akashic and a Vampire sniper with Celerity and his five ex-military ghoul retainers would be a pretty kick-ass crew where everyone's got something to contribute too.<br /> <br /> The biggest hangup once everyone is on the same storytelling page though is just vampires absolute inability to operate during daylight hours. An ordinary human can be active 16-18 hours a day with ease and can get by on just a few hours sleep or even skip a night entirely in a crisis situation. Werewolves with their superior endurance and mages with life magic could go days without sleep if they had to and not have it impact their ability to function.<br /> <br /> By contrast, vampires all but shut down when the sun is up, needing 5 successes on a difficulty 8 humanity check to stay awake for a single scene (after making a difficulty 8 Perception+Auspex check to notice the need to wake up first)... less than that and they're up just 1-4 turns before they collapse into sleep again. During the summer months this means maybe 9 hours a day of activity in someplace like Chicago (and only about 15 hours there even in the dead of winter) and they have to spend part of that time hunting and feeding too.<br /> <br /> Even adopting a nocturnal lifestyle the humans/mages and werewolves are going to have 50-100% more time during a day to actually get stuff done (particularly since their hunt for sustenance involves only the half-dozen steps between the fridge and the microwave) while the vampires are laying around in a coma-like sleep. Their utter combustibility from even indirect sunlight is a trivial mechanical nuisance by comparison (though it is excessive as well... a vampire without at least 3 dots in fortitude will die in less than 30 seconds even at twilight).<br /> <br /> My suggestion for crossovers is to...<br /> <br /> A) Use Mage's underlying cosmology as both the Werewolve's cosmology AND the Vampire cosmology can both be 100% correct at the same time in the Mage cosmology. If a Mage needs an explanation for why the others don't get paradox, its because Vampires are thaumivores that gains the Quintessence they need by draining the life essence of those it feeds on while Werewolves evade paradox because the Delirium they produce causes 98% of the population who even sees them in action to just forget or rationalize it away.<br /> <br /> B) Remove the &quot;must sleep during daylight&quot; limitation (keep the dice pools limited by humanity during daylight, but they don't have to roll anything just to be conscious), have only direct sunlight damage vampires (difficulty 8 to soak with fortitude; difficulty 6 if you can still make out the sun through the clouds) and let them burn blood points off in place of taking sunlight damage (one blood point per health level they'd take; not restricted by generation spending limits). This puts vampires on more even footing with the other characters, though they can still burn to a crisp pretty quickly in direct sun, but running from shadow to shadow and burning off a couple blood points to make it through a sunlit area in an emergency (provided they can make the courage check) is now an option.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T15:06:37-07:00","id":1150476,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150450#post1150450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By contrast, vampires all but shut down when the sun is up, needing 5 successes on a difficulty 8 humanity check to stay awake for a single scene (after making a difficulty 8 Perception+Auspex check to notice the need to wake up first)... less than that and they're up just 1-4 turns before they collapse into sleep again. During the summer months this means maybe 9 hours a day of activity in someplace like Chicago (and only about 15 hours there even in the dead of winter) and they have to spend part of that time hunting and feeding too.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's said a mage with the right spheres could shield a vamp from the sun, Maybe a mage with mind or life could keep said vamp awake?<br /> <br /> Also a campaign in the north during the Long Night, Sounds like an interesting idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T17:52:55-07:00","id":1150509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150450#post1150450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My experience is that the problems with baseline power levels are more due to the Storyteller not communicating a coherent theme to the players than to the rules. Its not really any worse than when one guy builds a Brujah with physical primary, five dots in brawl and their starting dots in celerity/potence while another one builds a Toreador with social primary (physical tertiary), no dots in any combat abilities at all and their starting dots in auspex/presence.<br /> <br /> Yeah, a combat focused Crinos can rip things up. So can a combat focused vampire with some dots in celerity, fortitude and/or potence (plus some ghouled retainers as backup) or an Akashic with Forces 2, Life 3, Mind 1.</div> </div> </div> </div>All Garou have access to Crinos and Rage, however. It's a common, binding element to being a werewolf. Everything else just adds on to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By contrast, a werewolf theurge with tertiary physical stats and couple dots in brawl might only have Str 5, Dex 4, Stam 5 and a dice pool of 6 for their attacks in Crinos and only 2 rage to take extra actions or heal with. It'd cost a lot of blood, but even a non-combat vampire could hit those stat levels for long enough to escape. </div> </div> </div>No, a non-combat vampire means absolutely no combat capabilities, whereas, again, any given Garou or meatier Fera is going to have access to what amounts to a six dot Discipline power (Crinos compares rather well to the Chiropteran Marauder in most capacities, and while it cannot fly, it is much tougher), and to top that off, the facility of Theurges for working with spirits is one of the other foremost advantages of Werewolf. In a match of CLAMP-esque noodle limbed vampire vs Theurge who doesn't work out, the advantage is still with the Theurge.<br /> <br /> (Also, Rage is still Rage, and there are ways to exceed the typical maximum for a character if something particularly threatening or off-putting happens).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is when the guy with the Werewolf builds a Get of Fenris Ahroun with physical primary attributes and high Rage, the one with the Vampire builds a Toreador with Auspex, Presence and tertiary physical attributes (because they want primary social and secondary mental) and the Mage builds a Virtual Adept with Correspondence, Entropy and just enough Mind to project into the Digital Web. Not only are they wildly disparate in combat ability, their existences are so removed from each other that just putting them in the same room would be problematic. </div> </div> </div>Agreed, but the Theurge from the above example would still be able to cause hellish trouble in a fight with the Mage and Vampire, not just because of Crinos, but because of spirits. Also, Werewolf and Mage are the result of flat out violently opposing philosophies that drip down to the way the setting cosmology is set up, so games trying to incorporate both of them have to choose which one to throw under the bus, unless the Mage is a Dreamspeaker or a Craft that has extensive disagreement with the way its own game line works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is mostly a communication problem.<br /> <br /> If the Storyteller let the players know that the planned campaign was going to be dealing with say, dealing with the Unquiet Dead in the wake of the Sixth Great Maelstrom then my group would probably be looking at something like a Silent Strider Theurge, a Giovanni Necromancer and a Euthanatos (w. entropy 3 and spirit 3) so they'd all have an interest in the subject and a means of contributing as well. Similarly, if the Storyteller says they want to run a combat heavy war against the forces of darkness (say a Baali/Nephandi/Black Spiral Dancer team up) then that Ahroun having the company of that aforementioned Akashic and a Vampire sniper with Celerity and his five ex-military ghoul retainers would be a pretty kick-ass crew where everyone's got something to contribute too. </div> </div> </div>Silent Striders are not going to work with vampires under any circumstances; they're one of the two tribes that hates them all (the other being Shadow Lords, who will work with them but attempt to get as many vampires to kill each other as possible, because they have much bad blood from fighting the Tzimisce and others in the Carpathians). The fact that the vampire is a Giovanni instead of a Setite just means that they won't immediately attempt to kill it, though since Striders are often finding themselves righting wrongs to put ghosts to rest, that might lead to tearing said Necromancer vampire apart, too. And, as demonstrated above, Theurges are still quite able to make vampires drop blood masonry from their pants. You'd be better off using another type of Fera for this, such as the Bastet (specifically, the Bubasti), Kitsune, or possibly Ajaba.<br /> <br /> Mostly, though, it is correct that choosing a given tone and setting down concrete campaign notes can vastly change the way that crossover shakes out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A) Use Mage's underlying cosmology as both the Werewolve's cosmology AND the Vampire cosmology can both be 100% correct at the same time in the Mage cosmology. If a Mage needs an explanation for why the others don't get paradox, its because Vampires are thaumivores that gains the Quintessence they need by draining the life essence of those it feeds on while Werewolves evade paradox because the Delirium they produce causes 98% of the population who even sees them in action to just forget or rationalize it away. </div> </div> </div>Actually, you cannot have both Werewolf's cosmology and Mage's cosmology coexist in the same setting. Werewolf gives a hard answer for existence in the form of animism - spirits make reality. Mage ascribes the basis of reality to something else, and puts humanity on more than a little bit of a pedestal in determining what reality is. The two are matter and antimatter, and, fittingly enough, will make your game explode if you try to incorporate both as correct.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-17T19:35:40-07:00","id":1150526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150450#post1150450\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My experience is that the problems with baseline power levels are more due to the Storyteller not communicating a coherent theme to the players than to the rules. Its not really any worse than when one guy builds a Brujah with physical primary, five dots in brawl and their starting dots in celerity/potence while another one builds a Toreador with social primary (physical tertiary), no dots in any combat abilities at all and their starting dots in auspex/presence.<br /> <br /> Yeah, a combat focused Crinos can rip things up. So can a combat focused vampire with some dots in celerity, fortitude and/or potence (plus some ghouled retainers as backup) or an Akashic with Forces 2, Life 3, Mind 1.<br /> <br /> By contrast, a werewolf theurge with tertiary physical stats and couple dots in brawl might only have Str 5, Dex 4, Stam 5 and a dice pool of 6 for their attacks in Crinos and only 2 rage to take extra actions or heal with. It'd cost a lot of blood, but even a non-combat vampire could hit those stat levels for long enough to escape.<br /> <br /> The problem is when the guy with the Werewolf builds a Get of Fenris Ahroun with physical primary attributes and high Rage, the one with the Vampire builds a Toreador with Auspex, Presence and tertiary physical attributes (because they want primary social and secondary mental) and the Mage builds a Virtual Adept with Correspondence, Entropy and just enough Mind to project into the Digital Web. Not only are they wildly disparate in combat ability, their existences are so removed from each other that just putting them in the same room would be problematic.<br /> <br /> This is mostly a communication problem.<br /> <br /> If the Storyteller let the players know that the planned campaign was going to be dealing with say, dealing with the Unquiet Dead in the wake of the Sixth Great Maelstrom then my group would probably be looking at something like a Silent Strider Theurge, a Giovanni Necromancer and a Euthanatos (w. entropy 3 and spirit 3) so they'd all have an interest in the subject and a means of contributing as well. Similarly, if the Storyteller says they want to run a combat heavy war against the forces of darkness (say a Baali/Nephandi/Black Spiral Dancer team up) then that Ahroun having the company of that aforementioned Akashic and a Vampire sniper with Celerity and his five ex-military ghoul retainers would be a pretty kick-ass crew where everyone's got something to contribute too.<br /> <br /> The biggest hangup once everyone is on the same storytelling page though is just vampires absolute inability to operate during daylight hours. An ordinary human can be active 16-18 hours a day with ease and can get by on just a few hours sleep or even skip a night entirely in a crisis situation. Werewolves with their superior endurance and mages with life magic could go days without sleep if they had to and not have it impact their ability to function.<br /> <br /> By contrast, vampires all but shut down when the sun is up, needing 5 successes on a difficulty 8 humanity check to stay awake for a single scene (after making a difficulty 8 Perception+Auspex check to notice the need to wake up first)... less than that and they're up just 1-4 turns before they collapse into sleep again. During the summer months this means maybe 9 hours a day of activity in someplace like Chicago (and only about 15 hours there even in the dead of winter) and they have to spend part of that time hunting and feeding too.<br /> <br /> Even adopting a nocturnal lifestyle the humans/mages and werewolves are going to have 50-100% more time during a day to actually get stuff done (particularly since their hunt for sustenance involves only the half-dozen steps between the fridge and the microwave) while the vampires are laying around in a coma-like sleep. Their utter combustibility from even indirect sunlight is a trivial mechanical nuisance by comparison (though it is excessive as well... a vampire without at least 3 dots in fortitude will die in less than 30 seconds even at twilight).<br /> <br /> My suggestion for crossovers is to...<br /> <br /> A) Use Mage's underlying cosmology as both the Werewolve's cosmology AND the Vampire cosmology can both be 100% correct at the same time in the Mage cosmology. If a Mage needs an explanation for why the others don't get paradox, its because Vampires are thaumivores that gains the Quintessence they need by draining the life essence of those it feeds on while Werewolves evade paradox because the Delirium they produce causes 98% of the population who even sees them in action to just forget or rationalize it away.<br /> <br /> B) Remove the &quot;must sleep during daylight&quot; limitation (keep the dice pools limited by humanity during daylight, but they don't have to roll anything just to be conscious), have only direct sunlight damage vampires (difficulty 8 to soak with fortitude; difficulty 6 if you can still make out the sun through the clouds) and let them burn blood points off in place of taking sunlight damage (one blood point per health level they'd take; not restricted by generation spending limits). This puts vampires on more even footing with the other characters, though they can still burn to a crisp pretty quickly in direct sun, but running from shadow to shadow and burning off a couple blood points to make it through a sunlit area in an emergency (provided they can make the courage check) is now an option.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd agree with most of that.<br /> <br /> For the OP: honestly, if you want to do crossover, just go for it and don't listen to the naysayers! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The 20ths are reasonably compatible, by-the-large. The bigger problem is indeed miscommunication, or the lack of communication between the players and the ST, I'd say too.<br /> <br /> Yes, you would have to manage several different systems for supernatural powers, hence yes, you'd have to make some rules calls, but that's fine. Yes, with a crossover, you probably won't go for any one games's particular themes as much as you'd with a one-splat-only game and you'd have to make compromises, but IMO, it's worth it and I don't see it as big a deal as some people tend to make it.<br /> <br /> About power levels: I think that also tends to be exaggerated. All splats have their strengths and weaknesses. Yes, werewolves in crinos are strong, because it (and rage) makes even the non-combat character into an average-good combatant (and they tend to come in packs...). On the other hand, vampires could be ridiculously strong too, with very high dice pools, without even being an elder. Werewolves, on the other hand tend to suck in interacting with humans. Mages are scary if you let them prepare for you, or work from a distance and without time restrictions but why should they can?<br /> <br /> Yes, the splats aren't balanced, like D&amp;D classes (and those aren't either..), but they could work, if you take time with the players to establish story and a workable troupe.<br /> <br /> Edit: it also worth considering that White Wolf wants to unify the WoD more under the new editions (at least they said so), but that'd be a while away to get all the major lines, so, right now, just go with the 20ths! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 10-17-2017, 10:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T07:30:22-07:00","id":1150637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e0rk</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, the splats aren't balanced, like D&amp;D classes (and those aren't either..), but they could work</div> </div> </div> </div>Mechanically speaking mages and werewolves are opposite. Ironically, this means they complement each other. Mages are bad at everything werewolves are good, and vice versa. Problems that would require werewolves to make deals with potent spirits in order to surpass (like the Tremere Wards that are on rage lately) are easier to solve for mages, and enemies that would be hard for mages to face without a lot of preparation or wonders (like your regular vampire combat build) can be dealt with just Crinos form and a few gifts.<br /> <br /> I think that the key to solve the mechanical disparity it's to play to each splat's strength instead of trying to take the torch of the splat. In that sense I do not agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> . An Akashick will be outclased by a Get of Fenris at every turn without spamming rituals and wonders, and even then, all that effort that risks lots of Paradox will just allow the Akasick to be there and survive. On the other hand a Correspondence/Mind user can shine, as Garou are bad dealing with stuff at distance, and aren't OP as social manipulators compared to Mind mages. You just need to allow these things to shine by creating problems that can't be solved with pure brute strenght, or with pure finesse and ranged hex.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T14:10:32-07:00","id":1150773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150637#post1150637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> You just need to allow these things to shine by creating problems that can't be solved with pure brute strenght, or with pure finesse and ranged hex.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agree. Everybody is good at different things and ideally, the ST should create stories where everybody could shine at some point. But really, that's the same with a splat-only game too, the ST would have the same headache to manage the loner, combat heavy Gangrel, the social butterfly Malkavian, the Tremere scholar and the Sneaky Nosferatu in the same group. Not everybody is equally good at everything, but everybody have their strengths. Swallow it and go on!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T12:49:14-07:00","id":1151091,"title":"","content":"That's my point. A werewolf, a mage and a vampire on the same page won't be too far apart. That's been my experience since long before VWM20 and remains so now. In our latest crossover there's a bigger disparity in ability between the vamps in the group than between the combat focused mage and the combat focused vamp and between the garou theurge bass guitarist and the ravnos singer (both social focused).<br /> <br /> Yeah, Crinos is nice, but only if you're building for combat. If you're starting from tertiary physical and no brawl/melee then you're probably looking at about STR 6, DEX 3, STA 5 with an attack of just 3 dice. A vampire starting with the same could get straight 5's in all three scores (so an attack of 6 dice with fangs) for a scene if they're willing to spend the blood to do so (depending on generation it would take time to get up to full and they'd be hungry afterwards but its on par with what Crinos gives the werewolf non-combatant, can choose which stats they want to bump and don't have to look like a 9' tall death machine to get them). A mage with Life 3 could get to all 5's and be able to soak agg and still be coincidental.<br /> <br /> Yeah that werewolf probably has spirit allies to help, but the vampire probably has presence, dominate or some other discipline to balance the scales with and the mage, even without life has six spheres worth of crazy to evade or confound their enemies.<br /> <br /> And a combat focused werewolf is a beast with probably STR 7, DEX 5, STA 6 in Crinos with brawl 5. But a combat focused vamp could start with STR 3, DEX 4, STA 3, Celerity 3, Firearms 5 and use its resources an AR-15, magazines of silver and incendiary bullets and military combat armor). Even with the armor penalty its got a ten dice attack and can soak 8 lethal or 5 agg that isn't fire/sun. A combat focused mage with Forces 2, Life 3 and Mind 1 can buff their stamina to the human limit and soak agg for a scene with just 4-5 successes. Throw in reinforced clothing and they can have seven dice of soak against agg. Plus they need just one success on their attacks to use forces 2 with &quot;violence as focus&quot; to make their attacks deal 6-8 levels (not dice) of damage per hit, possibly as unsoakable agg if they're using silver or incendiary bullets.<br /> <br /> Its also worth pointing out that, as great as Crinos is, the beginning gifts of a Garou are nowhere near what three dots of disciplines or six dots of spheres can pull off. Werewolves in essence &quot;pay&quot; for Crinos with weaker options elsewhere. In D&amp;D terms they're stuck with 2-3 levels of fighter before they can multiclass into anything else and they either have to devote enough other selections (physical attributes and ability picks) to make those fighter levels useful or end up with the D&amp;D equivalent of wasted levels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T18:58:38-07:00","id":1151176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151091#post1151091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Its also worth pointing out that, as great as Crinos is, the beginning gifts of a Garou are nowhere near what three dots of disciplines or six dots of spheres can pull off. Werewolves in essence &quot;pay&quot; for Crinos with weaker options elsewhere. In D&amp;D terms they're stuck with 2-3 levels of fighter before they can multiclass into anything else and they either have to devote enough other selections (physical attributes and ability picks) to make those fighter levels useful or end up with the D&amp;D equivalent of wasted levels.</div> </div> </div> </div>Essentially agree, but I'd stress the same thing is which makes werewolves scary. If we compare a mixed coterie with a mixed ww pack, then probably the combat.monster characters will be more-or-less on par (I'd say wws are tougher -minus silver-, but the vamps could get ridiculous initiative ratings, attack and damage pools, especially in V20 and yeah, they don't have to turn into a big-ass furball for it), but for the rest, yeah <i>every</i> ww has 2-3 levels of fighter, while the non-combat vamps probably have none, so of course, in a straight-up combat, the wws would tear them to pieces. On the flipside, mindless violence is rarely the best option in WoD and the vamps would probably outclass the wws in almost any other aspect.<br /> <br /> And really, yeah, mages are OP as hell, if you let them, but how often cold you pull off a major ritual, or bring out all the toys for a fight? The problem with preparation is that you can't always.<br /> <br /> So, to wrap it up, I never seen as much disparity, when speaking about the big three as people tend to make it.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T19:47:15-07:00","id":1151190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151091#post1151091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's my point. A werewolf, a mage and a vampire on the same page won't be too far apart. That's been my experience since long before VWM20 and remains so now. In our latest crossover there's a bigger disparity in ability between the vamps in the group than between the combat focused mage and the combat focused vamp and between the garou theurge bass guitarist and the ravnos singer (both social focused).<br /> <br /> Yeah, Crinos is nice, but only if you're building for combat. If you're starting from tertiary physical and no brawl/melee then you're probably looking at about STR 6, DEX 3, STA 5 with an attack of just 3 dice. A vampire starting with the same could get straight 5's in all three scores (so an attack of 6 dice with fangs) for a scene if they're willing to spend the blood to do so (depending on generation it would take time to get up to full and they'd be hungry afterwards but its on par with what Crinos gives the werewolf non-combatant, can choose which stats they want to bump and don't have to look like a 9' tall death machine to get them). A mage with Life 3 could get to all 5's and be able to soak agg and still be coincidental.<br /> <br /> Yeah that werewolf probably has spirit allies to help, but the vampire probably has presence, dominate or some other discipline to balance the scales with and the mage, even without life has six spheres worth of crazy to evade or confound their enemies.<br /> <br /> And a combat focused werewolf is a beast with probably STR 7, DEX 5, STA 6 in Crinos with brawl 5. But a combat focused vamp could start with STR 3, DEX 4, STA 3, Celerity 3, Firearms 5 and use its resources an AR-15, magazines of silver and incendiary bullets and military combat armor). Even with the armor penalty its got a ten dice attack and can soak 8 lethal or 5 agg that isn't fire/sun. A combat focused mage with Forces 2, Life 3 and Mind 1 can buff their stamina to the human limit and soak agg for a scene with just 4-5 successes. Throw in reinforced clothing and they can have seven dice of soak against agg. Plus they need just one success on their attacks to use forces 2 with &quot;violence as focus&quot; to make their attacks deal 6-8 levels (not dice) of damage per hit, possibly as unsoakable agg if they're using silver or incendiary bullets.<br /> <br /> Its also worth pointing out that, as great as Crinos is, the beginning gifts of a Garou are nowhere near what three dots of disciplines or six dots of spheres can pull off. Werewolves in essence &quot;pay&quot; for Crinos with weaker options elsewhere. In D&amp;D terms they're stuck with 2-3 levels of fighter before they can multiclass into anything else and they either have to devote enough other selections (physical attributes and ability picks) to make those fighter levels useful or end up with the D&amp;D equivalent of wasted levels.</div> </div> </div> </div>...You'd think that, but then a mere cliath CoG uses Jam Weapon, and all of the artificially-produced weapons nearby stop working for several turns. Firearms becomes completely useless without magical powers that rely on it as an Ability roll, Melee becomes useless unless you accept the shame of using a fallen tree branch (which doesn't exactly do much damage) or sharp rock. This is assuming that someone doesn't just use Create Element to set you on fire and make you regret carrying all of those incendiary rounds (which another cliath-available Gift can change to near-harmless bashing damage). For extra hilarity, lure that vampire into a granary with massive amounts of flour lying around. See what happens when they switch to incendiary rounds after the bags get torn up and flour is everywhere...<br /> <br /> These are not capacities to be discounted. They are difficult to get for a lot of characters because spirits need proof of your worthiness to wield their Gifts, but the results aren't nearly as underwhelming as you're suggesting with your D&amp;D analogy. Then, too, you get to rites, which you can go wild with sans any experience point expenditure or rank requirements after you have 5 dots in Rituals. The Croatan even knew a level six rite for making the sun rise at night (presumably, it requires a totem's patronage to get an effective Rituals 6+).<br /> <br /> (Your analogy also fails to take into account how fighters are typically way better off earlier on, so being &quot;forced&quot; to take them isn't really a penalty. Also, in 2e and 4e, they're really quite impressive and characters you'd want to have around at higher levels, as in 2e, everything had way fewer hit points and so a higher-level one could kill an ancient red dragon in one turn before going on to kill a powerful demon and a lich at about the same rate. In 4e, meanwhile, the game was actually structured to make everyone useful and casters aren't getting their game-breaking shit like in other editions during higher level play.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T20:58:16-07:00","id":1151201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151190#post1151190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> but then a mere cliath CoG uses Jam Weapon, and all of the artificially-produced weapons nearby stop working for several turns.</div> </div> </div> </div>And vampires don't relying on artificial weapons won't care. It's equally probable that a coterie will contain a Gangrel than a pack a CoG. Also, every vampire could bite.<br /> <br /> Also, the ww would need to beat the <i>highest </i>willpower score on the scene in a contested roll and probably it won't beat the Tremere, or the Ventrue present.<br /> <br /> Against mages? Forget it, they're <i>starting</i> from WP 5.<br /> <br /> Create element from a cliath? Come on, anyone with fortitude will soak <i>that</i> and the Tremere would respond in kind.<br /> <br /> Or what if we're just stop pretending that any splat's tricks are working automatically and omnipotently, every time and without a hitch and acknowledge that everyone have their bag of nastiness, but nothing is a sure way to win every time?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your analogy also fails to take into account how fighters are typically way better off earlier on, so being &quot;forced&quot; to take them isn't really a penalty. </div> </div> </div>Depends on what you want to do with a character. I'd be pissed off, if I want to play a rogue, or a wizard, or a bard and the GM forces me to trudge through lower levels as a fighter, because that means I won't be a 4th level rogue or wizard on level 4, but a level one. Note, I'm not saying it's not a viable way, it is, but that's why I wrote that yeah, in a straight-up fight between two group of characters it'll be an advantage that even the least combat-focused garou is a passable fighter, while other splats are not, but those other splats' differently focused characters will outdo the non-combat garou in most things. Look, it's a tradeoff, no one is supreme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, in 2e and 4e, they're really quite impressive and characters you'd want to have around at higher levels, as in 2e, everything had way fewer hit points and so a higher-level one could kill an ancient red dragon in one turn before going on to kill a powerful demon and a lich at about the same rate. </div> </div> </div>Whoever said that fighters aren't awesome? They are really-really good at being fighters.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In 4e, meanwhile, the game was actually structured to make everyone useful and casters aren't getting their game-breaking shit like in other editions during higher level play.) </div> </div> </div>At the cost of making everybody working the same. Thanks, not my cup of tea. Daily, or per encounter martial &quot;powers&quot; still don't make sense, but yes, it's balanced, if you want close to perfect mechanical balance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 10-20-2017, 12:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T22:59:08-07:00","id":1151219,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151201#post1151201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And vampires don't relying on artificial weapons won't care. It's equally probable that a coterie will contain a Gangrel than a pack a CoG. Also, every vampire could bite.</div> </div> </div> </div>[deadpan] Oh no. A Gangrel. Whatever shall we do?<br /> <br /> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0AZ5XZHIDh0\" target=\"_blank\">Lashes vampires with sun whip</a>)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, the ww would need to beat the <i>highest </i>willpower score on the scene in a contested roll and probably it won't beat the Tremere, or the Ventrue present.<br /> <br /> Against mages? Forget it, they're <i>starting</i> from WP 5. </div> </div> </div>Only slightly above a CoG, and Willpower is both cheap and central to the concept and mission of most CoGs. Also, it's not an opposed roll, but one with a difficulty of the opposing side's highest rating, which can be mollified more easily by having a high WP on the CoG's part, given how dice probabilities work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Create element from a cliath? Come on, anyone with fortitude will soak <i>that</i> and the Tremere would respond in kind. </div> </div> </div>But will you if it ignites something that explodes around you, as any given particulate in the air will? Will vampires recognize the danger of Garou stealing flour and fighting around it before they end up at the center of an explosion? Besides, CE to burn things does automatic levels of damage. On average, Fortitude 5 would be required to soak that fully (only need three successes to hit the damage ceiling), and would do so only half the time. Then you need to worry about the fuel that might be on you...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or what if we're just stop pretending that any splat's tricks are working automatically and omnipotently, every time and without a hitch and acknowledge that everyone have their bag of nastiness, but nothing is a sure way to win every time? </div> </div> </div>That could apply to Jam Weapon, but blowing up vampires with flour bombs or incidental and flammable materials that they might have on them is way, way easier to make happen. Gas canisters from a hardware store would probably be even better for this purpose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Depends on what you want to do with a character. I'd be pissed off, if I want to play a rogue, or a wizard, or a bard and the GM forces me to trudge through lower levels as a fighter, because that means I won't be a 4th level rogue or wizard on level 4, but a level one. Note, I'm not saying it's not a viable way, it is, but that's why I wrote that yeah, in a straight-up fight between two group of characters it'll be an advantage that even the least combat-focused garou is a passable fighter, while other splats are not, but those other splats' differently focused characters will outdo the non-combat garou in most things. Look, it's a tradeoff, no one is supreme. </div> </div> </div>Good thing the anaology didn't even really hit home, then, because totems, rites, talens, and fetishes bypass Rank.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Whoever said that fighters aren't awesome? They are really-really good at being fighters. </div> </div> </div>Presumably the poster making the analogy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> At the cost of making everybody working the same. Thanks, not my cup of tea. Daily, or per encounter martial &quot;powers&quot; still don't make sense, but yes, it's balanced, if you want close to perfect mechanical balance. </div> </div> </div>I would have preferred wagering hit points myself, but I'm unlikely to be in charge of D&amp;D any time soon, or ever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T23:39:16-07:00","id":1151226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151219#post1151219\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> [deadpan] Oh no. A Gangrel. Whatever shall we do?<br /> <br /> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0AZ5XZHIDh0\" target=\"_blank\">Lashes vampires with sun whip</a>)</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is a level 3 fetish. Yeah, a cliath would totally wander around with that. Should take into account state of the art body armor, combination disciplines, ghouls, blood-bonded swat forces, etc. on the vampire's part too?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Only slightly above a CoG, and Willpower is both cheap and central to the concept and mission of most CoGs. </div> </div> </div>Just like it is central for a Ventrue<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, it's not an opposed roll, but one with a difficulty of the opposing side's highest rating, which can be mollified more easily by having a high WP on the CoG's part, given how dice probabilities work. </div> </div> </div>Ok, i misread that, fair point. It's somewhat more efficient then. Still, it's not a surety it'll work and again, a lot of vam\u0151ire tricks don't depend on artificial weapons. Moreover, the caster didn't do anything else at that turn and spent a gonsis, which is a precious commodity, especially at early &quot;levels&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But will you if it ignites something that explodes around you, as any given particulate in the air will? Will vampires recognize the danger of Garou stealing flour and fighting around it before they end up at the center of an explosion? Besides, CE to burn things does automatic levels of damage. On average, Fortitude 5 would be required to soak that fully (only need three successes to hit the damage ceiling), and would do so only half the time. Then you need to worry about the fuel that might be on you...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That could apply to Jam Weapon, but blowing up vampires with flour bombs or incidental and flammable materials that they might have on them is way, way easier to make happen. Gas canisters from a hardware store would probably be even better for this purpose.<br /> </div> </div> </div>That, just... Come on, you assume every bit of tactical and preparational advantage on the Garou's part, as weel as cherry picked gifts, etc. That doesn't work like that. I could come up with a million and one scenarios and cleverly devised plans how the vampires could beat the shit out of the furrballs with careful preparation too as well as coteries gunned hard for werewolves with discipline choices. Sorry, but this is silly.<br /> <br /> Besides, CE elements needs spent gnosis too, a roll and can inflict 3 levels of damage at best. Not exactly a death ray.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Good thing the anaology didn't even really hit home, then, because totems, rites, talens, and fetishes bypass Rank. </div> </div> </div>Look, make your hypothetical prodigy cliath pack as much a Christmas tree as you want. It's basically the same baseline and I'm standing by it: every splat has it's strengths and advantages, no one is supreme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Presumably the poster making the analogy. </div> </div> </div>It said that Crinos and rage are strong and werewolves are &quot;paying for it&quot; in other things. I think that's a fair remark, expect you just want to see the Garou as the be-all-end-all.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 10-20-2017, 03:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T23:47:10-07:00","id":1151229,"title":"","content":"And I'd like to reiterate: an average pack of Garou would probably mop the floor with an average coterie, because a non-combat Garou is still a better combatant than a non-combat vamp. It's a little consolation that the coterie's main combatant could go more-or-less toe-to-toe with the Fenris Ahroun, if the other 3 members of the coterie are practically dog food and she suddenly have to face 4 wolves. That doesn't make wws <i>better</i> but it is why the other splats fear them, but it comes with weaknesses in other areas and bashing heads isn't frequently the best choice. Again, it's a tradeoff.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 10-20-2017, 03:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T08:52:00-07:00","id":1151342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151226#post1151226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Which is a level 3 fetish. Yeah, a cliath would totally wander around with that. Should take into account state of the art body armor, combination disciplines, ghouls, blood-bonded swat forces, etc. on the vampire's part too?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, they totally could. It's not like it's a klaive. Which have been on sample starting characters in tribebooks, sometimes as far as grand klaives.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just like it is central for a Ventrue </div> </div> </div>Tamping the urge to kill the senseless idiots around you is hard work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ok, i misread that, fair point. It's somewhat more efficient then. Still, it's not a surety it'll work and again, a lot of vam\u0151ire tricks don't depend on artificial weapons. Moreover, the caster didn't do anything else at that turn and spent a gonsis, which is a precious commodity, especially at early &quot;levels&quot;. </div> </div> </div>For homids, yes. Metis and lupus can afford to throw it around a bit more often. Especially if the aforementioned vampire with an AR-15 shows up. (Analogy poster's scenario, not mine)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That, just... Come on, you assume every bit of tactical and preparational advantage on the Garou's part, as weel as cherry picked gifts, etc. That doesn't work like that. I could come up with a million and one scenarios and cleverly devised plans how the vampires could beat the shit out of the furrballs with careful preparation too as well as coteries gunned hard for werewolves with discipline choices. Sorry, but this is silly. </div> </div> </div>It's not very elaborate. You get a bunch of flammable material packed into a small space... and you ignite it. Because CE can work at a distance, you have a much higher safety margin than relying on Master of Fire to do the same while in the middle of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Besides, CE elements needs spent gnosis too, a roll and can inflict 3 levels of damage at best. Not exactly a death ray. </div> </div> </div>Again, without fuel, sure. With fuel, I says, bad is good baby! Down with government!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Look, make your hypothetical prodigy cliath pack as much a Christmas tree as you want. It's basically the same baseline and I'm standing by it: every splat has it's strengths and advantages, no one is supreme. </div> </div> </div>All of this - standard cliath Gifts, shit you can scrounge up, maybe having one specialized weapon that's only really powerful for killing vampires and other sun-vulnerable critters. - is all fairly pedestrian. It's not like they all have high Melee with specialties in klaive dueling and grand klaives (though that could also be potentially explained away by having high Pure Breed, I guess).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It said that Crinos and rage are strong and werewolves are &quot;paying for it&quot; in other things. I think that's a fair remark, expect you just want to see the Garou as the be-all-end-all. </div> </div> </div>It was... inaccurate, since the mechanical equivalent of a lot of cliath Gifts in other systems tend to be higher than first-dot powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T09:54:39-07:00","id":1151371,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151091#post1151091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And a combat focused werewolf is a beast with probably STR 7, DEX 5, STA 6 in Crinos with brawl 5. But a combat focused vamp could start with STR 3, DEX 4, STA 3, Celerity 3, Firearms 5 and use its resources an AR-15, magazines of silver and incendiary bullets and military combat armor). Even with the armor penalty its got a ten dice attack and can soak 8 lethal or 5 agg that isn't fire/sun. A combat focused mage with Forces 2, Life 3 and Mind 1 can buff their stamina to the human limit and soak agg for a scene with just 4-5 successes. Throw in reinforced clothing and they can have seven dice of soak against agg. Plus they need just one success on their attacks to use forces 2 with &quot;violence as focus&quot; to make their attacks deal 6-8 levels (not dice) of damage per hit, possibly as unsoakable agg if they're using silver or incendiary bullets.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair. Your example received the answers than it did because it sucks: You're giving Arete 3 and armor (8 freebies expended, + Resources) to the mage, special weapons (with Resources + the necessary balck market connections) to the vampire. You didn't do the same for the Garou (just Crinos). What you show it's far from what the average combat build Werewolf PC can do: Having a Totem it's a social custom, no pack would be without one, and 3 Gifts (from which you given zero) are mandatory. <i>The Garou that you presented it's below the exaple characters of the Basic</i> (that include Fetishes), <i>while the mage and vampire &quot;combat builds&quot; are stronger and better prepared that the examples in their books</i> (The last time I saw the Brujah motorbiker, had a leather armor, not a full military grade) . A tendency I've seen a lot lately. <b>Don't do that, it's cheap and nonsense.</b><br /> <br /> Also:<br /> The mage needs two turns (with luck) to get the soack and risks Paradox each time he does these attacks, and has no extra actions. All of that just to be on par with the Garou (who have extra actions, only need 1 point of Rage to change in Crinos, can raise from the death one time, and don't need to spend freebies to have any of this. And thus are generally better).<br /> The vampire would be unable to function a lot if she has to spend 5 blood points (nearly half an average reserve) just to be on par with the Garou, would need several turns to do so unless very low Generation, and will need more to heal or take full advatage of Celerity.<br /> Both are wearing uncomfortable armor that takes from Dextrety all the time.<br /> And all of this just to be there, not to be exeptional in comparison with the Garou, but to be on par. Nedless to say, if the Garou puts the same effort, he will surpass them both.<br /> <br /> And they are all combat builds, they would be unable to operate in situations where finesse or social skills would be the key.<br /> That's why packs usualy have more than Ahrouns","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-20-2017, 01:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T10:31:05-07:00","id":1151380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151190#post1151190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> ...You'd think that, but then a mere cliath CoG uses Jam Weapon, and all of the artificially-produced weapons nearby stop working for several turns.</div> </div> </div> </div> Sure... IF there's a Children of Gaia who happens to know that gift it could get pretty messy for a few turns if no one's got much willpower. Go to total defense for a few turns until your stuff starts working and use sticks and rocks in necessary... there's no actual shame in that - only to the garou if they still lose after trying to disable their opponent's weapons. Vampire fangs are aggravated damage and use Dex+Brawl+1 as their attack pool... they're not THAT hindered. Also, given that vamps and most mages are all about survival rather than honor they're not going to consider resorting to rocks or tree branches as all that &quot;shameful.&quot; That would be the werewolves when their would be victim kicks their heads in and takes their pelt as a trophy.<br /> <br /> Personally, I play mages though so I'd probably just carry around a couple nuggets of raw silver around if I were expecting that sort of thing so I could throw them at any werewolf stupid enough to try that. Dex+Athletics with only once success needed, then Forces 2 to accelerate it (eat the 1 paradox; its worth it) to score about 6-8 LEVELS of unsoakable aggravated damage as a chunk of silver lodges in the werewolf's brain or heart. Its action stops my gun working. Mine cripples it or kills it outright and gives serious pause to any other wolf who thinks I'm easy prey. If I'm an Akashic with even 1 dot in Do then my fists are lethal weapons to begin with to and I can parry werewolf bites/claws without penalty.<br /> <br /> But how many times is a vampire or weapon-dependent mage specifically going to go up against a Child of Gaia? Probably a lot less often than a werewolf will face a vampire with celerity or potence or a mage with forces 2 (or life 3 for better body/soak agg/grow claws or matter 2/prime 2 to just MAKE silver out of thin air or correspondence 3 to teleport away or just happens to have willpower 10... because its a mage and Willpower is practically their defining trait after Arete and Spheres, is very useful across the board and unlike starting arete [capped] and spheres [expensive], willpower is dirt cheap to max out).<br /> <br /> IF there happens to be a Metis on hand (admittedly slightly more likely than a CoG) then Create Elements might do some damage... up to 3 points for 1 Gnosis a pop... soakable by Fortitude and possibly body armor (since its described as no more than a 1 foot cube) at probably difficulty 5 (heat of a torch; anything greater isn't from a natural source of heat) or with Life magic or forces counter magic. IF the vampire or mage is within 60 feet of the Metis. The combat-oriented vampire in the current game I'm in would just put a .50 cal silver bullet through the garou's head with his Barrett while in his blackhawk helicopter at 1000 yards and then have his ghoul retainer pilot land so he can go lick up the leftovers. If it somehow got within 60 feet the mage in the current game would almost certainly soak that fire without harm (better body + soak agg and reinforced clothing) and put a forces accelerated silver bullet through the thing's skull like with the silver nugget only coincidental and with the ability to pull a mythic thread (silver bullets vs. werewolves) to lower the difficulty even more.<br /> <br /> See? These supposed invincible attacks that make one supernatural supposedly unbeatable can be countered and similarly employed by all the supernaturals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (Your analogy also fails to take into account how fighters are typically way better off earlier on, so being &quot;forced&quot; to take them isn't really a penalty. Also, in 2e and 4e, they're really quite impressive and characters you'd want to have around at higher levels, as in 2e, everything had way fewer hit points and so a higher-level one could kill an ancient red dragon in one turn before going on to kill a powerful demon and a lich at about the same rate. In 4e, meanwhile, the game was actually structured to make everyone useful and casters aren't getting their game-breaking shit like in other editions during higher level play.) </div> </div> </div>Point being... if you want to build a wizard or rogue or cleric the abilities you actually want to focus on are going to be running 2-3 levels behind where they'd be otherwise in exchange for a benefit that's not all that useful.if your goal is to be anything other than a nine-foot tall rage monster.<br /> <br /> Look at it this way for an analogy; a starting World of Darkness character is like a 6th level 3e D&amp;D characters with point buy ability scores. The wizard and cleric can cast multiple 3rd level spells a day, the rogue's sneak attack and extra skills start becoming meaningfully large. A werewolf wanting to be a wizard is stuck as Fighter 3, Wizard 3. They can't use their fighter armor proficiency if they wanna cast spells (arcane spell failure) and they couldn't put too many points into Str or Dex since they'll need a good Int score to use their spells. Plus, they're more than a full spell level behind the pure wizard/cleric... they've only just got their 2nd level spells and will only just be getting 7th level spells when the wizard and cleric are getting 9th level ones.<br /> <br /> Similarly, the werewolf who's not focused on combat isn't going to be able to put too many points into their physical scores since they'll need mental or social ones for their gifts. They'll also need skills other than brawl for those gifts so won't wanna put too many points there. And while the level one gifts are neat tricks, compare what they can do next to say Dominate or Presence 3... or what two spheres at 3 dots can pull off (life 3/matter 3; I turn into living silver; any wolf that even touches me takes aggravated damage... correspondence 3/mind 3; I implant subliminal suggestions that your packmates have been replaced by disguised wyrmspawn every time you go to sleep and I always escape before you ever find me because I know you're coming and can teleport).<br /> <br /> Crinos is nice... it'll take a non-combat wolf up to STR 6, DEX 3, STA 5 and let them soak agg that isn't silver, but a typical soldier though has the following suggested stats;<br /> <br /> <i>Suggested Attributes: Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Stamina 3, Charisma 3, Manipulation 4, Appearance 1-3, Perception 2-4, Intelligence 2-4, Wits 3-4<br /> Suggested Abilities: Alertness 2, Athletics 2-4, Brawl 3-4, Drive 2, Firearms 3-5, Intimidation 3, Martial Arts 0-4, Melee 1-3, Stealth 2, Streetwise 3-5, Subterfuge 2, Technology 1-3<br /> Willpower: 6</i><br /> Military Armor is 5 dice of soak with a -2 dex penalty; M-16's are 7/L damage and are three round burst capable; frag grenades to 12/L to a 10 yard blast area. A combat knife is diff 4 and would do 5/L base damage in their hands.<br /> <br /> Delirium effects will also help, but just one of those guys with a point higher willpower than normal (or was ghouled so they're supernatural) would stand a good chance of putting a non-combat wolf in crinos down in a straight one-on-one fight (the wolf's edge would be in their gifts and hopefully allow them to win... but a Glasswalker Ragabash with Persuasion, Open Seal and Plug and Play might not have a whole lot of options).<br /> <br /> So unless you want that ability to be basically wasted; any werewolf is going to need to put points into stuff like athletics and brawl and probably not want their physicals to be less than secondary in priority... points that could have gone elsewhere if they weren't a wolf with a locked in set of combat abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T10:54:39-07:00","id":1151391,"title":"","content":"What an amazing use of non sequitur:<br /> <br /> You post starts with an analogy with D&amp;D that isn't very fitting since Garou aren't &quot;impaired&quot; by Crinos to do magic-they don't put points in Crinos. Proving that Werewolves are bad at magic or social stuff would require a different kind of explanation. One that takes in count what a magically capable or a social capable werewolf can do vis a vis what other splats can do.<br /> <br /> Then you go to prove that a non combat Werewolf it's Soldier level, which you fail to do as you need to assume high willpower or a supernatural afiliation, and that the werewolf won't have usefull gifts and will be there with cero preparation...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So unless you want that ability to be basically wasted; any werewolf is going to need to put points into stuff like athletics and brawl and probably not want their physicals to be less than secondary in priority... points that could have gone elsewhere if they weren't a wolf with a locked in set of combat abilities. </div> </div> </div>How it's &quot;wasted&quot; to be able to stand agains any but the most prepared supernatural/high willpower soldiers?, do you think that the average combat can't be changed by having someone with those stats doing pack tactics with you?.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-20-2017, 01:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T11:34:07-07:00","id":1151401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I play mages though so I'd probably just carry around a couple nuggets of raw silver around if I were expecting that sort of thing so I could throw them at any werewolf stupid enough to try that. Dex+Athletics with only once success needed, then Forces 2 to accelerate it (eat the 1 paradox; its worth it) to score about 6-8 LEVELS of unsoakable aggravated damage as a chunk of silver lodges in the werewolf's brain or heart. Its action stops my gun working. Mine cripples it or kills it outright and gives serious pause to any other wolf who thinks I'm easy prey. If I'm an Akashic with even 1 dot in Do then my fists are lethal weapons to begin with to and I can parry werewolf bites/claws without penalty.</div> </div> </div> </div>While using silver that way it's pretty much &quot;the fireball for werewolves&quot;, and I DO recommend it in such a scenario, there are a few complications that make this a less that ideal situation for the mage:<br /> <br /> 1\u00ba Tool: You need to either have surpassed your Instrument in Forces (not doable by technomancers) or have another instrument to do that, otherwise penalties skyrocket and it may even be impossible to cast<br /> 2\u00ba Vulgar magick: You're casting at difficulty 6-7 with 3 dice, ideally. Not bad but you need 2 sucesses to get 6 levels with Forces, I would spend a Willpower to be on the safe side, perhaps even Quintessence. That way the kill it's almost assured. The problem it's that you're eating Paradox and probably need to spend resources as 3 dice can fail at those diff. It's not something that you want to do every fight. A combat build needs consistency because you want to fight a lot.<br /> 3\u00ba Initiative: What if you don't win initiative?, a combat werewolf can do short work of someone with just 5 dice to soack, especially when you can't dodge because you only have one action. And furries have a gift to auto win at that.<br /> 4\u00ba Dodge: The werewolf can dodge. Unsurprisingly, &quot;only one success of &quot;Dex+Athletics&quot; (plus magick) it's not so easy to pull off when the enemy has combat skils.<br /> 5\u00ba The werewolf likely resurrects after being killed, so you need to go trough all of the four steps again. Otherwise killing a normal werewolf using this strategy would be just as &quot;easy&quot; for a mage as killing a vampire with fire.<br /> <br /> Overal, I used similar strategies in my attempts to proove that mages weren't &quot;mere prey for other supernaturals&quot; in other thread. It proves that mages can potentialy represent a danger against anything, even with little preparation. It doesn't prove that mages can be as good as Garou at combat<i>.</i>..it's not that hard for a strong werewolf to survive such an attempt, and for the mage it's a bother, not so bad as having a werewolf claw on your guts, but not something you want to do a lot of times per session.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T12:36:33-07:00","id":1151421,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151380#post1151380\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Sure... IF there's a Children of Gaia who happens to know that gift it could get pretty messy for a few turns if no one's got much willpower.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's one of but a few common choices that CoGs get. It'd be rare to find one that wasn't at least intending to learn it, especially since it is so useful when confronted with any variety of manufactured weapon and can also help to de-escalate a situation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Go to total defense for a few turns until your stuff starts working and use sticks and rocks in necessary... there's no actual shame in that - only to the garou if they still lose after trying to disable their opponent's weapons. Vampire fangs are aggravated damage and use Dex+Brawl+1 as their attack pool... they're not THAT hindered. Also, given that vamps and most mages are all about survival rather than honor they're not going to consider resorting to rocks or tree branches as all that &quot;shameful.&quot; That would be the werewolves when their would be victim kicks their heads in and takes their pelt as a trophy.<br /> <br /> Personally, I play mages though so I'd probably just carry around a couple nuggets of raw silver around if I were expecting that sort of thing so I could throw them at any werewolf stupid enough to try that. Dex+Athletics with only once success needed, then Forces 2 to accelerate it (eat the 1 paradox; its worth it) to score about 6-8 LEVELS of unsoakable aggravated damage as a chunk of silver lodges in the werewolf's brain or heart. Its action stops my gun working. Mine cripples it or kills it outright and gives serious pause to any other wolf who thinks I'm easy prey. If I'm an Akashic with even 1 dot in Do then my fists are lethal weapons to begin with to and I can parry werewolf bites/claws without penalty. </div> </div> </div>Blur of the Milky Eye on a dark night. Do you know where your werewolf is?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But how many times is a vampire or weapon-dependent mage specifically going to go up against a Child of Gaia? Probably a lot less often than a werewolf will face a vampire with celerity or potence or a mage with forces 2 (or life 3 for better body/soak agg/grow claws or matter 2/prime 2 to just MAKE silver out of thin air or correspondence 3 to teleport away or just happens to have willpower 10... because its a mage and Willpower is practically their defining trait after Arete and Spheres, is very useful across the board and unlike starting arete [capped] and spheres [expensive], willpower is dirt cheap to max out). </div> </div> </div>CoGs are around #3 on the most populous Gaian tribes in Garou Nation territory (the first slots being Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers, respectively). The only way they're going to be unusual in a pack is if you head over to Japan, which has hundreds of Hakken all to itself and far fewer werewolves of other tribes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> IF there happens to be a Metis on hand (admittedly slightly more likely than a CoG) </div> </div> </div>There are high odds that the CoG is born of the forbidden union, since they tend to be of the mind to take in all of the unwanted Gaian Garou that the Bone Gnawers don't. Also, metis are a relatively slight cross-section of all Garou, since their existence results from a proscribed act.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> then Create Elements might do some damage... up to 3 points for 1 Gnosis a pop... soakable by Fortitude and possibly body armor (since its described as no more than a 1 foot cube) at probably difficulty 5 (heat of a torch; anything greater isn't from a natural source of heat) </div> </div> </div>Default difficult for anything unlisted is 6. This is presumably the case for CE. Anything flammable or explosive that you have on you or is around you is going to burn, such as your ammo. And if you actually did walk into a place where werewolves have stashed gas or flour, there's also that thermobaric explosion to worry about.<br /> <br /> This isn't very difficult to set up. There are Gifts to fake sounds, and Gifts to hide. You can lead enemies into a trap with great ease as long as you're alright with it being more Wisdom that you're getting than Glory. Social concerns are the main thing holding Garou back from this sort of tactic.<br /> <br /> Against Ratkin, meanwhile, you'd be running into those tactics every time, and your supporters would come down with sudden, mysterious, and deadly illnesses with great frequency. Because the Ratkin's creed isn't for Glory, but Infamy, and they know better than to get into a stand-up fight ever, even though they can do really well in one because they can get Dexterity 9 in Crinos. Wholesale mayhem is the order of the day for them. Bone Gnawers split the difference, and can toss huge ass things you normally find in the city on you or collapse buildings on their enemies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> or with Life magic or forces counter magic. IF the vampire or mage is within 60 feet of the Metis. The combat-oriented vampire in the current game I'm in would just put a .50 cal silver bullet through the garou's head with his Barrett while in his blackhawk helicopter at 1000 yards and then have his ghoul retainer pilot land so he can go lick up the leftovers. </div> </div> </div>Garou are actually pretty good at hiding; Blur of the Milky Eye, Camouflage, Blissful Ignorance, there's not really a shortage of powers for this. If you're using a helicopter and it isn't some kind of Technocratic Union standard (spoilers: you aren't, they keep their black helis to themselves), you're going to give away your position some time from the very loud noise before you get a visual. And then they can just wait for you to waste fuel and follow you back to wherever the fuck you keep that shit and where the hell is this confrontation even taking place? Because if it's in a city, the prince will probably have your hide for bringing in military weapons to their territory.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If it somehow got within 60 feet the mage in the current game would almost certainly soak that fire without harm (better body + soak agg and reinforced clothing) and put a forces accelerated silver bullet through the thing's skull like with the silver nugget only coincidental and with the ability to pull a mythic thread (silver bullets vs. werewolves) to lower the difficulty even more.<br /> <br /> See? These supposed invincible attacks that make one supernatural supposedly unbeatable can be countered and similarly employed by all the supernaturals. </div> </div> </div>Wasn't the original point that you said that first rank Gifts were not as good as three dot powers from other games? When, in fact, they tend to be about on par with them?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Point being... if you want to build a wizard or rogue or cleric the abilities you actually want to focus on are going to be running 2-3 levels behind where they'd be otherwise in exchange for a benefit that's not all that useful.if your goal is to be anything other than a nine-foot tall rage monster. </div> </div> </div>Cut it out with the D&amp;D. It isn't working.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Look at it this way for an analogy; a starting World of Darkness character is like a 6th level 3e D&amp;D characters </div> </div> </div>...especially if you're using the worst D&amp;D there is. Yes, I am counting the early, rough efforts in that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> with point buy ability scores. The wizard and cleric can cast multiple 3rd level spells a day, the rogue's sneak attack and extra skills start becoming meaningfully large. A werewolf wanting to be a wizard is stuck as Fighter 3, Wizard 3. They can't use their fighter armor proficiency if they wanna cast spells (arcane spell failure) and they couldn't put too many points into Str or Dex since they'll need a good Int score to use their spells. Plus, they're more than a full spell level behind the pure wizard/cleric... they've only just got their 2nd level spells and will only just be getting 7th level spells when the wizard and cleric are getting 9th level ones. </div> </div> </div>Not even remotely relevant to the discussion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Similarly, the werewolf who's not focused on combat isn't going to be able to put too many points into their physical scores since they'll need mental or social ones for their gifts. They'll also need skills other than brawl for those gifts so won't wanna put too many points there. And while the level one gifts are neat tricks, compare what they can do next to say Dominate or Presence 3... or what two spheres at 3 dots can pull off (life 3/matter 3; I turn into living silver; any wolf that even touches me takes aggravated damage... correspondence 3/mind 3; I implant subliminal suggestions that your packmates have been replaced by disguised wyrmspawn every time you go to sleep and I always escape before you ever find me because I know you're coming and can teleport). </div> </div> </div>Not being focused on combat comes in a few levels. And taking, say, Typhon as a totem can remedy it substantially, since it gives Brawl and spare Rage points in abundance.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Crinos is nice... it'll take a non-combat wolf up to STR 6, DEX 3, STA 5 and let them soak agg that isn't silver, but a typical soldier though has the following suggested stats;<br /> <br /> <i>Suggested Attributes: Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Stamina 3, Charisma 3, Manipulation 4, Appearance 1-3, Perception 2-4, Intelligence 2-4, Wits 3-4<br /> Suggested Abilities: Alertness 2, Athletics 2-4, Brawl 3-4, Drive 2, Firearms 3-5, Intimidation 3, Martial Arts 0-4, Melee 1-3, Stealth 2, Streetwise 3-5, Subterfuge 2, Technology 1-3<br /> Willpower: 6</i><br /> Military Armor is 5 dice of soak with a -2 dex penalty; M-16's are 7/L damage and are three round burst capable; frag grenades to 12/L to a 10 yard blast area. A combat knife is diff 4 and would do 5/L base damage in their hands. </div> </div> </div>And CE would both deprive them of all their gear as the ammo and explosives cook off. And without ammo, well, a retort from a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WlvrylwMG8\" target=\"_blank\"> scene in V for Vendetta comes to mind</a>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Delirium effects will also help, but just one of those guys with a point higher willpower than normal (or was ghouled so they're supernatural) would stand a good chance of putting a non-combat wolf in crinos down in a straight one-on-one fight (the wolf's edge would be in their gifts and hopefully allow them to win... but a Glasswalker Ragabash with Persuasion, Open Seal and Plug and Play might not have a whole lot of options). </div> </div> </div>Control Simple Machine would also allow you to prematurely activate a grenade on someone's person in this scenario. If this happens, then ghoul or no, that person is hamburger, as is anyone standing nearby. Ain't Glass Walker Ragabashes with Blur of the Milky Eye a bunch of stinkers?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So unless you want that ability to be basically wasted; any werewolf is going to need to put points into stuff like athletics and brawl and probably not want their physicals to be less than secondary in priority... points that could have gone elsewhere if they weren't a wolf with a locked in set of combat abilities. </div> </div> </div>You don't need to put that many points into them. That's the real takeaway, here; Garou can actually afford to be well-rounded and still be utterly frightening. And their &quot;non-combat&quot; expertise might just include Abilities that cause their enemies problems in vastly different fashion, like not being able to find them, or even managing to bridge the rifts between themselves and other Fera, so that they get help from the Ratkin mentioned above (only really likely with Bone Gnawers or following Rat as a totem, but it is possible nonetheless).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T18:29:39-07:00","id":1151527,"title":"","content":"Okay, since this appears to be devolving into a Splat vs Splat thread(Again...). Does anyone remember the title for that one thread that computer simulated the chances of each splat winning in a death match?<br /> <br /> I'd also like to point out that in any given first meeting with splats where no one has time to prepare, this werewolf is Most Likely to win.(it's not given under any means as luck, stat distribution, and strategy all can play a part).<br /> <br /> This however does set a dangerous president for the roided out rovers. If they do end up in a messy encounter with a respected vampire in a city and win, then the prince of that domain now has to have them hunted down or lose face for failing to defend his domain. Now the pact has to contend with a vampire far out of their league who has a good chunk of the city blood bonded/dominated/presenced into loyalty.<br /> <br /> If the said unfortunate corpse is a mage, then they just put the fear of god into every tradition/craft/independent mage in a mile radius and who are now doomsday-preping for werewolf attacks. They've also just increased technocracy surveillance in said city now that the techies A.) have to cover up a supernatural attack and B.) know a Dangerous supernatural element is in the area killing humans. Suddenly that city is a powder keg and any garou in the area just became a spark.<br /> <br /> The Werewolves are some of the strongest combatants in classic WOD(short of possibly the mokole or some of the spirits in the same splatbook), but they also have much less resources to work with. The WOD is a lot more complicated than just combat and any garou that forgets that is going to find their city life infinitely harder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T20:30:16-07:00","id":1151547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151401#post1151401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1\u00ba Tool: You need to either have surpassed your Instrument in Forces (not doable by technomancers) or have another instrument to do that, otherwise penalties skyrocket and it may even be impossible to cast</div> </div> </div> </div>Instruments aren't tied to any particular sphere in M20. So any of my seven could work. I'd rule out bodywork, meditation and thought-forms as those take longer and work better for my nightly meditations to strengthen my body before I go out to face the terrors that prey upon mankind.<br /> <br /> For this case, and presuming this particular CoG was doing something that would cause me to act against them I'd go with my surpassed one; movement and gestures (precede and follow up the throw with with the precise hand movements necessary to guide the flow of chi through the silver nugget and guide it to its target); but an invocation (of a principle of Do related to accuracy or precision), energy (focusing of chi into the throw) or weapons (improvised though it might be; I focus my desire to bring down my foe through this item to strike down my foe).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151401#post1151401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2\u00ba Vulgar magick: You're casting at difficulty 6-7 with 3 dice, ideally. Not bad but you need 2 sucesses to get 6 levels with Forces, I would spend a Willpower to be on the safe side, perhaps even Quintessence. That way the kill it's almost assured. The problem it's that you're eating Paradox and probably need to spend resources as 3 dice can fail at those diff. It's not something that you want to do every fight. A combat build needs consistency because you want to fight a lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Under normal situations I just use my gun and accelerate the rounds. That's coincidental forces 2 for a base difficulty 5; -1 for a surpassed focus makes it 4. I got my high resources specifically so I could afford my $10,000 custom-fitted kevlar/nomex suit and gun's frame cosmetically sculpted to look like something out of the various Batman films to tug on that mythic thread of the badass avenger of the night coming to save the innocent from some terror for an extra -1 to drop the difficulty to 3 (the limit).<br /> <br /> Before adding the extra success for using forces, my odds of success on three dice are 92% with a 0.7% change of botching and an average of 2.3 successes scored (so 3.3 successes or 6 health levels)<br /> <br /> Making it vulgar by throwing and accelerating the silver with raw chi force and gestures just means the difficulty goes up to 4 on three dice (the werewolf's own delirium takes care of any witnesses for me)... an 86.8% chance of success, only a 1.9% chance of botching (both not counting the free success for using forces) and the average number of successes I'd score would be 3.1 (2.1+1 extra for forces). Still 6 health levels in exchange for 1 paradox.<br /> <br /> Either way, no quintessence or willpower is needed... though if I get initiative I might pop the willpower in the first round if its just one wolf just to make the average successes 4.3 to make sure its insta-killed with eight unsoakable levels of aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151401#post1151401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3\u00ba Initiative: What if you don't win initiative?, a combat werewolf can do short work of someone with just 5 dice to soack, especially when you can't dodge because you only have one action. And furries have a gift to auto win at that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Spirit of the Fray is a level 2 gift... I thought we were comparing starting characters here. I've got Dex 5, Wits 3 and better body just needs two extra successes when I cast it before I go out hunting for the night to get it to 5 too. Plus with Forces 2 I can turn invisible (there's even the &quot;Batman exit&quot; rote), with Life 3 I can mask my scent and Mind 1 I can mask my aura. My odds of getting initiative are better than average against a starting werewolf I'd say; unless its got a Dex + Wis of 10 dice too. Also, its seven dice of soak, there's another +2 for the reinforced clothing (one of the reasons for resources) and that's only if I want to keep it coincidental.<br /> <br /> If I lose initiative on the opening round I'll go total defensive and use Counter Throw; works like a dodge with my full dice pool and if I succeed I get a throw as a free action (i.e. I don't have to split the dice pool for it). I'd do the kinetic acceleration forces trick here too to increase the damage on the off chance it causes a stun, but that's gravy. The main element is that the big hairy beast is now several yards away and on its butt so it'll have to take an action just to get back up. Does it really want to burn a rage point on that when I can just throw it again because I've gone full defensive this round? If it does then that's one less rage it has for extra attacks or to heal when I do land a blow with aggravate damage.<br /> <br /> Come to think of it, this would also let me wait out the Jam Weapon gift too (if it got any successes on its difficulty 10 check against me in the first place).<br /> <br /> If I win the initiative and can use my gun; I'd probably split my dice pool between the shot and snake step (since it adds an extra three dice to the action) and if I hit deliver an average of six unsoakable aggravated health levels to it (or 8 lethal to anything else). The wolf is now crippled (-5 dice to all its actions, can only crawl 1 yard/turn and cannot move at all and still attack in the same action), stunned (no actions next turn unless it spends rage) and isn't actually regenerating since it was aggravated damage and that takes a day per level.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4\u00ba Dodge: The werewolf can dodge. Unsurprisingly, &quot;only one success of &quot;Dex+Athletics&quot; (plus magick) it's not so easy to pull off when the enemy has combat skils. </div> </div> </div>Good. Its either going to waste its turn or have to burn off more rage for multiple actions. If I wanted to be vulgar about it, I'd pull a Shadow with the aforementioned invisibility/no scent (which I do NOT have to drop to into combat) and then it doesn't GET a dodge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5\u00ba The werewolf likely resurrects after being killed, so you need to go trough all of the four steps again. Otherwise killing a normal werewolf using this strategy would be just as &quot;easy&quot; for a mage as killing a vampire with fire. </div> </div> </div>It only heals one level per success by rolling permanent rage at difficulty 8 (and only once a scene). Even with a rage of 10 that's only about an 80% chance of scoring 1 success and an average of only 2.7 successes. Call it three and that just brings it to mauled where it can only hobble 3 yards a turn, suffers -2 dice to actions and moving any distance counts as an action for it. This is aggravated damage so beyond that its going to need DAYS to heal up further... and that's for an absolute rage machine; Rage 4 has only a 60% of scoring at least one success and will average only 1.6 successes. You're not dead, you're just incapacitated or possibly crippled and you can't pull that trick a second time. You're also back in your breed form so you can't soak any type of agg or even lethal damage at that point.<br /> <br /> * * * *<br /> <br /> I'm not saying werewolves aren't tough, but both mages and vampires are more than capable of playing at that level (which is all my original point was about in the first place). My mage isn't even the best combat mage build out there. I could have easily gone Time 3 instead of force 2, mind 1 (life can be an Akashic's affinity sphere) and then been able to better body and gain extra actions that don't even cost me resources every round to maintain; just the point of paradox for the vulgar spell).<br /> <br /> The funny thing to me is that, for all the fear associated with it, paradox is a lot more manageable than recovering Gnosis (or even Rage for that matter). If you stick to mostly coincidental effects you're only taking paradox from a botch; which, even at difficulty 6 with Arete 3, is only about 1 in 17 rolls. A little extra time, a little research, a surpassed instrument and perhaps a mythic thread and you floor a rank 3 or less coincidental effect at difficulty 3 and the botch chance drops down to less than 1% and is only 1-3 points if it DOES happen.<br /> <br /> Even the vulgar stuff is only 1 per successful effect. Given the recommendations on not even bothering to check unless its 5+ that's nearly half a dozen vulgar effects before a backlash is even likely to be rolled for and even the worst vulgar with witnesses botch a starting mage could suffer is eight points; which won't even do more than bashing damage if it ALL unloads on you.<br /> <br /> As a practical matter, the vampires and mages in various crossover games I've played in (and am playing in now) have no problems standing on even footing with the combat-focused werewolves if they wanted to be combat-focused themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T20:57:43-07:00","id":1151552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151527#post1151527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, since this appears to be devolving into a Splat vs Splat thread(Again...). Does anyone remember the title for that one thread that computer simulated the chances of each splat winning in a death match?<br /> <br /> I'd also like to point out that in any given first meeting with splats where no one has time to prepare, this werewolf is Most Likely to win.(it's not given under any means as luck, stat distribution, and strategy all can play a part).<br /> <br /> This however does set a dangerous president for the roided out rovers. If they do end up in a messy encounter with a respected vampire in a city and win, then the prince of that domain now has to have them hunted down or lose face for failing to defend his domain. Now the pact has to contend with a vampire far out of their league who has a good chunk of the city blood bonded/dominated/presenced into loyalty.<br /> <br /> If the said unfortunate corpse is a mage, then they just put the fear of god into every tradition/craft/independent mage in a mile radius and who are now doomsday-preping for werewolf attacks. They've also just increased technocracy surveillance in said city now that the techies A.) have to cover up a supernatural attack and B.) know a Dangerous supernatural element is in the area killing humans. Suddenly that city is a powder keg and any garou in the area just became a spark.<br /> <br /> The Werewolves are some of the strongest combatants in classic WOD(short of possibly the mokole or some of the spirits in the same splatbook), but they also have much less resources to work with. The WOD is a lot more complicated than just combat and any garou that forgets that is going to find their city life infinitely harder.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, given the vampires and mages that typically get used for these threads, the princes and the mystick authorities would probably all say &quot;Good riddance!&quot; and go on doing what they had been planning originally, without the flood of Masquerade breaches or chantry rivalries that they might otherwise suffer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T21:04:21-07:00","id":1151554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151547#post1151547\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I<br /> <br /> <br /> It only heals one level per success by rolling permanent rage at difficulty 8 (and only once a scene). Even with a rage of 10 that's only about an 80% chance of scoring 1 success and an average of only 2.7 successes. Call it three and that just brings it to mauled where it can only hobble 3 yards a turn, suffers -2 dice to actions and moving any distance counts as an action for it. This is aggravated damage so beyond that its going to need DAYS to heal up further... and that's for an absolute rage machine; Rage 4 has only a 60% of scoring at least one success and will average only 1.6 successes. You're not dead, you're just incapacitated or possibly crippled and you can't pull that trick a second time. You're also back in your breed form so you can't soak any type of agg or even lethal damage at that point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rage recovery automatically results in coming back into a berserk frenzy if successful, so they won't suffer any wound or movement penalties.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not saying werewolves aren't tough, but both mages and vampires are more than capable of playing at that level (which is all my original point was about in the first place). My mage isn't even the best combat mage build out there. I could have easily gone Time 3 instead of force 2, mind 1 (life can be an Akashic's affinity sphere) and then been able to better body and gain extra actions that don't even cost me resources every round to maintain; just the point of paradox for the vulgar spell). </div> </div> </div>Except that they can't, because of the stronger grounding that Garou and Fera have in possessing war forms. Any other being has to take time out of advancing their powers, eat Permadox, etc. for the same benefit. Trying to play the same game is a bad idea when your opponent doesn't really have to pay for it, and they get other stuff on top of it. This is simple math.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T21:10:07-07:00","id":1151557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151527#post1151527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, since this appears to be devolving into a Splat vs Splat thread(Again...).</div> </div> </div> </div>Sigh. I guess people just want to cheer for their favorite team. I like them all and am still standing by the opinion that no splat is &quot;the best&quot;, they're just focused differently.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Does anyone remember the title for that one thread that computer simulated the chances of each splat winning in a death match? </div> </div> </div>Missed that, what was the result?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd also like to point out that in any given first meeting with splats where no one has time to prepare, this werewolf is Most Likely to win.(it's not given under any means as luck, stat distribution, and strategy all can play a part). </div> </div> </div>Agree. They're very good at beating shit up, that's what they do.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Werewolves are some of the strongest combatants in classic WOD(short of possibly the mokole or some of the spirits in the same splatbook), but they also have much less resources to work with. The WOD is a lot more complicated than just combat and any garou that forgets that is going to find their city life infinitely harder. </div> </div> </div>Agree too. Other splats could reach the level of badassness the Garou have in combat and sheer brutality, or at least close to it, but it's not a given and doesn't belittle the fact that they are generally the best at it. But why are we being that fixated on just straight-up combat, the kind the Garou excels at?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T03:37:47-07:00","id":1151608,"title":"","content":"given this thread (that i created) had become splat v splat, i recently got hold of the new C20, how well on power levels do Changelings compare to the other splats, I have heard that they can be more powerful than mages but more focused and less of a Swiss army knife magic?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12845-ahather","name":"ahather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12845_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T06:26:01-07:00","id":1151629,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151554#post1151554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except that they can't, because of the stronger grounding that Garou and Fera have in possessing war forms. Any other being has to take time out of advancing their powers, eat Permadox, etc. for the same benefit. Trying to play the same game is a bad idea when your opponent doesn't really have to pay for it, and they get other stuff on top of it. This is simple math.</div> </div> </div> </div>My experience in actual play, not theorycrafting, suggests otherwise. Combat-focused mages go toe to toe with Black Spirals and walk away from it just like the a combat-focused werewolf or vampire. The disparity between the three main types of supernatural are NOT as extreme as some people want to make them out to be. Werewolves are a severe danger to a mage or vampire, mages are extreme dangers to vampires and werewolves and vampires are extreme dangers to mages and werewolves (unless the sun is out, then they're useless).<br /> <br /> While the theorycrafting is occasionally amusing in a &quot;Batman vs. Superman, who'd win?&quot; sort of way, the fact of the matter is that in a typical crossover game, the mage and the werewolf are going to be allies (fellow PC's) not enemies.<br /> <br /> Which again brings me back to my original suggested house rule, which is about the only one I think is actually needed if the players and Storyteller are actually talking to each other during the setup phase of the campaign... remove the &quot;must sleep during the day&quot; rule (keep the humanity/path limits on dice pools if you must) and make only direct sunlight cause damage so the vamps aren't sitting out a third to two-thirds of the game.<br /> <br /> Mages and werewolves (and changelings, wraiths, hunters, mummies, demons and ordinary mortals for that matter) can be active for 16-18 hours a day (and go a couple days without sleep in an emergency) while the vamps have a hard limit of just 3-12 hours of activity a night (because even twilight burns them) depending on the season and latitude; and that's not counting time spent waking, feeding and time needed to get back to their haven before the morning twilight comes.<br /> <br /> A game set during the summer in Seattle would CRIPPLE vampire players... from mid-April to mid-August actual night is less than 6 hours long (peaking at just 1:44 minutes of night on June 21). Even at the Winter Solstice, actual night in Seattle is just 11:48 minutes long. Miami, Florida is probably best for vamps in the United States with the shortest nights being just over seven hours and the longest being nearly 11 hours. Their ghouls would have to essentially be their mains in any crossover scenario by rules as written.<br /> <br /> If vamps could be active during twilight without burning to a crisp though it'd up the active times to 10-14 hours in Miami and 8-16 hours in Seattle. If it were just direct sun they had to worry about, they could be active as much as the non-vampire PC's; just a bit more limited in their movement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T12:38:28-07:00","id":1151714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151629#post1151629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My experience in actual play, not theorycrafting, suggests otherwise. Combat-focused mages go toe to toe with Black Spirals and walk away from it just like the a combat-focused werewolf or vampire. The disparity between the three main types of supernatural are NOT as extreme as some people want to make them out to be. Werewolves are a severe danger to a mage or vampire, mages are extreme dangers to vampires and werewolves and vampires are extreme dangers to mages and werewolves (unless the sun is out, then they're useless).</div> </div> </div> </div>Honest question: what material were you using for each game? Editions can make or break mechanical interactions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While the theorycrafting is occasionally amusing in a &quot;Batman vs. Superman, who'd win?&quot; sort of way, the fact of the matter is that in a typical crossover game, the mage and the werewolf are going to be allies (fellow PC's) not enemies.<br /> <br /> Which again brings me back to my original suggested house rule, which is about the only one I think is actually needed if the players and Storyteller are actually talking to each other during the setup phase of the campaign... remove the &quot;must sleep during the day&quot; rule (keep the humanity/path limits on dice pools if you must) and make only direct sunlight cause damage so the vamps aren't sitting out a third to two-thirds of the game.<br /> <br /> Mages and werewolves (and changelings, wraiths, hunters, mummies, demons and ordinary mortals for that matter) can be active for 16-18 hours a day (and go a couple days without sleep in an emergency) while the vamps have a hard limit of just 3-12 hours of activity a night (because even twilight burns them) depending on the season and latitude; and that's not counting time spent waking, feeding and time needed to get back to their haven before the morning twilight comes.<br /> <br /> A game set during the summer in Seattle would CRIPPLE vampire players... from mid-April to mid-August actual night is less than 6 hours long (peaking at just 1:44 minutes of night on June 21). Even at the Winter Solstice, actual night in Seattle is just 11:48 minutes long. Miami, Florida is probably best for vamps in the United States with the shortest nights being just over seven hours and the longest being nearly 11 hours. Their ghouls would have to essentially be their mains in any crossover scenario by rules as written.<br /> <br /> If vamps could be active during twilight without burning to a crisp though it'd up the active times to 10-14 hours in Miami and 8-16 hours in Seattle. If it were just direct sun they had to worry about, they could be active as much as the non-vampire PC's; just a bit more limited in their movement. </div> </div> </div>Just so long as we don't get any more D&amp;D analogies... (^VVVVVVV^)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T12:40:40-07:00","id":1151716,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ahather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151608#post1151608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">given this thread (that i created) had become splat v splat, i recently got hold of the new C20, how well on power levels do Changelings compare to the other splats, I have heard that they can be more powerful than mages but more focused and less of a Swiss army knife magic?</div> </div> </div> </div>Kithain ended up much more impressive than they have been before, but the round of edits between the back pdf release and the general release nerfed them slightly from that initial high point. They can still cause some absolutely terrible calamities via Unleashing, in any case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T13:43:19-07:00","id":1151736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151629#post1151629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If vamps could be active during twilight without burning to a crisp though it'd up the active times to 10-14 hours in Miami and 8-16 hours in Seattle. If it were just direct sun they had to worry about, they could be active as much as the non-vampire PC's; just a bit more limited in their movement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I never gave it much thought, but during this summer, it actually dawned on me (pun not intended) that it'd be really unfun to be a vampire in Budapest during the summer months. Actual darkness falls at around 21 pm and at 4 am it's already dawn. They'd miss most of the nightlife (which is especially bad, because otherwise that's the real tourist rush) and barely have enough time to do anything meaningful. Of course it's the reverse during the other half of the year, but still, it's quite much.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, cities like that could be very atmospheric, essentially having two cities, one during the summer months, when the tourists come, with abundant nightlife and laughter and a much bleaker, more dangerous one during the winter half, when the undead could hunt more freely.<br /> <br /> There was a thread about this whole sun-daylight-sleeping thing a little while ago on the vamp forum and I'm on the side that even burning in the sun wouldn't be really necessary, (Dracula), but if we want to keep to the contemporary vampire tropes, then at least it should be direct sunlight only, otherwise it's just a bit silly, having 4-ish active hours to get things done during one-third to half of the year. It could be done in a lot of ways to keep daylight sleeping and burning sunlight. Maybe you could stay awake for *humanity* hours after dawn, without penalties, and then increasing penalties by the hour. At least that'd be prudent for a working crossover, IMO.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 10-21-2017, 04:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T15:14:38-07:00","id":1151759,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151714#post1151714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Honest question: what material were you using for each game? Editions can make or break mechanical interactions.</div> </div> </div> </div>We're currently using the 20th Anniversary editions of all three lines (for the Mage side we're also using How Do You Do That? and Book of Secrets). As such, paradox is much less of a problem (just one point for a successful vulgar cast with or without witnesses and no automatic backlash each time a point is added) and casting a bit easier than in Revised. We lost specialized focus by sphere but gained the ability to use surpassed instruments with any effect and mythic threads, plus clarification on &quot;violence as focus&quot; (don't have to split the dice pool if you're using an action to hit, like throwing a fireball, shooting a gun or throwing a punch) and clarification of &quot;Hypothetical Average Passerby&quot; for determining coincidence or vulgar (which made our teleporter VERY happy).<br /> <br /> Likewise, Martial Arts and Do were cleaned up and clarified (I like that you have to have dots in other specific abilities including things like art, crafts, etiquette and academics in order to advance your rating in Do; its supposed to be a holistic lifestyle not just kicking people's heads in) and can really give an edge to the combat focused mage... particularly with the clarification that Martial Arts isn't just some esoteric Far East training, but employed by law enforcement and most modern military forces as well. It also provided better options for armor (reinforced clothing can provide up to 2 dice with no Dex penalty and full military armor is at 5 dice with only a 2 dice Dex penalty) than previous editions.<br /> <br /> The combat-focused vampires took a minor hit with Potence no longer being automatic successes without spending blood, but gained with Celerity now adding to the Dexterity when you're not spending blood on it (Dex 5, Celerity 3 and Brawl 5 could let you bite and parry with 7 dice each without spending any blood points on it) and a couple of the lower level Disciplines being made more useful (The first dot in the sepulchur path now letting you see/speak to the dead for example).<br /> <br /> If someone wants something from a previous edition we've generally allowed things from Revised (subject to Storyteller approval), but not from the 2e or 1e rules (no 1e Gaki with their Rift discipline thank you very much).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151714#post1151714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Just so long as we don't get any more D&amp;D analogies... (^VVVVVVV^)</div> </div> </div> </div>Analogies are just means of explaining something... any time you do a comparison its never going to be perfect. I'm sorry the D&amp;D one offended you so; but the use of levels and ability score priorities was a very easy way to illustrate my point.<br /> <br /> In this case my point was just that, because a lot of their ability is loaded into a specific non-variable trait (their alternate forms) and those forms require a bit of further investment to really show their worth (having tertiary physical stats and no ranks in brawl makes Crinos far less useful than it could be; to the point where a single &quot;typical soldier&quot; could be a life or death threat to them) their gift selection tends to be more lackluster than for vamps or mages.<br /> <br /> Each one is probably about as good as a rank one discipline, but they can't actually get ANY rank two or three gifts until they've ranked up to the appropriate level while a vampire could start with three dots in one discipline; which is usually far more potent in effect than one dot in three disciplines; and only has to purchase abilities that best correspond to those gifts. Vampires also generally have a larger pool of resource points because they can buy up generation with background dots or 1 freebie point each and can usually replenish those points more quickly than a werewolf can (particular with gnosis). If they've got vessels or another really easy way to feed a vampire could burn through its entire pool a couple of times in a single night if it had to... Werewolves can recover some rage under the various circumstances, but only the first time each night they see the moon in the phase of auspice actually restores it all) and gnosis as well (the most common method is meditation and that has diminishing returns if used more than once a week).<br /> <br /> Meanwhile mages can do almost anything they want without even touching their resource pool (quintessence); they really only need to tap into it for emergencies and, with Prime 1 can theoretically carry around 20 - Paradox points at once if they have access to a sufficiently large node and with two spheres at three dots they can do all manner of things. Arete 3 is 8 freebies, but mages also get 7 dots in backgrounds instead of 5 so its more like a net -6 points relative to the other supernaturals and what you get with being able to put 3 dots in a sphere is worth way more than those six points would ever do you in a background or merit.<br /> <br /> The result is that a player who wants their werewolf to be more of a spellcaster/priest for their pack is going to feel much less like one than say, a vampire starting with 3 dots in thaumaturgy or one of the more mystical specialty disciplines or a mage with their six dots in spheres. But if they want to be really good at the &quot;spellcasting&quot; they eventually want to have they're going to need to be good with mental and social attributes and with mostly non-combat abilities. The reason I compare that to D&amp;D is that its similar to (but not identical) the old MAD (multi-attribute dependency) problem in that system when multiclassing between two radically different things (like fighter and wizard in D&amp;D... or combat-focus vs. more &quot;magical&quot; gift focused with werewolves). You have a finite pool of points to spend and too many things that need points to be useful and one option that's being counted as valuable on its own, but is really only valuable if enough points are put into it that someone building for something other than straight combat probably needs elsewhere.<br /> <br /> That's my point with it. For Crinos to be good enough to beat an average human soldier without difficulty (something just about vampire or mage could pull off in some fashion) a werewolf needs to devote points to Crinos enhancing attributes and abilities that some werewolf builds would probably rather spend elsewhere if they could. The result is that both its combat abilities and its magical abilities will both feel watered down compared to both a combat-focused werewolf or a truly magic focused mage/vampire... its basically stuck as the Jack of All Trades, Master of None... which isn't nearly as fun in a game where all the bases are mostly covered already because it means you end up just being second best at everything.<br /> <br /> Its less a problem in a straight werewolf campaign as the theurge is the best spellcaster of the wolves so they can shine relative to other wolves; but in a crossover campaign that lack of ability to shine really becomes apparent when a mage with Spirit 3, Life 3 can pull off most of your abilities and more as well (especially since mage magic is complete divorced from attributes and abilities so no requirement for dots in certain attributes or abilities just to make your gifts function optimally).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-21T18:07:01-07:00","id":1151802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151759#post1151759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're currently using the 20th Anniversary editions of all three lines (for the Mage side we're also using How Do You Do That? and Book of Secrets). As such, paradox is much less of a problem (just one point for a successful vulgar cast with or without witnesses and no automatic backlash each time a point is added) and casting a bit easier than in Revised. We lost specialized focus by sphere but gained the ability to use surpassed instruments with any effect and mythic threads, plus clarification on &quot;violence as focus&quot; (don't have to split the dice pool if you're using an action to hit, like throwing a fireball, shooting a gun or throwing a punch) and clarification of &quot;Hypothetical Average Passerby&quot; for determining coincidence or vulgar (which made our teleporter VERY happy).<br /> <br /> Likewise, Martial Arts and Do were cleaned up and clarified (I like that you have to have dots in other specific abilities including things like art, crafts, etiquette and academics in order to advance your rating in Do; its supposed to be a holistic lifestyle not just kicking people's heads in) and can really give an edge to the combat focused mage... particularly with the clarification that Martial Arts isn't just some esoteric Far East training, but employed by law enforcement and most modern military forces as well. It also provided better options for armor (reinforced clothing can provide up to 2 dice with no Dex penalty and full military armor is at 5 dice with only a 2 dice Dex penalty) than previous editions.<br /> <br /> The combat-focused vampires took a minor hit with Potence no longer being automatic successes without spending blood, but gained with Celerity now adding to the Dexterity when you're not spending blood on it (Dex 5, Celerity 3 and Brawl 5 could let you bite and parry with 7 dice each without spending any blood points on it) and a couple of the lower level Disciplines being made more useful (The first dot in the sepulchur path now letting you see/speak to the dead for example).<br /> <br /> If someone wants something from a previous edition we've generally allowed things from Revised (subject to Storyteller approval), but not from the 2e or 1e rules (no 1e Gaki with their Rift discipline thank you very much).</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't say that I feel the need of inclusion for a separate Martial Arts Ability (we already have Athletics, Brawl, Melee, and a system of maneuvers), but that's admittedly tangential to the thread topic.<br /> <br /> Have you ever had a Boli Zousizhe or post-W20 Mokole in the group? Because Fu Xi's Honor/Rage of the Nest Mother can provide a rather extreme tipping point to a defensive confrontation involving a Garou or Mokole and anything trying to attack their kin, their pack or wallow while at rest, etc. Adding permanent Honor to all physical Attributes for the scene is not a subtle benefit that would go unnoticed. W20 also goes in a new direction with some versions of Gifts that used to have over-wrought, Byzantine systems, cutting them in favor of adding extra dots to an Attribute or Ability, or bonus successes. No Hidden Thing, for example, flat out adds three extra dice to all Enigmas rolls, while King Fish nets you an effective bonus dot of Charisma (snag Venus Rising, and you might be able to start with Charisma contributing 7 dice instead of just 5). There are a lot of Gifts yet to receive revision, though, so it's still not exactly an effort as far along as listing every last Vampire Discipline twice (V20 and V20 DA), plus combo Disciplines from V20's aggregate Clanbook.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Analogies are just means of explaining something... any time you do a comparison its never going to be perfect. I'm sorry the D&amp;D one offended you so; but the use of levels and ability score priorities was a very easy way to illustrate my point. </div> </div> </div>It wasn't so much offensive as head-tiltingly nonsensical. Levels in D&amp;D are roughly equivalent to Ability ratings in the ST system (every Ability rating, but Ability ratings nonetheless), and the way it handles powers is fundamentally in the opposite direction of most WoD games, either in progression or in who gets allotted what. Everyone can conceivably learn to fly, but it seems that Garou and Fera can learn to do this much more easily, for example.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In this case my point was just that, because a lot of their ability is loaded into a specific non-variable trait (their alternate forms) and those forms require a bit of further investment to really show their worth (having tertiary physical stats and no ranks in brawl makes Crinos far less useful than it could be; to the point where a single &quot;typical soldier&quot; could be a life or death threat to them) their gift selection tends to be more lackluster than for vamps or mages. </div> </div> </div>Grappling can turn into the great equalizer even with no Brawl, though, since you can roll Strength to hit and deal damage, and Crinos gets +4 Strength...<br /> <br /> Alternately, effects like Fu Xi's Honor or Rage of the Nest Mother might come into play and just provide the kind of boosts that even blood can't get for vampires. Sure, a Philodox might not be such great shakes in most fights, but threaten something that can't fight back, and they rapidly turn into the scariest damn thing that you've ever seen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Each one is probably about as good as a rank one discipline </div> </div> </div>First Rank Gifts can vary considerably in potency; Master of Fire, for example, changes all fire damage to bashing for a scene, which is a good deal more effective than Fortitude 1, especially in concert with being able to heal bashing damage automatically. CE is the equivalent of Eldritch Prime, or an effect of Forces 2, Matter 2, and Prime 2. Some Fera even have higher ranked Garou gifts as starting powers, usually because they don't have the same facility with stepping sideways or level of durability (Ananasi can learn the equivalent of Icy Chill of Despair at chargen, which is a Rank 3 Gift on par with Presence 5).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> but they can't actually get ANY rank two or three gifts until they've ranked up to the appropriate level while a vampire could start with three dots in one discipline </div> </div> </div>I could have sworn that we went over totems. Including totems that can net you Rank 5 Gifts, like Katanka-Sonnak/Hyperion, which, if you put enough into it, will see everyone in the pack lighting up with Kiss of Helios. So Garou and Fera can, but all the members of a pack or sentai are going to have the same higher-ranked Gift or Gifts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> which is usually far more potent in effect than one dot in three disciplines; and only has to purchase abilities that best correspond to those gifts. Vampires also generally have a larger pool of resource points because they can buy up generation with background dots or 1 freebie point each and can usually replenish those points more quickly than a werewolf can (particular with gnosis). If they've got vessels or another really easy way to feed a vampire could burn through its entire pool a couple of times in a single night if it had to... Werewolves can recover some rage under the various circumstances, but only the first time each night they see the moon in the phase of auspice actually restores it all) and gnosis as well (the most common method is meditation and that has diminishing returns if used more than once a week). </div> </div> </div>Gnosis recovery is why you hang around the caern for an engling hunt or perform tasks for spirits. Rage, though, that's so easy to get that you can exceed your normal maximum; one of the lost rules from 1e that seemed to be a pretty good guideline was that Rage rolls that don't catapult you into frenzy give you back points equal to the successes rolled, but you can, by W20's own word on the matter, get Rage back just by getting into a fight, or because the screwy dice probabilities caused something irritating to happen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Meanwhile mages can do almost anything they want without even touching their resource pool (quintessence); they really only need to tap into it for emergencies and, with Prime 1 can theoretically carry around 20 - Paradox points at once if they have access to a sufficiently large node and with two spheres at three dots they can do all manner of things. Arete 3 is 8 freebies, but mages also get 7 dots in backgrounds instead of 5 so its more like a net -6 points relative to the other supernaturals and what you get with being able to put 3 dots in a sphere is worth way more than those six points would ever do you in a background or merit. </div> </div> </div>On the other hand, Arete is the only thing that really matters in Mage, so any personality and quirks your character has that really demand a mechanical grounding, like their past in car repair, tend to impair their magickal capabilities at chargen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The result is that a player who wants their werewolf to be more of a spellcaster/priest for their pack </div> </div> </div>There is no such role. We don't need fireballs in this modern era of high explosives (and quite frankly, I'm skeptical that they're even a thing outside of D&amp;D's very wargame-influenced take on magic), and all Garou tend to learn a few rites, since it's often difficult to get Wisdom and Honor Renown otherwise. Rites, incidentally, require no xp to learn.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> is going to feel much less like one than say, a vampire starting with 3 dots in thaumaturgy or one of the more mystical specialty disciplines or a mage with their six dots in spheres. </div> </div> </div>Garou and Fera can get Rituals 5 and start out the game with the ability to travel on moon bridges or do things that take mages Spirit 4 and, often, combination of other Spheres to accomplish. It's really only as limited as the willingness of writers to write higher-level rites, and in a pinch, I wouldn't be averse to using the guidelines in the Bone Flowers Dharma Book (p 35) to abscond with rituals normally limited to those same vampire Disciplines, so long as the character had a high enough Rituals rating and the requisite Occult Ability.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But if they want to be really good at the &quot;spellcasting&quot; they eventually want to have they're going to need to be good with mental and social attributes and with mostly non-combat abilities. The reason I compare that to D&amp;D is that its similar to (but not identical) the old MAD (multi-attribute dependency) problem in that system when multiclassing between two radically different things (like fighter and wizard in D&amp;D... or combat-focus vs. more &quot;magical&quot; gift focused with werewolves). You have a finite pool of points to spend and too many things that need points to be useful and one option that's being counted as valuable on its own, but is really only valuable if enough points are put into it that someone building for something other than straight combat probably needs elsewhere. </div> </div> </div>Far less MAD here, since Garou and Fera only need to buy up Rituals along with Charisma, Wits... and Stamina. You can do that much easier than you get limited Ability score boosts in D&amp;D. Especially since the priority system of chargen that WoD games use means that you don't really ever have extreme dump stats unless you really want to emphasize a quality and don't have freebies left to boost it up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's my point with it. For Crinos to be good enough to beat an average human soldier without difficulty (something just about vampire or mage could pull off in some fashion) a werewolf needs to devote points to Crinos enhancing attributes and abilities that some werewolf builds would probably rather spend elsewhere if they could. </div> </div> </div>You've been going rather far to the extreme in ineptness for that, though. Garou can pick up an incidental extra dot or two here and there and have substantial &quot;newbie gains&quot; that other games' characters won't. Getting higher Brawl and Dex can wait until your Rage is also high (again, grappling is the changers' friend!)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The result is that both its combat abilities and its magical abilities will both feel watered down compared to both a combat-focused werewolf or a truly magic focused mage/vampire... its basically stuck as the Jack of All Trades, Master of None... which isn't nearly as fun in a game where all the bases are mostly covered already because it means you end up just being second best at everything.<br /> <br /> Its less a problem in a straight werewolf campaign as the theurge is the best spellcaster of the wolves </div> </div> </div>Those things are not even remotely alike, again. Rites are not meant to be anything like casting magic missile at the darkness. Some Gifts are, though. There are a number of different ones for burning people up in varying ways; the Bastet one is particularly nasty, since it does Ferocity Renown in automatic levels of aggravated damage, and it does continuous damage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> so they can shine relative to other wolves; but in a crossover campaign that lack of ability to shine really becomes apparent when a mage with Spirit 3, Life 3 can pull off most of your abilities and more as well (especially since mage magic is complete divorced from attributes and abilities so no requirement for dots in certain attributes or abilities just to make your gifts function optimally). </div> </div> </div>A mage that spend the time and xp to learn those won't be able to fly or run through the sky. Also, mages who devote that time and xp to Spheres and Arete will have to lean on them in order to shoot, fight, etc, or even simply just influence and convince others, because Arete and spheres are far more expensive than Abilities are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-22T08:47:22-07:00","id":1151946,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151802#post1151802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have you ever had a Boli Zousizhe or post-W20 Mokole in the group?</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope... the only time we even see werewolf PC's is when we run crossovers. We have one player who likes werewolves, the rest will play in a game with a werewolf as long as we don't have to play one. In crossovers, our mix tends to be 1-2 mages, 2-3 vamps and the one werewolf while our single splat games are 90% Mage (due to available ST interest).<br /> <br /> Since we're always set in some major US city (Chicago, New York, Boston, Seattle) the werewolves tend to be mostly Glasswalkers and Bonegnawers with our lone wolf player going with another common wolf tribe occasionally. Anything that's supposed to be ultra-rare and/or not generally indigenous to the continent just doesn't show up. The vamp players also stick to Camarilla and the occasional Ravnos and avoid the rare bloodlines/disciplines and the mage players always run with Traditionalists or Orphans in the crossovers.<br /> <br /> That lack of actual PC werewolves is why I don't pay much attention to Totems or Rites. One PC wolf does not a decent pack totem make and you need three wolves minimum to perform a rite. How much access to those is largely dependent on the ST's whim rather than anything an actual player pack could build for. Likewise, if its not in the core book only the werewolf fan has even heard of it. Heck, I only know what a Mokole is because W20 had it in the back end of the book and we picked up a copy of W20 core because we'd decided to use M/V20 for our most recent game and wanted our one werewolf fan to be on the same edition as far as rules went. Oddball totems like Hyperion that you mentioned aren't even on our radar.<br /> <br /> I suspect a LOT of our disconnect comes because you're used to playing in games that are either werewolf exclusive or werewolf heavy crossovers. A lot of the stuff you're mentioning sounds like stuff from obscure splatbooks and/or that require multiple wolves around to really pull off. We don't get those things in our games because other than the one PC, every other wolf we'd meet is a ST-controlled NPC.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151802#post1151802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Grappling can turn into the great equalizer even with no Brawl, though, since you can roll Strength to hit and deal damage, and Crinos gets +4 Strength...</div> </div> </div> </div>The default soldier from the rules has Str 4 and Brawl 3-4. They're more likely to win that grappling match than a tertiary physical no brawl wolf (i.e. Str 6 + Brawl 0). Even if it succeeds, grapple damage is bashing; and the soldier has decent stamina and body armor that can soak bashing.<br /> <br /> Which is, again, my point. One generic soldier guy can go toe to toe with a non-combat wolf and have a decent shot of taking down that wolf. Having an ability that almost lets you win against one generic non-supernatural mook isn't all that useful. So the wolf can devote their points to what will let it use its more auspice specific abilities better and accept that a big chunk of their supposed power isn't all that useful -OR- they sacrifice being better at what sets them apart for enough combat focused attributes/abilities to not get clobbered by an average soldier... while still not actually being as good at that as the guy who IS focused purely on combat.<br /> <br /> Which is why a lot of our players in the crossovers who wants the werewolf spirituality, but don't want to be combat focused just make Dreamspeaker mages.<br /> <br /> Those can easily start with Life 3/Spirit 3, which covers quite a bit of the Werewolf paradigm right out of the gate (all from the common magickal effects charts in M20 core)... adapt self/others to environments, cause/cure disease, cosmetic alterations of form (self or others), growing yourself new limbs or other features (claws, fangs, wings), healing/harming self /others (including werecreatures and spirits), increasing attributes, soaking aggravated damage, awakening an object's spirit, conjuring a spirit, seeing/speaking to spirits, stepping sideways and seeing auras.<br /> <br /> They can also get their own version of the Totem Background and can have Fetishes via the Wonder background and they don't have half their supernatural abilities tied up in growing into a furry rage beast that has only about a 50/50 shot at taking out a typical human infantryman on its own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151802#post1151802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CE is the equivalent of Eldritch Prime, or an effect of Forces 2, Matter 2, and Prime 2.</div> </div> </div> </div>.<br /> Except having just that one ability is a two-dot background for a mage (as a wonder) while actual Forces 2, Matter 2 and Prime 2 let you use <b>telekinesis</b>, <b>accelerate your speed</b>, <b>fly</b>, <b>turn invisible</b>, create physical illusions, <b>control/move fire and electricity</b>, <b>create gusts of wind</b>, <b>augment damage using existing forces</b>, enchant weapons and armor, hold extra quintessence in your pattern, make ordinary weapons inflict aggravated damage, transmute elements into other elements or into energy... and those are just the common effects in the core book... That's roughly equivalent to a DOZEN different gifts from those six sphere ranks. Heck, the BOLDED in the list above can be done with JUST Forces 2 alone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151802#post1151802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Gnosis recovery is why you hang around the caern for an engling hunt or perform tasks for spirits. Rage, though, that's so easy to get that you can exceed your normal maximum; <b>one of the lost rules from 1e that seemed to be a pretty good guideline was that Rage rolls that don't catapult you into frenzy give you back points equal to the successes rolled</b>, but you can, by W20's own word on the matter, get Rage back just by getting into a fight, or because the screwy dice probabilities caused something irritating to happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Engling hunts when you're the only PC werewolf kinda suck. A lot of things for a lone werewolf in a crossover game suck (you need at least three werewolves to perform any Rite for example). Meanwhile the Mage just mediates at the node to get back lost Quintessence (if they've even spent any) and the vamp goes clubbing for a bit and comes back topped off with blood.<br /> <br /> And that rule you mentioned in bold is no longer official (I'd never even heard of it). The official guideline now is that botching, being humiliated or starting a confrontation MAY, at the storyteller's option, restore a single rage point to the wolf. The only automatic means of gaining one back are seeing the moon for the first time each night or starting a new story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, Arete is the only thing that really matters in Mage, so any personality and quirks your character has that really demand a mechanical grounding, like their past in car repair, tend to impair their magickal capabilities at chargen. </div> </div> </div>Car repair is 3-5 dots in technology. Since they don't have to have any rank in any abilities to work their magic (unless they're using that as an instrument for their magic... Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Repair) they could pick up 3 dots in Technology with their starting ability spreads and pop 2-4 freebies into it if they wanted it to start at 4-5. It only costs 8 freebies for Arete 3 which leaves you seven more to spend on other stuff before flaws.<br /> <br /> Likewise, spellcasting doesn't care about your attributes either so you can be as combat or non-combat focused in those as you need to be for your concept and it won't generally affect your ability to work magic.<br /> <br /> You start with six dots in spheres for free so there's rarely a need to blow seven points on a seventh at Chargen; not when a new one is just 10 xp or improving one from 1 to 2 is just current rating x8 while you could take three abilities from 3 to 4 dots and improve a background by 1 for the same price... which would cost you a lot more XP.<br /> <br /> As befits them, mages can start out as just about anyone you want them to be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You've been going rather far to the extreme in ineptness for that, though. Garou can pick up an incidental extra dot or two here and there and have substantial &quot;newbie gains&quot; that other games' characters won't. Getting higher Brawl and Dex can wait until your Rage is also high (again, grappling is the changers' friend!) </div> </div> </div>Just make sure your Willpower is higher than your Rage in a crossover game or your inability to interact with others socially can be a huge hindrance. God help the Rage 10, Willpower 5, Social tertiary wolf with -5 dice to all their social tasks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A mage that spend the time and xp to learn those won't be able to fly or run through the sky. Also, mages who devote that time and xp to Spheres and Arete will have to lean on them in order to shoot, fight, etc, or even simply just influence and convince others, because Arete and spheres are far more expensive than Abilities are. </div> </div> </div>I can start with my primary attributes in any grouping I need them to be and can get any talent, skill or knowledge I want to three dots using my starting points. Getting a skill to 4 or 5 is 2-4 freebies and I've still got 7 left over before flaws after getting to Arete 3. I've also got 7 dots of backgrounds (instead of 5 for vampires and werewolves) required so I'll have some extra interesting stuff there too.<br /> <br /> Blowing 7 freebies on an extra sphere is a complete waste at chargen when you've got six to start and new ones at low levels aren't all that expensive. You'll spend as much to get an ability from 4 to 5 (current rating 4 x 2) as it'll take you to get your second dot in a sphere (current rating 1 x 8). Better to use those 7 freebies on adding 1-2 dots of abilities and getting your willpower to the 8-10 range. Then take some flaws to add another 3 dots of abilities and another background point.<br /> <br /> A starting mage can easily have your usual 7/5/3 attributes in whatever you want (since they have no effect on your magic), 13/9/5 abilities in whatever you want + another 4 dots wherever you want (because unless you're using one as an instrument it has no effect on your magic either), 8 dots in backgrounds, Arete 3, Willpower 10, 6 dots in spheres and 7 points in flaws that suit the character. That doesn't sound like a character who has to rely on magic just to navigate basic situations to me at all.<br /> <br /> Personally, I tend to go a little heavier on the backgrounds and less on the abilities since you can only improve backgrounds with ST permission, but there's no one right answer to building a starting mage other than &quot;Buy Arete 3; its more than worth it.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yeah, it costs a LOT more to up the spheres (and arete first) but that's because Arete and the spheres are exponential. Rituals allow mages to build successes for a spell over the course of a number actions. A mage can make a maximum number of Arete rolls equal to their Willpower + Arete (which is another reason why WP 8-10 is extremely common for mages). Arete 3/Willpower 10 means up to 3 dice x 13 rolls = 39 dice. Increase the Arete to 4 and you've now got 4 dice x 14 dice rolls = 56 dice for your extended ritual.<br /> <br /> Similarly, Forces 2 can lift about 300 lb., Forces 3 can lift 2 tons, Forces 4 &quot;can lift small buildings&quot; and Forces 5 &quot;can lift most things this side of a mountain.&quot; (though that would certainly take a ritual... with minimum Arete 5 that's a LOT of potential successes on that ritual.<br /> <br /> There's also a LOT of improvement that can be achieved just by getting more spheres in the 1-3 dots range and that's much less expensive. Three dice for arete can be annoying, but even the most difficult effects are going to cap out at difficulty 8 (vulgar with witnesses) before you apply modifiers to them. Coincidental stuff can get dragged down to difficulty 3 pretty easily.<br /> <br /> My general plan is to grab a couple more dots in spheres to fill any basic holes (my current mage started out without entropy or prime, but one of the other mages has those we're in the process of teaching each other the ones we're missing) then work on Arete 4, then start in on any other abilities/attributes and less useful spheres before pushing any of the spheres to 4. I've often found it more useful to push for Arete 5 with a lot of spheres in the 2-3 range than go with Arete 4 and just 1-2 spheres at 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1328,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","user":"Chris24601","date":".  because math before coffee can suck. 10-22-2017, 11:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-22T13:53:40-07:00","id":1152014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151946#post1151946\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nope... the only time we even see werewolf PC's is when we run crossovers. We have one player who likes werewolves, the rest will play in a game with a werewolf as long as we don't have to play one. In crossovers, our mix tends to be 1-2 mages, 2-3 vamps and the one werewolf while our single splat games are 90% Mage (due to available ST interest).</div> </div> </div> </div>Quoth numerous Archer characters, a ton of stuff just started making sense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since we're always set in some major US city (Chicago, New York, Boston, Seattle) the werewolves tend to be mostly Glasswalkers and Bonegnawers with our lone wolf player going with another common wolf tribe occasionally. Anything that's supposed to be ultra-rare and/or not generally indigenous to the continent just doesn't show up. The vamp players also stick to Camarilla and the occasional Ravnos and avoid the rare bloodlines/disciplines and the mage players always run with Traditionalists or Orphans in the crossovers. </div> </div> </div>Boli Zousizhe are Glass Walkers, albeit ones that historically hail from China. The Gift in question can be acquired once you get one rank beyond chargen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That lack of actual PC werewolves is why I don't pay much attention to Totems or Rites. One PC wolf does not a decent pack totem make and you need three wolves minimum to perform a rite. How much access to those is largely dependent on the ST's whim rather than anything an actual player pack could build for. Likewise, if its not in the core book only the werewolf fan has even heard of it. Heck, I only know what a Mokole is because W20 had it in the back end of the book and we picked up a copy of W20 core because we'd decided to use M/V20 for our most recent game and wanted our one werewolf fan to be on the same edition as far as rules went. Oddball totems like Hyperion that you mentioned aren't even on our radar. </div> </div> </div>Garou can actually get personal totems at a high cost. As for rites, the main use of the rite of binding is for making talens, which can come in quite handy as one-shot items. Also, Katanka-Sonnak isn't really an oddball, since it acts as a secondary tribal totem for the Wendigo where anything heavenly is concerned, and the CoGs also like it, because its ban is to go out and help Fera.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I suspect a LOT of our disconnect comes because you're used to playing in games that are either werewolf exclusive or werewolf heavy crossovers. A lot of the stuff you're mentioning sounds like stuff from obscure splatbooks and/or that require multiple wolves around to really pull off. We don't get those things in our games because other than the one PC, every other wolf we'd meet is a ST-controlled NPC. </div> </div> </div>I got into W:tA around 21 years ago. My old group didn't favor it nearly as much when it came to the WoD, though. More recently, I did back most of the KS campaigns for W20 books, and my favorite parts of the game are the Mokole and Rokea, though Ananasi could have an interesting hook in digging in and using webbing to lay down traps. They can also get a sort of ghoul or blood bound servant equivalent with a rite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The default soldier from the rules has Str 4 and Brawl 3-4. They're more likely to win that grappling match than a tertiary physical no brawl wolf (i.e. Str 6 + Brawl 0). Even if it succeeds, grapple damage is bashing; and the soldier has decent stamina and body armor that can soak bashing. </div> </div> </div>That is not a typical soldier. That's the kind of talent that Dead Man's Hand would like to have, sans the focus on Brawl (naturally, they find Heavy Weapons to be preferable, so they might have avoided contacting such an operative because they believe this focus is suicidal). So an extremely exceptional soldier has about a coin flip's chance to take out the Laziest Garou On Mars, who doesn't have any form of compensation that you might expect for a Garou dealing with other matters, like a talen to absorb all projectiles fired toward it (it's a powder on the ground), to hide, or to start a tremendously hot fire.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which is, again, my point. One generic soldier guy can go toe to toe with a non-combat wolf and have a decent shot of taking down that wolf. Having an ability that almost lets you win against one generic non-supernatural mook isn't all that useful. So the wolf can devote their points to what will let it use its more auspice specific abilities better and accept that a big chunk of their supposed power isn't all that useful -OR- they sacrifice being better at what sets them apart for enough combat focused attributes/abilities to not get clobbered by an average soldier... while still not actually being as good at that as the guy who IS focused purely on combat. </div> </div> </div>Your point is spoiled by the lack of use of actual generic soldiers. Three and four dots is rather far above the norm here, since Dead Man's Hand typically have 3s in physicals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which is why a lot of our players in the crossovers who wants the werewolf spirituality, but don't want to be combat focused just make Dreamspeaker mages. </div> </div> </div>That's probably your group's bias over anything else. Dreamspeakers aren't quite so close to the way they operate, so it's not even remotely the same thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Those can easily start with Life 3/Spirit 3, which covers quite a bit of the Werewolf paradigm right out of the gate (all from the common magickal effects charts in M20 core)... adapt self/others to environments, cause/cure disease, cosmetic alterations of form (self or others), growing yourself new limbs or other features (claws, fangs, wings), healing/harming self /others (including werecreatures and spirits), increasing attributes, soaking aggravated damage, awakening an object's spirit, conjuring a spirit, seeing/speaking to spirits, stepping sideways and seeing auras. </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They can also get their own version of the Totem Background </div> </div> </div>Mage's version of Totem is rather underwhelming and can't provide an ally that can push the upper limits of Jaggling power across the board and fight alongside you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and can have Fetishes via the Wonder background </div> </div> </div>But without the ability to make more until they reach Arete 4 and raise Spirit to a similar level. For Garou, this requires a Knowledge at 3 dots and a three dot rite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and they don't have half their supernatural abilities tied up in growing into a furry rage beast that has only about a 50/50 shot at taking out a typical human infantryman on its own. </div> </div> </div>Again, that wasn't a typical grunt that you provided stats for.<br /> <br /> .<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except having just that one ability is a two-dot background for a mage (as a wonder) while actual Forces 2, Matter 2 and Prime 2 let you use <b>telekinesis</b>, <b>accelerate your speed</b>, <b>fly</b>, <b>turn invisible</b>, create physical illusions, <b>control/move fire and electricity</b>, <b>create gusts of wind</b>, <b>augment damage using existing forces</b>, enchant weapons and armor, hold extra quintessence in your pattern, make ordinary weapons inflict aggravated damage, transmute elements into other elements or into energy... and those are just the common effects in the core book... That's roughly equivalent to a DOZEN different gifts from those six sphere ranks. Heck, the BOLDED in the list above can be done with JUST Forces 2 alone. </div> </div> </div>It still takes the Mage another dot in Arete and several Spheres to do, and Changelings also have it as a two-dot power. So it is not, in fact, a one-dot power. And it's just the tip of the iceberg, here. Your unfamiliarity with Werewolf is telling, and your blind spots could hide a truck barreling down (said truck being everything not in the core book).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Engling hunts when you're the only PC werewolf kinda suck. </div> </div> </div>Offscreen them, then.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of things for a lone werewolf in a crossover game suck (you need at least three werewolves to perform any Rite for example). Meanwhile the Mage just mediates at the node to get back lost Quintessence (if they've even spent any) and the vamp goes clubbing for a bit and comes back topped off with blood. </div> </div> </div>Quite frankly, the werewolf should be ripping the mage and vampire apart for that, but that's another dimension of crossover dilemmas.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And that rule you mentioned in bold is no longer official (I'd never even heard of it). </div> </div> </div>On the other hand, I've had a lot of people vehemently argue that past editions aren't necessarily off-limits for pages and pages of forum space, and it does make some degree of sense, so that's really on your lack of interest in the matter which you stepped into as an ostensible expert.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The official guideline now is that botching, being humiliated or starting a confrontation MAY, at the storyteller's option, restore a single rage point to the wolf. The only automatic means of gaining one back are seeing the moon for the first time each night or starting a new story. </div> </div> </div>And if you keep shaving sources of power off from anything, it could be solely ST fiat for anyone to regain their stores.<br /> <br /> [quote]<br /> Car repair is 3-5 dots in technology[quote]<br /> <br /> Crafts, from where i'm sitting. Technology is for electronics and a Knowledge. This is one juncture where Mage and the other games don't agree... and I'm not keen on giving Mage any room, so I'm going to say that it's very much the odd one out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since they don't have to have any rank in any abilities to work their magic (unless they're using that as an instrument for their magic... Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Repair) they could pick up 3 dots in Technology with their starting ability spreads and pop 2-4 freebies into it if they wanted it to start at 4-5. It only costs 8 freebies for Arete 3 which leaves you seven more to spend on other stuff before flaws. </div> </div> </div>And that would detract from spending all your freebies on Arete and Willpower.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Likewise, spellcasting doesn't care about your attributes either so you can be as combat or non-combat focused in those as you need to be for your concept and it won't generally affect your ability to work magic. </div> </div> </div>Likewise, you're proving my point. The more character you give a Mage accounting for concept, the less likely they are to focus on things about being a Mage. This seems... odd.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You start with six dots in spheres for free so there's rarely a need to blow seven points on a seventh at Chargen; not when a new one is just 10 xp or improving one from 1 to 2 is just current rating x8 while you could take three abilities from 3 to 4 dots and improve a background by 1 for the same price... which would cost you a lot more XP.<br /> <br /> As befits them, mages can start out as just about anyone you want them to be. </div> </div> </div>You conveniently ignore how Mage really rewards you for sinking points into as much Arete as possible to start and ignoring as much of your base concept as possible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Just make sure your Willpower is higher than your Rage in a crossover game or your inability to interact with others socially can be a huge hindrance. God help the Rage 10, Willpower 5, Social tertiary wolf with -5 dice to all their social tasks. </div> </div> </div>Rage 10 would also bring The Curse into effect when dealing with normal humans, but then again, you don't have to roll Intimidation to scare most people, and you have a high store of extra actions. Also, Rage 10 could much more easily derive from a central part of a character concept.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can start with my primary attributes in any grouping I need them to be and can get any talent, skill or knowledge I want to three dots using my starting points. Getting a skill to 4 or 5 is 2-4 freebies and I've still got 7 left over before flaws after getting to Arete 3. I've also got 7 dots of backgrounds (instead of 5 for vampires and werewolves) required so I'll have some extra interesting stuff there too. </div> </div> </div>So why does this game leave the option of not starting with more Arete around, again? It seems tedious to make that accounting every time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Blowing 7 freebies on an extra sphere is a complete waste at chargen when you've got six to start and new ones at low levels aren't all that expensive. You'll spend as much to get an ability from 4 to 5 (current rating 4 x 2) as it'll take you to get your second dot in a sphere (current rating 1 x 8). Better to use those 7 freebies on adding 1-2 dots of abilities and getting your willpower to the 8-10 range. Then take some flaws to add another 3 dots of abilities and another background point.<br /> <br /> A starting mage can easily have your usual 7/5/3 attributes in whatever you want (since they have no effect on your magic), 13/9/5 abilities in whatever you want + another 4 dots wherever you want (because unless you're using one as an instrument it has no effect on your magic either), 8 dots in backgrounds, Arete 3, Willpower 10, 6 dots in spheres and 7 points in flaws that suit the character. That doesn't sound like a character who has to rely on magic just to navigate basic situations to me at all.<br /> <br /> Personally, I tend to go a little heavier on the backgrounds and less on the abilities since you can only improve backgrounds with ST permission, but there's no one right answer to building a starting mage other than &quot;Buy Arete 3; its more than worth it.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yeah, it costs a LOT more to up the spheres (and arete first) but that's because Arete and the spheres are exponential. Rituals allow mages to build successes for a spell over the course of a number actions. A mage can make a maximum number of Arete rolls equal to their Willpower + Arete (which is another reason why WP 8-10 is extremely common for mages). Arete 3/Willpower 10 means up to 3 dice x 13 rolls = 39 dice. Increase the Arete to 4 and you've now got 4 dice x 14 dice rolls = 56 dice for your extended ritual. </div> </div> </div>Yes. Mage hoists itself with this dilemma. I feel it kind of ruins at least a bit of whatever message it's trying to present in &quot;power is an illusion and a trap&quot;. If that's even supposed to be one, I can't keep track and it might not even matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Similarly, Forces 2 can lift about 300 lb., Forces 3 can lift 2 tons, Forces 4 &quot;can lift small buildings&quot; and Forces 5 &quot;can lift most things this side of a mountain.&quot; (though that would certainly take a ritual... with minimum Arete 5 that's a LOT of potential successes on that ritual. </div> </div> </div>And a level 5 rite can create a caern. You can even start with knowledge of the rite internalized. It'd be odd, but not impossible, since a lot of W:tA revolves around trying to protect fraying bits of wisdom and culture.<br /> <br /> That's, what, Prime and Spirit 5 for a mage, if crossover concerns even allow it due to important differences between the concept of a caern and a node?<br /> <br /> (On a side note, Rokea can use another rite of the same level to fuse several of their kind into a ship-sinking shark kaijuu that has stats over and above what you would get simply from using Leviathan, which is an extra 5 all around on Physicals. But then, the stat boosts for that tend to be a bit of a moot point if you're fighting dry land critters in the water... unless you have the Darkwater Gift that allows your shark and huge shark forms to breathe air and fly through it as well.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's also a LOT of improvement that can be achieved just by getting more spheres in the 1-3 dots range and that's much less expensive. Three dice for arete can be annoying, but even the most difficult effects are going to cap out at difficulty 8 (vulgar with witnesses) before you apply modifiers to them. Coincidental stuff can get dragged down to difficulty 3 pretty easily.<br /> <br /> My general plan is to grab a couple more dots in spheres to fill any basic holes (my current mage started out without entropy or prime, but one of the other mages has those we're in the process of teaching each other the ones we're missing) then work on Arete 4, then start in on any other abilities/attributes and less useful spheres before pushing any of the spheres to 4. I've often found it more useful to push for Arete 5 with a lot of spheres in the 2-3 range than go with Arete 4 and just 1-2 spheres at 4. </div> </div> </div>A GAME WHERE CREATIVITY COUNTS ABOVE ALL ELSE!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T09:54:19-07:00","id":1152221,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151557#post1151557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sigh. I guess people just want to cheer for their favorite team. I like them all and am still standing by the opinion that no splat is &quot;the best&quot;, they're just focused differently.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bMy favorite team it's <u>mages</u>...but when I see an Akashic that's a <b>wealthy</b> martial artist<u> with military equipment </u>and both <i>mystic firearm skill</i> and <i>movements</i> as Instrument that always wears his armor...<br /> <br /> Vs a plain werewolf...<br /> <br /> I have to protest.<br /> <br /> Those aren't PC, one it's a clear combat munchkin and the other it's so conservative that one can hardly talk about a combat specialist-may as well be a sports specialist that's good at pugilism. No one would think that's a fair comparison. At least expend the same points and think about tactics for both of them<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Missed that, what was the result? </div> </div> </div>It was predictable. Abomination PC won. With Garou and other Fera as a &quot;second&quot; and vampires as third. Mages were consistently unimpressive, but the mystic with the &quot;sword of awesomeness&quot; did surprisingly good against Fera (lost, but not as bad as others). I didn't like how mages were represented, mages aren't like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T10:33:09-07:00","id":1152227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152014#post1152014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Boli Zousizhe are Glass Walkers, albeit ones that historically hail from China. The Gift in question can be acquired once you get one rank beyond chargen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Finally found the gift, It still requires a lot of Storyteller fiat to invoke. If no one is attacking a helpless member of your actual pack or your kinfolk its never going to come up. That makes it very limited in a crossover where many members of the PC group aren't fellow garou or kinfolk and the PC mages/vamps aren't interested in attacking a bunch of werewolves on the other side of the planet (the book even states that those gifts just aren't seen outside of the Boli Zousizhe offshoot that is found almost exclusively in China).<br /> <br /> If we ever run a campaign set in China our werewolf player might be interested in trying one, but again, almost no utility outside of that.<br /> <br /> I've noticed you like to cite a lot of edge cases; like were-dinosaurs and minor tribes with exclusive gifts; in proving how awesome werewolves are. Not every Storyteller is going to allow those.<br /> Our games stick to the things that the setting says are common to an area. Weredinosaurs from Africa and a tribe of werewolves whose domains are in China are not likely to turn up during a campaign set during a long dark Chicago winter (because we want the vampire players to not be quite so limited).<br /> <br /> Its hard enough getting a normal city werewolf, some vamps and a few mages to cooperate in a way that doesn't stretch credibility.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152014#post1152014\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Garou can actually get personal totems at a high cost. As for rites, the main use of the rite of binding is for making talens, which can come in quite handy as one-shot items.Also, Katanka-Sonnak isn't really an oddball, since it acts as a secondary tribal totem for the Wendigo where anything heavenly is concerned, and the CoGs also like it, because its ban is to go out and help Fera.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yet Katanka-Sonnak only even shows up in the core book as part of the name of a gift that was used by the extinct Croatoan tribe. If its so common a totem why is that literally the only place it even appears in the core book? Like I said... edge cases.<br /> <br /> I kinda get the sense that you seem to think African weredinosaurs with a totem linked to an extinct North American Garou tribe hanging out with a Garou from a Chinese outsider tribe is the default way to play werewolf and they just go over to werespider's house for curds and whey periodically.<br /> <br /> As to personal totems, the only Storytellers willing to run a game with werewolf players are only familiar with the totems in the main book; none of which provide things like sun whips to their followers (which probably takes quite the investment in points... which are limited at chargen).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is not a typical soldier. That's the kind of talent that Dead Man's Hand would like to have, sans the focus on Brawl (naturally, they find Heavy Weapons to be preferable, so they might have avoided contacting such an operative because they believe this focus is suicidal). </div> </div> </div>I just grabbed the one right out M20 core. They say to use these stats for &quot;<i>security specialists, mob enforcers, SWAT team members, assassins, gangbangers, military personnel, martial artists, and so on.</i>&quot; The stats are reasonably close to a Criminal Enforcer in V20 as well (i.e. <i>&quot;a reasonably tough, savvy member of the underworld.&quot;</i>).<br /> <br /> The book further suggests &quot;<i>The Allies, Backup, and Spies Backgrounds generally employ these types of characters.</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> For comparison, here's a few others...<br /> <br /> <u><b>Thug</b></u> (M20)<br /> <i><b>Suggested Attributes:</b> Strength 3, Dexterity 2, Stamina 2, Charisma 2, Manipulation 2, Appearance 2, Perception 2, Intelligence 1, Wits 2<br /> <b>Suggested Abilities:</b> Alertness 2, Area Knowledge 1, Athletics 2, Brawl 2, Crafts 2, Drive 1, Intimidation 1, Melee 2, Streetwise 1, Technology 1<br /> <b>Willpower:</b> 3</i><br /> <br /> <u><b>Beat Cop</b></u> (M20)<br /> <i><b>Suggested Attributes:</b> Strength 3, Dexterity 2, Stamina 3, Charisma 2, Manipulation 3, Appearance 2, Perception 3, Intelligence 2, Wits 3<br /> <b>Suggested Abilities:</b> Alertness 2, Area Knowledge 3, Athletics 2, Brawl 2, Computer 1, Drive 2, Firearms 3, Intimidation 1, Investigation 2, Law 2, Melee 2, Stealth 1, Streetwise 2, Technology 2<br /> <b>Willpower:</b> 5</i><br /> <br /> <b><u>Security Specialist / Mob Enforcer / SWAT Team / Assassin / Gangbanger / Military Personnel / Martial Artist</u></b> (M20)<br /> <i><b>Suggested Attributes:</b> Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Stamina 3, Charisma 3, Manipulation 4, Appearance 1-3, Perception 2-4, Intelligence 2-4, Wits 3-4<br /> <b>Suggested Abilities:</b> Alertness 2, Athletics 2-4, Brawl 3-4, Drive 2, Firearms 3-5, Intimidation 3, Martial Arts 0-4, Melee 1-3, Stealth 2, Streetwise 3-5, Subterfuge 2, Technology 1-3<br /> <b>Willpower:</b> 6</i><br /> <br /> <u><b>Criminal Enforcer</b></u> (V20)<br /> <i><b>Attributes: </b>Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Stamina 3, Charisma 3, Manipulation 4, Appearance 1, Perception 2, Intelligence 2, Wits 3<br /> <b>Abilities: </b>Alertness 2, Athletics 3, Brawl 3, Drive 2, Finance 2, Firearms 3 (or higher), Larceny 3, Melee 2, Stealth 2, Streetwise 3 (or higher), Subterfuge 2<br /> <b>Willpower:</b> 6</i><br /> <br /> <u><b>Dead Man's Hand</b></u> (W20)<br /> <i><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 3, Dexterity 3, Stamina 3, Charisma 2, Manipulation 2, Perception 3, Intelligence 2, Wits 2<br /> <b>Abilities (typical):</b> Alertness 2, Athletics 3, Brawl 2, Intimidation 2, Leadership 1, Subterfuge 1, Drive 2, Firearms 2, Melee 2, Stealth 2, Survival 2, Investigation 2, Medicine 1, Technology 2<br /> <b>Willpower:</b> 6</i><br /> <br /> I guess Dead Man's Hand isn't all that elite if a &quot;reasonably tough, savvy member of the underworld&quot; can kick their ass. Or maybe something that toned down is all a werewolf can handle and still feel powerful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your point is spoiled by the lack of use of actual generic soldiers. Three and four dots is rather far above the norm here, since Dead Man's Hand typically have 3s in physicals. </div> </div> </div>The average soldier IS well above the norm in terms of physical ability. You have to run, jump and climb in 80+ pounds of gear, go days on end with 1-2 hours of sleep a night in the field and drill in a number of actions that hone your reflexes and hand-eye coordination.<br /> <br /> 3 dots in your physical abilities would be the minimum to not get washed out of the military (MINIMUM PFT requirements for males age 17-21 are 42 pushups in 2 minutes, 53 sit-us in 2 minutes and 2 miles in 15:54 seconds). 4 dots in Strength wouldn't be unusual... think about how much a man in full body armor weighs. Now imagine having to fireman carry him out of a hot zone because he's been wounded while still in your own gear. Does that sound closer to &quot;Lift 250 lb.&quot; (STR 3) or &quot;Lift 400 lb.&quot; (STR 4) to you?<br /> <br /> 2 dots is an everyday person on the street. Cops, Soldiers and those engaged in regular physical activity are going to be above average and they comprise less than 1% of the population (ex. 300+ million people in the United States... 1 million in law enforcement, 2 million in armed forces both active and reserve in the united states).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mage's version of Totem is rather underwhelming and can't provide an ally that can push the upper limits of Jaggling power across the board and fight alongside you. </div> </div> </div>Here's the five dot description;<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Your totem loves you! In dreams, visions, omens, and solid form, that spirit provides frequent company and aid. You get three dice toward the appropriate Ability, add two dots each in Cosmology and Spirit Lore, and may receive help in desperate situations. Your bond to that spirit is obvious to anyone and marks you as a very strange, primal, and probably intimidating person.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> It also notes; <i>&quot;For a variety of potential spirit guides and their associated characteristics, check out the Totem Spirits section of Appendix I, (pp. 633-636).&quot;</i><br /> <br /> One of those traits is Manifestations: <i>&quot;When a totem sends an aspect of itself in physical form, that totem avatar usually manifests as an animal or human-like figure. Generally, the manifestation has the game Traits of a related animal or character, although it can \u2013 at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion \u2013 be as powerful as the spirit (and the Storyteller) wants it to be.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> It also notes that they have a Brood: <i>&quot;Most totems, especially animal spirit totems, have a brood: lesser spirit Gafflings in its family. Such spirits reflect the totem\u2019s Associations and identity: Raven sends spirit crows, crow girls, and raven men, but Glitch spits out electrical spirits and data-eater viruses. Unlike the totem itself, these spirits can be caught, fought, or contained\u2026 although a mage who does such a thing had better watch out for the associated totem afterward unless that totem has sent its broodling to aid its human ally.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But without the ability to make more until they reach Arete 4 and raise Spirit to a similar level. For Garou, this requires a Knowledge at 3 dots and a three dot rite. </div> </div> </div>Mages can use their abilities at-will and only make items with abilities they already possess, so their need to make items isn't as important to them... it'd be more about letting an ally have that power when you're not there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, that wasn't a typical grunt that you provided stats for. </div> </div> </div>Except it was a typical grunt.<br /> <br /> You just have the mistaken notion that a typical grunt can't lift more than hundred pounds and would need over 25 minutes to run 2 miles... which is what 2 dots in the physical stats translates to. That's not a typical grunt... that's a washout from basic training.<br /> <br /> DEX 3, STA 3 can just BARELY meet the 2 mile time minimum for basic training if you let them run at full speed for a full 2 minutes (20 turns) per point of Stamina and then jog the rest of the way. You need STR 3 just for a grown man to do a pull up (since they usually weigh more than 100 lb.).<br /> <br /> Dead Man's Hand can barely meet the MINIMUM physical requirements to graduate basic training. If they're elite its because they've got 4 dots in some abilities, not because their attributes are superhuman compared to other soldiers.<br /> .<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It still takes the Mage another dot in Arete and several Spheres to do, and Changelings also have it as a two-dot power. So it is not, in fact, a one-dot power. And it's just the tip of the iceberg, here. Your unfamiliarity with Werewolf is telling, and your blind spots could hide a truck barreling down (said truck being everything not in the core book). </div> </div> </div>And your unfamiliarity with Mage is telling. Remember, you were trying to say how awesome Create Elements was because it was equivalent to have six dots in spheres. Except those actual six dots in spheres for a mage aren't just that one little trick but the equivalent of a DOZEN+ more gifts as well. How many Ranks and Gifts would it take to get to even half that list of abilities a mage could pull off with their STARTING spheres (each costing 3 XP/level of the gift)?<br /> <br /> And yeah, it costs 4 freebie points to get to Arete 2... You make it sound like that's some sort of burden. Its not like I have to buy up my permanent rage (1 ea) or permanent gnosis (2 ea) just to make my abilities work more than a handful of times before I run out of gas. How many freebie points does the typical werewolf have to spend to get their rage, gnosis and willpower up to decent levels?<br /> <br /> Its costs a mage 8 freebies to max out their starting Arete. Mages also start with +2 dots in backgrounds (7 instead of 5) and +1-2 dots in Willpower (5 instead 3-4) relative to the Fera so its more like they spend 4 freebies relative to a Fera to max it out. That's ALL THEY NEED to cast any effect their spheres will allow as often as they want to cast it (Willpower and Avatar can help, but aren't needed to use magic at-will). So for the cost of just 2 permanent Gnosis... the mage is maxed in his spellcasting. No further abilities to buy up past 3 dots, no extra Gnosis points to use them more than a few times... they're free to spend points on whatever they want to (it costs them 5 extra points to max their Willpower if they want and Avatar can be bought with background points).<br /> <br /> Hell, it would cost more just to get an Ahroun's Permanent Rage to 10 (5 freebies, not counting the WP you need to perform even the simplest social tasks) than the relative cost of getting Arete to 3 (8-4 extras is 4 points). It would cost a Lupus more to get to Permanent Gnosis 10 (10 freebies) than it does for Arete 3 when you're not using the relative costs... and if you're a Homid Ragabash Glasswalker? Forget about it... all 22 points from freebies and flaws won't max your Rage and Gnosis (not to mention Willpower)... you'd cap at 7 Gnosis (12 points), 7 Rage (6 points), 7 Willpower (4 points) and have nothing to spend on anything else.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Quite frankly, the werewolf should be ripping the mage and vampire apart for that, but that's another dimension of crossover dilemmas. </div> </div> </div>So a mage doesn't have a right to use a node his own mentor created and a humanity 10 vampire can't accept voluntary gifts from his herd (the Kiss is quite pleasurably and if the vamp is careful, no more dangerous than what I do for the Red Cross every 8 weeks)? Thanks for reinforcing my impression of werewolves as self-entitled twink-munches who are going extinct because they're too stupid to live.<br /> <br /> They're furries with zero impulse control fighting Captain Planet villains. &quot;It smells BAD. MUST SMASH!!!&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, I've had a lot of people vehemently argue that past editions aren't necessarily off-limits for pages and pages of forum space, and it does make some degree of sense, so that's really on your lack of interest in the matter which you stepped into as an ostensible expert. </div> </div> </div>My expertise is in Mage and general WoD crossovers. My only point was that Mages and Vamps aren't nearly as behind the curve as you seem to think and outside of you dragging in edge-case African were-dinosaurs with pack totems too obscure to make it into the main book and wielding gifts exclusive to a tribe that is centered in China, I think I've proven the case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Crafts, from where i'm sitting. Technology is for electronics and a Knowledge. This is one juncture where Mage and the other games don't agree... and I'm not keen on giving Mage any room, so I'm going to say that it's very much the odd one out. </div> </div> </div>3 dots in Crafts then... big honking deal. You can still get it with your starting 13/9/5 allotment. Heck, you could get 3 dots in Crafts (automotive) and Technology (engineering) and Computers and Science (chemistry) and Academics, Alertness (to have a good ear for what the problem might be based on the sound the engine is making) and Athletics (just because they tend to be in decent shape) with your starting points and still have more left over.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And that would detract from spending all your freebies on Arete and Willpower. </div> </div> </div>As discussed... your Arete is capped at 3 dots (8 freebies). Willpower starts at 5 and can be capped for 5 freebies. That still leaves you 2 extra freebies and 7 background points before you even start picking up flaws (which I find to be fun RP hooks and will often end up with 8-9 points of flaws that fit a theme even if I only get points for 7 of them).<br /> <br /> As I said... Attributes 7/5/3, Abilities 13/9/5 +4 to any, Backgrounds 8, Arete 3, Willpower 10, Spheres 6 and Flaws 7 is a 100% viable starting Mage character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Likewise, you're proving my point. The more character you give a Mage accounting for concept, the less likely they are to focus on things about being a Mage. This seems... odd.<br /> <br /> You conveniently ignore how Mage really rewards you for sinking points into as much Arete as possible to start and ignoring as much of your base concept as possible. </div> </div> </div>You clearly have zero idea of what's actually involved in building a Mage character.<br /> <br /> You can only sink EIGHT freebie points into your Arete to start. That leaves you with 7 freebies + 7 backgrounds + any flaws on top of your basic attributes and abilities (which are not constrained by the ones needed to work gifts or disciplines) to further your concept while still having the maximum starting Arete.<br /> <br /> You also don't seem to get that because there's no one true way to work magic in Mage your Arete and magic can always work in support of your concept. A Mage mechanic might would use his ability to repair things as part of his focus for casting spells. His tools and his dots in Crafts/Engineering let him MacGuyver himself up a solution to any problem he faces (Forces 3, Matter 3 would create any number of abilities the Mage MacGuyver could pull off).<br /> <br /> Mages are holistic beings... they have no warring nature like a Vampire with its Beast or Werewolf and its Rage. Their magic supports and augments their mortal abilities (one of the reasons why one the larger chunks of the magic section is related &quot;magic enhancing skills&quot; (using magic to lower common task difficulties)&quot; and to &quot;skills enhancing magic (using practical knowledge to lower the difficulty in casting a spell).&quot;<br /> <br /> Let's put this together shall we?<br /> <br /> ----------<br /> <b>Angus the Auto Mechanic</b><br /> <br /> He's smart, a bit above average physically, but only average socially. STR 3, DEX 3, STA 2 (secondary), CHA 2, MAN 2, APP 2 (tertiary), PER 3, INT 4, WIT 3 (primary).<br /> <br /> Talent-wise, he's alert and likes to work out, and likes to get a read on people before speaking (Teritary). Alertness 2, Athletics 3, Empathy 2. (2 points from freebies)<br /> <br /> His skills are primarily with his hands, he's good at fixing stuff (and &quot;fixing&quot; stuff), can swing a pipe wrench if he has to, but prefers to sneak around if he can (Primary), Craft 4 (automotive), Drive 1, Melee 2, Security 3, Stealth 1, Technology 4 (engineering; its a skill in mage rather than a knowledge). (2 points from freebies)<br /> <br /> He's got some book learning and he picked up first aid in the boy scouts, but its not his top priority (Secondary): Academics 3, Computers 1, Science 3 (chemistry), Medicine 2.<br /> <br /> For Backgrounds he's got autoshop that makes decent money (Resources 3), his co-workers have seen him do enough crazy stuff with tech that they can actually help him out (Cult 2) and he's got a decently strong avatar (Avatar 3) that appears to him in the form of plans and blueprints and scribbled notes and vectors (like you see on shows like Mythbusters when they're analyzing the data). Eight points total.<br /> <br /> His paradigm is that &quot;Tech Holds All the Answers&quot; and his main practice is &quot;Weird Science.&quot; He uses brews/concoctions, devices/machines, the elements, mathematics, inventions, vehicles and &quot;weapons&quot; (mostly booby traps) as his instruments. He's got Arete 3; Forces 3 and Matter 3 that uses to MacGuyver/Mythbuster his way through any sort of problems he might run into.<br /> <br /> He's also a strong-willed fella; Willpower 10. Being strong-willed though he's also got a bit of a short fuse (2 point flaw) and his curiosity, particularly about new tech (2 point flaw) can sometimes get the better of him. But he won't go too far because he's got a wife and two kids at home to take care of (3 point flaw). Seven points in flaws total.<br /> ----------<br /> <br /> Does that seem to have the concept sacrificed in any way in the name of having better magic to you?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Rage 10 would also bring The Curse into effect when dealing with normal humans, but then again, you don't have to roll Intimidation to scare most people, and you have a high store of extra actions. Also, Rage 10 could much more easily derive from a central part of a character concept. </div> </div> </div>And it costs more, relatively speaking, than the maximum starting Arete does for a mage. You're also at potentially -7 dice to ALL social actions, even with other werewolves unless you brought your Willpower up quite a bit too.<br /> <br /> In fact, in a typical crossover game where there's more going on than just hitting people like some mindless misanthrope, I'd say unless your WP was at least 8 you'd be damned near unplayable as a character as you'd be nearly incapable of even communicating with others beyond the level of &quot;KILL YOU!!! RRRHAAAAAAHHHH!!!&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So why does this game leave the option of not starting with more Arete around, again? It seems tedious to make that accounting every time. </div> </div> </div>Because despite its utility, not everyone buys Arete 3 because of their concept. Some people want to play an neophyte with just 1-2 Arete who has to get by on their wits and just a bit of odds adjustment. You only need one success to spot weaknesses in a system with Entropy 1 or see in the dark with Forces 1.<br /> <br /> Also, going from Arete 2 to 3 is only 16 XP... about the same as going from rank 3-5 in an ability (14 XP) and a Seeking to raise their Arete score can require the Storyteller to put more focus on that character than they normally would in a session. If you start with Arete 1 then its only 8 XP and a Seeking to get to Arete 2. If your goal isn't &quot;Best-est character ever&quot; but having your Storyteller put the focus on your character, starting with an Arete 1-2 isn't a bad way to build up momentum in that respect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes. Mage hoists itself with this dilemma. I feel it kind of ruins at least a bit of whatever message it's trying to present in &quot;power is an illusion and a trap&quot;. If that's even supposed to be one, I can't keep track and it might not even matter. </div> </div> </div>You might be thinking of Awakening because the message of Mage is &quot;You have the power to change the world. What do you do with it?&quot;<br /> <br /> There was some stuff in the 2e Masters of the Art book that pointed out pursing the Arch-spheres (rank 6+ in a sphere) closed off the path to Ascension because you were putting too much focus in one tiny part of reality instead of trying to understand reality as a whole, but high Arete is generally a measure of your path to Ascension as the increased understanding that comes with it allows you to cast aside the tools you once relied on because you now understand they were just props and not the true source of your magic.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, the core conflict of Mage is &quot;What type of world do you want to leave behind?&quot; The Nephandi want to tear the world down. The Technocracy say Safety is worth the loss of Freedom and Wonder. The Traditions say &quot;Freedom and Wonder are worth the risks&quot; and the Marauders say &quot;I am the Walrus! Coo coo ca choo!&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A GAME WHERE CREATIVITY COUNTS ABOVE ALL ELSE! </div> </div> </div>Is there a reason you're screaming this?<br /> <br /> Did you score four+ successes on a rage check?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1328,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","user":"Chris24601","date":". 10-23-2017, 01:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T11:26:45-07:00","id":1152242,"title":"","content":"Holy wall of text Batman!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T11:58:56-07:00","id":1152250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152221#post1152221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was predictable. Abomination PC won. With Garou and other Fera as a &quot;second&quot; and vampires as third. Mages were consistently unimpressive, but the mystic with the &quot;sword of awesomeness&quot; did surprisingly good against Fera (lost, but not as bad as others). I didn't like how mages were represented, mages aren't like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, no. The mokole won, abomination was a close second though with get of fenris as third. Everything else was pwned.<br /> <br /> See here: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/837527-deadliest-supernatural-warrior-results-and-analysis\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...s-and-analysis</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T13:24:17-07:00","id":1152273,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6383\" data-vbnamecard=\"6383\">Prometheas</a><br /> \u200bThanks for the tread and the clarification.<br /> <br /> Perhaps I remembered the Abomination winning because it did won against the Mokole, albeit the Mokole won more battles overall.<br /> <br /> That thread holds a lot of info, but it had several premises, as can be seen in &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/769128-deadliest-supernatural-warrior-the-setup\">Deadliest supernatural warrior: the setup</a>&quot; that influence the results. The non combatant Garou is fairly above the strong human under these premises.<br /> <br /> Also all of this misses the point of having a crossover game.<br /> <br /> Since we're taliking about &quot;actual play experience&quot; (which can vary a lot according to a number of factors, for instance in my group the &quot;combat mage&quot; propposed by <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> would be a tiny bit suspect of the henious crime of min/max. I, for one, do not like to min/max my characters, and wouldn't add Resources 4 to a combat character unless being rich it's actually part of his concept, certainly not to buy an armor), let me talk about an &quot;actual play experience&quot; where it was better to have the members of the group to specialize in what they're good rather than having all of them &quot;on the same page&quot;:<br /> <br /> I'm a mage with a very strong perceptual capabilities (6 Spheres at 1-2), my character it's a very good investigator both by Spheres and by Focus (and skillset). In the group we have a Visionary Hunter that has a lot of perception based Edges, and his mundane investigation skills are great too. It happens that we both have a lot in common, but this causes an overlap of our expertices that often results in situations where it's enough for one of us to use our capabilities.<br /> In cases where our skills overlap the Hunter it's just better. A lot of times he hasn't to roll and thus do not risk failure, and when he has to roll he has a HUGE dicepool while I have few dice. Fortunately, my mage senses are more versatile (my mage IS more versatile), and thus it's not like I have nothing to do, but we really shine when we do not overlap. Also, we are both bad at combat and would benefit from someone who can tackle that part because supernatural creatures do not like to be investigated.<br /> <br /> You don't need to be the best at combat if you're the one that has the small army of ghouls, or the one that can disable the Tremere defenses. I could have a lot of fun as the cyber-shaman that's guiding a Garou pack trough the defenses of a high-tech Pentex facility, (and the ST would do well to introduce threats that would cut the comunication and risk my PC, eventually), sure, the PC it's in his room, but that's hardly an impediment with Corr 3, sure the PC can't easily handle a BSD, but that's not what he will handle (and in the case, fleeing it's feasible). Of course, the cyber-shaman it's usually a Glasswalker in a Werewolf game, but this is no usual Werewolf game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-23-2017, 04:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T13:36:09-07:00","id":1152280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152227#post1152227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Finally found the gift, It still requires a lot of Storyteller fiat to invoke. If no one is attacking a helpless member of your actual pack or your kinfolk its never going to come up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, because no one ever tries to attack the people around you or take hostages.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That makes it very limited in a crossover where many members of the PC group aren't fellow garou or kinfolk and the PC mages/vamps aren't interested in attacking a bunch of werewolves on the other side of the planet (the book even states that those gifts just aren't seen outside of the Boli Zousizhe offshoot that is found almost exclusively in China). </div> </div> </div>There was a major disapora of Chinese people in the 19th century, and they do communicate with other Garou, so it's not inconceivable that you would run into someone with that Gift just about anywhere there are Glass Walkers. Also, Warding Mokole have a version of it as an auspice Gift.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've noticed you like to cite a lot of edge cases </div> </div> </div>And you didn't read vast amounts of Werewolf material, yet continued on about it as if you knew everything.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> like were-dinosaurs and minor tribes with exclusive gifts </div> </div> </div>There are no strict tribe-exclusive Gifts among the Garou, particularly not when it comes to the Glass Walkers. They find a way around everything; that's why the Random Interrupts can learn a version of Chill of Early Frost. They call it Cooling System and learn it from different spirits (since Wendigo really doesn't like them), but Gifts tend to have general rules re-done or re-skinned quite a great deal throughout the line. If the writing directives bent over backwards to detail everything and give them more powers like, say, Vampire, you'd probably see a lot more examples.<br /> <br /> Also, since certain Fera got me into the game, I refuse to see them as edge cases.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> in proving how awesome werewolves are. Not every Storyteller is going to allow those.<br /> Our games stick to the things that the setting says are common to an area. Weredinosaurs from Africa and a tribe of werewolves whose domains are in China are not likely to turn up during a campaign set during a long dark Chicago winter (because we want the vampire players to not be quite so limited). </div> </div> </div>Mokole are, per the breed book and on into Revised and W20, worldwide, divided into four Streams that are vaguely tribe-ish. Also, again, diaspora with Garou tagging along.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its hard enough getting a normal city werewolf, some vamps and a few mages to cooperate in a way that doesn't stretch credibility. </div> </div> </div>Mages cooperating stretches credulity on its own. There is no &quot;normal&quot; with a story. There is no natural. There's only what you choose to construct.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yet Katanka-Sonnak only even shows up in the core book as part of the name of a gift that was used by the extinct Croatoan tribe. If its so common a totem why is that literally the only place it even appears in the core book? Like I said... edge cases. </div> </div> </div>Katanka-Sonnak is the Aetherial Incarna of the Sun. Just look up during daylight hours. Also, can we drop this ridiculous notion that anything not in the core is an edge case, especially for totems that have been around since the original Umbra: Velvet Shadow book? Because if that's the case, we could throw away vast amounts of Vampire and Mage, too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I kinda get the sense that you seem to think African weredinosaurs with a totem linked to an extinct North American Garou tribe hanging out with a Garou from a Chinese outsider tribe is the default way to play werewolf and they just go over to werespider's house for curds and whey periodically. </div> </div> </div>I get the sense that you don't know how people move around or cooperate. There was a comic about it in the beginning of W20 Changing Breeds. The participants note that they don't do it very often, but you're choosing to go into angst-bait territory on your own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As to personal totems, the only Storytellers willing to run a game with werewolf players are only familiar with the totems in the main book; none of which provide things like sun whips to their followers (which probably takes quite the investment in points... which are limited at chargen). </div> </div> </div>This section is borderline unintelligible. Sun whips are three dot fetishes. If you don't want to write a character as inheriting it, you can pick up rite of the fetish, so long as you have Rituals 3. These traits are had independently of Totem, personal or otherwise.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I just grabbed the one right out M20 core. They say to use these stats for &quot;<i>security specialists, mob enforcers, SWAT team members, assassins, gangbangers, military personnel, martial artists, and so on.</i>&quot; The stats are reasonably close to a Criminal Enforcer in V20 as well (i.e. <i>&quot;a reasonably tough, savvy member of the underworld.&quot;</i>). </div> </div> </div>Mage misrepresents a lot of things. That was pure old-school WW, where police officers were superhuman.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I guess Dead Man's Hand isn't all that elite if a &quot;reasonably tough, savvy member of the underworld&quot; can kick their ass. Or maybe something that toned down is all a werewolf can handle and still feel powerful. </div> </div> </div>Or WW is bad at statting things out. Given that they've given us superhuman beat cops and casual Dexterity 10 werecats in the past, and Mage plays fast and loose with everything, you can guess where it probably goes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The average soldier IS well above the norm in terms of physical ability. You have to run, jump and climb in 80+ pounds of gear, go days on end with 1-2 hours of sleep a night in the field and drill in a number of actions that hone your reflexes and hand-eye coordination.<br /> <br /> 3 dots in your physical abilities would be the minimum to not get washed out of the military (MINIMUM PFT requirements for males age 17-21 are 42 pushups in 2 minutes, 53 sit-us in 2 minutes and 2 miles in 15:54 seconds). 4 dots in Strength wouldn't be unusual... think about how much a man in full body armor weighs. Now imagine having to fireman carry him out of a hot zone because he's been wounded while still in your own gear. Does that sound closer to &quot;Lift 250 lb.&quot; (STR 3) or &quot;Lift 400 lb.&quot; (STR 4) to you? </div> </div> </div>If the requirements were actually incredibly strict, there would be far, far fewer potential soldiers. You need to realize how much the &quot;average&quot; person can actually do if pushed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2 dots is an everyday person on the street. Cops, Soldiers and those engaged in regular physical activity are going to be above average and they comprise less than 1% of the population (ex. 300+ million people in the United States... 1 million in law enforcement, 2 million in armed forces both active and reserve in the united states). </div> </div> </div>An awful lot of people in any organization are &quot;normal people off the street&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Here's the five dot description; </div> </div> </div>I have M20, thanks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mages can use their abilities at-will and only make items with abilities they already possess, so their need to make items isn't as important to them... it'd be more about letting an ally have that power when you're not there. </div> </div> </div>Spirits tend to open the door to powers that you don't have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Except it was a typical grunt.<br /> <br /> You just have the mistaken notion that a typical grunt can't lift more than hundred pounds and would need over 25 minutes to run 2 miles... which is what 2 dots in the physical stats translates to. That's not a typical grunt... that's a washout from basic training. </div> </div> </div>Mage is using slightly different rules, it seems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> DEX 3, STA 3 can just BARELY meet the 2 mile time minimum for basic training if you let them run at full speed for a full 2 minutes (20 turns) per point of Stamina and then jog the rest of the way. You need STR 3 just for a grown man to do a pull up (since they usually weigh more than 100 lb.). </div> </div> </div>Technically if you want to read the rules that strictly, humans in Werewolf can run for far longer because they don't have the arbitrary cap on running time that Mage does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Dead Man's Hand can barely meet the MINIMUM physical requirements to graduate basic training. If they're elite its because they've got 4 dots in some abilities, not because their attributes are superhuman compared to other soldiers. </div> </div> </div>Stop using a terrible system, or up their Stamina and Willpower. Also, since there's no proscription against it, you can have the results from extra effort lifting last for an extended period of time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And your unfamiliarity with Mage is telling. Remember, you were trying to say how awesome Create Elements was because it was equivalent to have six dots in spheres. Except those actual six dots in spheres for a mage aren't just that one little trick but the equivalent of a DOZEN+ more gifts as well. How many Ranks and Gifts would it take to get to even half that list of abilities a mage could pull off with their STARTING spheres (each costing 3 XP/level of the gift)? </div> </div> </div>They are a group of effects on par with two dots in each of three spheres, by a reading of the rules. I don't care if there are theoretically infinite rotes; they aren't equivalent to a one-dot power in Mage, because having one dot in each sphere and Arete 1 cannot get you what CE does.. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And yeah, it costs 4 freebie points to get to Arete 2... You make it sound like that's some sort of burden. Its not like I have to buy up my permanent rage (1 ea) or permanent gnosis (2 ea) just to make my abilities work more than a handful of times before I run out of gas. How many freebie points does the typical werewolf have to spend to get their rage, gnosis and willpower up to decent levels? </div> </div> </div>It is a burden. In any other system, those would be going to other Traits. Again, simple mathematics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its costs a mage 8 freebies to max out their starting Arete. Mages also start with +2 dots in backgrounds (7 instead of 5) and +1-2 dots in Willpower (5 instead 3-4) relative to the Fera so its more like they spend 4 freebies relative to a Fera to max it out. That's ALL THEY NEED to cast any effect their spheres will allow as often as they want to cast it (Willpower and Avatar can help, but aren't needed to use magic at-will). So for the cost of just 2 permanent Gnosis... the mage is maxed in his spellcasting. No further abilities to buy up past 3 dots, no extra Gnosis points to use them more than a few times... they're free to spend points on whatever they want to (it costs them 5 extra points to max their Willpower if they want and Avatar can be bought with background points).<br /> <br /> Hell, it would cost more just to get an Ahroun's Permanent Rage to 10 (5 freebies, not counting the WP you need to perform even the simplest social tasks) than the relative cost of getting Arete to 3 (8-4 extras is 4 points). It would cost a Lupus more to get to Permanent Gnosis 10 (10 freebies) than it does for Arete 3 when you're not using the relative costs... and if you're a Homid Ragabash Glasswalker? Forget about it... all 22 points from freebies and flaws won't max your Rage and Gnosis (not to mention Willpower)... you'd cap at 7 Gnosis (12 points), 7 Rage (6 points), 7 Willpower (4 points) and have nothing to spend on anything else. </div> </div> </div>And in the end, it would be more character-defining than those dots in Arete. What do those dots even mean?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So a mage doesn't have a right to use a node his own mentor created </div> </div> </div>Much like Werewolf rejects humanity having the inherent right to use whatever they find for their own, sole benefit, yes. The mage is using up something that isn't theirs. They aren't an inherent part of the cosmos, and they tear out its substance without regard for what remains afterward, so that they can reshape the cosmos to their own short-sighted whims.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and a humanity 10 vampire </div> </div> </div>A veritable unicorn, but whatever, let's roll with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> can't accept voluntary gifts from his herd (the Kiss is quite pleasurably and if the vamp is careful, no more dangerous than what I do for the Red Cross every 8 weeks)? </div> </div> </div>One wonders how voluntary it is with the Kiss doing what it does. My suspicions would be that the vampire's Humanity wouldn't be at 10 for very long.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thanks for reinforcing my impression of werewolves as self-entitled twink-munches who are going extinct because they're too stupid to live. </div> </div> </div>Societal pressures, dude. They can actually put up a very effective fight if they put their egos aside and work with the Fera. Not with any other monsters, mind you; Garou and Fera were all produced as parts of a whole, comprehensive body of shapechangers to get into the tiny nooks and crannies and solve problems so that the Incarnae don't cause shit to catch fire dealing with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They're furries with zero impulse control fighting Captain Planet villains. &quot;It smells BAD. MUST SMASH!!!&quot; </div> </div> </div>I do believe that Captain Planet is in Mage's camp. It has way, way more in common with its oddball magical effects disguised as scifi plots. At the hammiest, Werewolf is more like Inhumanoids, except with Garou et al. taking the place of the ridiculously hypermasculine scientist-weightlifters of Earth Corps.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My expertise is in Mage and general WoD crossovers. My only point was that Mages and Vamps aren't nearly as behind the curve as you seem to think and outside of you dragging in edge-case African were-dinosaurs with pack totems too obscure to make it into the main book and wielding gifts exclusive to a tribe that is centered in China, I think I've proven the case. </div> </div> </div>Again, if we're discounting what's in the main book, get ready to lose a vast amount of material from any other game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3 dots in Crafts then... big honking deal. You can still get it with your starting 13/9/5 allotment. Heck, you could get 3 dots in Crafts (automotive) and Technology (engineering) and Computers and Science (chemistry) and Academics, Alertness (to have a good ear for what the problem might be based on the sound the engine is making) and Athletics (just because they tend to be in decent shape) with your starting points and still have more left over. </div> </div> </div>And if you want to make someone extra-good at it... it's made more worth it to have Arete than that fourth or fifth dot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As discussed... your Arete is capped at 3 dots (8 freebies). </div> </div> </div>So you really only have 7 freebies to spend and a starting Arete of 3. That makes a budget calling for, say, Attributes quite tight, or any extra Spheres that their background story might call for. Set aside the calculations and optimization for a moment - does having extra spheres fit the concept or not?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Willpower starts at 5 and can be capped for 5 freebies. That still leaves you 2 extra freebies and 7 background points before you even start picking up flaws (which I find to be fun RP hooks and will often end up with 8-9 points of flaws that fit a theme even if I only get points for 7 of them).<br /> <br /> As I said... Attributes 7/5/3, Abilities 13/9/5 +4 to any, Backgrounds 8, Arete 3, Willpower 10, Spheres 6 and Flaws 7 is a 100% viable starting Mage characte </div> </div> </div>It's still a very degenerate system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You clearly have zero idea of what's actually involved in building a Mage character.<br /> <br /> You can only sink EIGHT freebie points into your Arete to start. That leaves you with 7 freebies + 7 backgrounds + any flaws on top of your basic attributes and abilities (which are not constrained by the ones needed to work gifts or disciplines) to further your concept while still having the maximum starting Arete. </div> </div> </div>Technically, the &quot;Abilities and Attributes&quot; that you need to make something work are Spheres, which are much more expensive than Attributes and Abilities.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You also don't seem to get that because there's no one true way to work magic in Mage your Arete and magic can always work in support of your concept. A Mage mechanic might would use his ability to repair things as part of his focus for casting spells. His tools and his dots in Crafts/Engineering let him MacGuyver himself up a solution to any problem he faces (Forces 3, Matter 3 would create any number of abilities the Mage MacGuyver could pull off). </div> </div> </div>And you have to split points between Spheres and other Traits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mages are holistic beings... they have no warring nature like a Vampire with its Beast or Werewolf and its Rage. Their magic supports and augments their mortal abilities (one of the reasons why one the larger chunks of the magic section is related &quot;magic enhancing skills&quot; (using magic to lower common task difficulties)&quot; and to &quot;skills enhancing magic (using practical knowledge to lower the difficulty in casting a spell).&quot; </div> </div> </div>Getting good at both involves having an auxilary set of Attributes that you have to buy, though. Gnosis has the same cost as an Ability, and Rage is half as expensive as that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's put this together shall we?<br /> <br /> ----------<br /> <b>Angus the Auto Mechanic</b><br /> <br /> He's smart, a bit above average physically, but only average socially. STR 3, DEX 3, STA 2 (secondary), CHA 2, MAN 2, APP 2 (tertiary), PER 3, INT 4, WIT 3 (primary).<br /> <br /> Talent-wise, he's alert and likes to work out, and likes to get a read on people before speaking (Teritary). Alertness 2, Athletics 3, Empathy 2. (2 points from freebies)<br /> <br /> His skills are primarily with his hands, he's good at fixing stuff (and &quot;fixing&quot; stuff), can swing a pipe wrench if he has to, but prefers to sneak around if he can (Primary), Craft 4 (automotive), Drive 1, Melee 2, Security 3, Stealth 1, Technology 4 (engineering; its a skill in mage rather than a knowledge). (2 points from freebies)<br /> <br /> He's got some book learning and he picked up first aid in the boy scouts, but its not his top priority (Secondary): Academics 3, Computers 1, Science 3 (chemistry), Medicine 2.<br /> <br /> For Backgrounds he's got autoshop that makes decent money (Resources 3), his co-workers have seen him do enough crazy stuff with tech that they can actually help him out (Cult 2) and he's got a decently strong avatar (Avatar 3) that appears to him in the form of plans and blueprints and scribbled notes and vectors (like you see on shows like Mythbusters when they're analyzing the data). Eight points total.<br /> <br /> His paradigm is that &quot;Tech Holds All the Answers&quot; and his main practice is &quot;Weird Science.&quot; He uses brews/concoctions, devices/machines, the elements, mathematics, inventions, vehicles and &quot;weapons&quot; (mostly booby traps) as his instruments. He's got Arete 3; Forces 3 and Matter 3 that uses to MacGuyver/Mythbuster his way through any sort of problems he might run into.<br /> <br /> He's also a strong-willed fella; Willpower 10. Being strong-willed though he's also got a bit of a short fuse (2 point flaw) and his curiosity, particularly about new tech (2 point flaw) can sometimes get the better of him. But he won't go too far because he's got a wife and two kids at home to take care of (3 point flaw). Seven points in flaws total. </div> </div> </div>I'm wondering how it is that Mage can justify having Short Fuse be worth that much when it doesn't blow up nearly as spectacularly as having a -2 difficulty to all Rage rolls does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Does that seem to have the concept sacrificed in any way in the name of having better magic to you? </div> </div> </div>The resources spent on Arete could have boosted Craft and Technology to 5, and Science to it as well. Here, you're going to end up paying for that with xp, and then you'll have to buy up more Spheres to do more things when an alternative system could have expanded basic, common functions of tools and practices and centralized training and talent into it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And it costs more, relatively speaking, than the maximum starting Arete does for a mage. You're also at potentially -7 dice to ALL social actions, even with other werewolves unless you brought your Willpower up quite a bit too. </div> </div> </div>It's the absolute cap, though. You'll never need to spend any xp on it, ever again, and in fact cannot. Arete and Spheres will continue to be an xp burden for some time to come, and you will be under a treadmill on costs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In fact, in a typical crossover game where there's more going on than just hitting people like some mindless misanthrope, I'd say unless your WP was at least 8 you'd be damned near unplayable as a character as you'd be nearly incapable of even communicating with others beyond the level of &quot;KILL YOU!!! RRRHAAAAAAHHHH!!!&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Or you could be Social Primary with Rage and have high Social Abilities. Which would not necessarily rule out also being a combat character, since you could still have other Abilities at notable professional levels. The quest for inner peace can be a tight, managable character arc in comparison to the nebulous, never-ending sandbox of seekings.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because despite its utility, not everyone buys Arete 3 because of their concept. Some people want to play an neophyte with just 1-2 Arete who has to get by on their wits and just a bit of odds adjustment. You only need one success to spot weaknesses in a system with Entropy 1 or see in the dark with Forces 1. </div> </div> </div>Those systems options probably would have been better served by having tweaked chargen processes for each level (much as Mokole would perhaps better be served with differing stat lines for sizes of Archid than with size being a Trait to buy, because holy shit, those mechanics do not scale well, so to speak). Exalted learned this and implemented it in its third edition. To do otherwise invites the degenerate state of the chargen game and punishes anyone who's just making a character according to concept. At least when you buy up Rage, it's legitimately a double-edged sword. Gnosis is a bit of a sticky thing, as is Willpower, but the Willpower cost game is common to all of the WoD games, and Gnosis is still cheaper than Arete.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, going from Arete 2 to 3 is only 16 XP... about the same as going from rank 3-5 in an ability (14 XP) and a Seeking to raise their Arete score can require the Storyteller to put more focus on that character than they normally would in a session. If you start with Arete 1 then its only 8 XP and a Seeking to get to Arete 2. If your goal isn't &quot;Best-est character ever&quot; but having your Storyteller put the focus on your character, starting with an Arete 1-2 isn't a bad way to build up momentum in that respect. </div> </div> </div>Then you have to buy up the spheres. Which are more expensive when bought from 1 and up with xp than from 3 and up, even if you have many fewer ones at 3.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You might be thinking of Awakening because the message of Mage is &quot;You have the power to change the world. What do you do with it?&quot;<br /> <br /> There was some stuff in the 2e Masters of the Art book that pointed out pursing the Arch-spheres (rank 6+ in a sphere) closed off the path to Ascension because you were putting too much focus in one tiny part of reality instead of trying to understand reality as a whole, but high Arete is generally a measure of your path to Ascension as the increased understanding that comes with it allows you to cast aside the tools you once relied on because you now understand they were just props and not the true source of your magic. </div> </div> </div>No, I'm fairly certain that Satyr had a long speech in a podcast about Ascension being the realization that power isn't everything/is an illusion. I don't pay enough attention to CoD or Awakening to internalize any of those, so it definitely isn't a case of mistaken identity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ultimately, the core conflict of Mage is &quot;What type of world do you want to leave behind?&quot; The Nephandi want to tear the world down. The Technocracy say Safety is worth the loss of Freedom and Wonder. The Traditions say &quot;Freedom and Wonder are worth the risks&quot; and the Marauders say &quot;I am the Walrus! Coo coo ca choo!&quot; </div> </div> </div>No, again, given that podcast, the Marauders demand to know where some facet of their imagined reality is, with the threat of terrible violence falling on anyone they push and who cannot comply. They aren't too terrible different from most mages in this regard. Maybe lacking in subtlety, but the bludgeoning of others in their way is definitely on point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is there a reason you're screaming this? </div> </div> </div>Caps cannot always be taken for screaming text. Loud, perhaps, but not screaming. And it's because you're not crunching the numbers particularly well. Gifts + two or three extra Abilities is way, way cheaper than Arete plus Spheres. With the amount of xp that you spend to increase Arete from 1 to 3 alone, you can increase a Homid's neglected Gnosis rating (doubtless from having pursued other Traits that made sense for the concept) from 1 to 5. Add in boosting up your six level one Spheres to three dots each, and, assuming that one is an affinity Sphere, you get a whopping 141 xp, a veritable waste of riches. Just raising the affinity sphere and one other is 45 xp, which can get you from Gnosis 5 to 8, with about 10 xp left over from that exchange, which you can pump into Primal-Urge to increase it from 1 to 3.<br /> <br /> Sure, you might have a lot of things you can do with six Spheres at three dots each, but they only do this at the cost of rules that enable other parts of the game, had with the base Attribute + Ability system. You could do a lot with the amount of word count dedicated to Spheres and how they're supposed to work and still leave a rather open-ended base system interacting with technology and mystic practices based on oddball Abilities. But no, Sphere system, Arete, Special K. And it's expensive as hell to try to support both that and an overshadowed basic system. You basically have two different character sheets instead of one with some extra stuff on it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Did you score four+ successes on a rage check? </div> </div> </div>Then it never would have been posted, silly. Besides, humans can't feel enough hatred to register on the Rage scale. Even my visceral dislike of Vampire and Mage can't get me anywhere near there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T13:59:57-07:00","id":1152292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152221#post1152221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> \u200bMy favorite team it's <u>mages</u>...but when I see an Akashic that's a <b>wealthy</b> martial artist<u> with military equipment </u>and both <i>mystic firearm skill</i> and <i>movements</i> as Instrument that always wears his armor...<br /> <br /> Vs a plain werewolf...<br /> <br /> I have to protest.<br /> <br /> Those aren't PC, one it's a clear combat munchkin and the other it's so conservative that one can hardly talk about a combat specialist-may as well be a sports specialist that's good at pugilism. No one would think that's a fair comparison. At least expend the same points and think about tactics for both of them</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually the character was inspired by Batman, Arrow and The Shadow... particularly the latter since they know the secrets of hiding from the sight of men and have a high rating in Spies (used to gather info, spread rumors, sway opinions and pick up on threats before they emerge).<br /> <br /> In addition to some combat skills, the build also has ranks in Academics, Awareness, Etiquette, Investigate, Leadership and even a dot in Pilot since the underlying concept is rich socialite turned vigilante hunter of monsters. They also have a 2 dot ally Virtual Adept who acts Overwatch/Oracle because every good avenger of the night needs one of those (and someone to be a last minute date to various social functions because they're too busy fighting the forces of darkness to have much of a dating life).<br /> <br /> They aren't using military equipment either. Its reinforced clothing and a 9mm semi-automatic semi-automatic pistol. They've just been tricked out to LOOK super hi-tech (mostly by the VA ally. While it is real equipment in that sense, its mostly just really good cosplay so their magic is concealed from the masses (the armored suit didn't actually stop the bullet... that was the &quot;Iron Skin Body Technique,&quot; but the masses will believe the suit must be a lot stronger than it really it is more readily than &quot;magic&quot; made their skin as hard as steel).<br /> <br /> Nor do they have a mystic firearms skill. They have dots in firearms and martial arts/Do and magic that uses the practice of martial arts to augment any attack they make (their kick can do as much as the gun via magic, but a gun has range that a punch or kick lacks and is more coincidental when it does lethal or aggravated damage).<br /> <br /> They can also channel their Chi to heal people, remove diseases and negate toxins. They can see in the dark, hear beyond the range of human hearing, read auras, turn invisible, fly (though they do it more wire-fu style because paradigm matters), perform telekinetic feats (more like Chi-bending), throw things really hard, manipulate fire and electricity and alter their appearance.<br /> <br /> They don't always wear the armor either. They wear what ever's fashionable during those lovely fundraising gallas and corporate dinners, workout clothes when practicing in the gym and swimsuit at the pool. But they do always wear armor when they're expressly going out to hunt down things that prey on people. To do otherwise would be like a soldier going into a known warzone without his armor or weapon.<br /> <br /> In other words, they have a lot of fun and interesting options available to them above and beyond combat... they just also happen to be pretty good at combat as well. If all I cared about was combat munchkin-ism I'd have done Life 3, Time 3 so they'd not only be able to Pump their stats up to stupid levels, but also give themselves extra actions that don't even require rage or blood points to maintain and forego most of the wealth/spy network to cap out relevant combat abilities at 5 dots.<br /> <br /> The true combat munchkin would be ex-military player whose playing a Brujah with Celerity 3 who's an ex-military sniper with certification 5, resources 5, military force 5 and retainers 5 all wrapped up in a Blackwater-like private military contractor business (the 100 soldiers are his front line people, the herd is his office staff, the retainers are his ghoul elite forces w. potence 1 and celerity 1). His preferred method of combat is to blow your head off with a .50 cal sniper rifle from 1000 yards away before you even know he's there. Option two is go in with 10-20+ battle hardened vets in full military gear. He absolutely will not engage unless he's actually got what he feels is sufficient force to make it a curb stomp in his favor (&quot;Fair fights are for losers&quot; is his motto).<br /> <br /> They and the other combat focused vampire (also with Resources 5 and martial arts) are a big part of what pushed my character more in the Batman direction (other than knowing I'd be playing a mage, I didn't even start building my character until everyone else had already shared their concepts. Apparently for this particular crossover we ended up with a &quot;Supernatural Spycraft&quot; type theme.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T14:59:10-07:00","id":1152306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The resources spent on Arete could have boosted Craft and Technology to 5, and Science to it as well. Here, you're going to end up paying for that with xp, and then you'll have to buy up more Spheres to do more things when an alternative system could have expanded basic, common functions of tools and practices and centralized training and talent into it. </div> </div> </div>Just one brief bit because its clear we're on two separate planets here and there's not really point in continuing the discussion when your view and mine are so far apart, but this one I think sums up everything in a nutshell...<br /> <br /> Why would an AUTO MECHANIC have a PHD in Engineering and Chemistry (which is what 5 dots represents)?<br /> <br /> 3-4 dots accurately covers a typical auto mechanic's level of ability.<br /> <br /> Your definition of building to concept seems to be &quot;put as many points into a trait as possible even if that doesn't actually make sense for the character.&quot;<br /> <br /> That's not role-playing to me. Role-playing to me also includes saying, &quot;my guy ISN'T supposed to be a rocket scientist... he's an auto mechanic so 4 dots in that skill accurately represents the concept I'm going for and I shouldn't really be looking to improve it too much without a good reason for it in game.&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't give teenagers 3 dots in any ability (1-2 max) because it doesn't fit the concept. Characters with 5 dots in ANY ability are among the best in the world in that field so you should think long and hard about giving any character a 5... particularly at the START of their career.<br /> <br /> In the case of Angus the Auto Mechanic... 4 dots and a focus on magic after that is fine because he's reached the end of where conventional science can take him. His Arete is a measure of his divine inspiration... his ability to reach above what he's learned and create what science says shouldn't be possible. Devoting himself to a PHD in technology or chemistry (5 dots) doesn't let you make a car that can fly... opening up to his inner genius (i.e. improving his Arete) and learning the principles of forces and matter (improving those spheres) will let flying cars be just the start of the wonders he could build in his garage; particularly once the Sons of Ether get in touch with him (if they haven't already).<br /> <br /> I'll take that... or the Virtual Adept DJ who influences people through music or the Perky Goth (&quot;Putting the Romance back in Nec-romance-y&quot;) Hollow One who works at the morgue and talks to ghosts to solve murder mysteries or the Celestial Choruser trying to restore an abandoned church in the bad part of town to bring hope back to the community or any of dozens of others in the Mage games I've run over the years... any day of the week over Abominations and Were-dinosaurs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T15:20:48-07:00","id":1152313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152250#post1152250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually, no. The mokole won, abomination was a close second though with get of fenris as third. Everything else was pwned.<br /> <br /> See here: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/837527-deadliest-supernatural-warrior-results-and-analysis\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...s-and-analysis</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks! Actually, it seems pretty even. The melee powerhouses are the best in a white-room combat, no big surprise, but the big picture is quite okay, IMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T19:23:22-07:00","id":1152365,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152306#post1152306\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just one brief bit because its clear we're on two separate planets here and there's not really point in continuing the discussion when your view and mine are so far apart, but this one I think sums up everything in a nutshell...<br /> <br /> Why would an AUTO MECHANIC have a PHD in Engineering and Chemistry (which is what 5 dots represents)?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because of a level of skill and intellectual curiosity that is above and beyond the norm? Is that not the concept of genius/avatar? That they can know how to revolutionize the world even without Special K?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3-4 dots accurately covers a typical auto mechanic's level of ability. </div> </div> </div>Technically, two dots tends to represent solid professional expertise. Exceeding that makes you notably better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your definition of building to concept seems to be &quot;put as many points into a trait as possible even if that doesn't actually make sense for the character.&quot; </div> </div> </div>No, I had an expectation that the character in question would be an undiscovered genius. Again, is this not what Mage tends toward?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's not role-playing to me. Role-playing to me also includes saying, &quot;my guy ISN'T supposed to be a rocket scientist... he's an auto mechanic so 4 dots in that skill accurately represents the concept I'm going for and I shouldn't really be looking to improve it too much without a good reason for it in game.&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't give teenagers 3 dots in any ability (1-2 max) because it doesn't fit the concept. Characters with 5 dots in ANY ability are among the best in the world in that field so you should think long and hard about giving any character a 5... particularly at the START of their career. </div> </div> </div>Whoever said that it was the start of their career?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the case of Angus the Auto Mechanic... 4 dots and a focus on magic after that is fine because he's reached the end of where conventional science can take him. His Arete is a measure of his divine inspiration... his ability to reach above what he's learned and create what science says shouldn't be possible. Devoting himself to a PHD in technology or chemistry (5 dots) doesn't let you make a car that can fly... opening up to his inner genius (i.e. improving his Arete) and learning the principles of forces and matter (improving those spheres) will let flying cars be just the start of the wonders he could build in his garage; particularly once the Sons of Ether get in touch with him (if they haven't already). </div> </div> </div>Well, actually, the device in question would still need to be built to certain specs. M20 threw a lot of this into the Hypertech Ability, but I'm not fond of the million Secondary Abilities model.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'll take that... or the Virtual Adept DJ who influences people through music or the Perky Goth (&quot;Putting the Romance back in Nec-romance-y&quot;) Hollow One who works at the morgue and talks to ghosts to solve murder mysteries or the Celestial Choruser trying to restore an abandoned church in the bad part of town to bring hope back to the community or any of dozens of others in the Mage games I've run over the years... any day of the week over Abominations and Were-dinosaurs. </div> </div> </div>I guess the only thing there is that neither one of us cares for Abominations.<br /> <br /> (Mokole are chimeric reptilian monsters representing the dream of grandeur that their relations had in prehistoric eras. Dinosaurs are but one part of it; in the strictest sense, Corax are the only weredinosaurs in W:tA.)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-23T19:24:09-07:00","id":1152366,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152313#post1152313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks! Actually, it seems pretty even. The melee powerhouses are the best in a white-room combat, no big surprise, but the big picture is quite okay, IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'd need to have an awfully large room to bring in some Mokole...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T09:10:33-07:00","id":1152531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152292#post1152292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually the character was inspired by Batman, Arrow and The Shadow... particularly the latter since they know the secrets of hiding from the sight of men and have a high rating in Spies (used to gather info, spread rumors, sway opinions and pick up on threats before they emerge).<br /> <br /> In addition to some combat skills, the build also has ranks in Academics, Awareness, Etiquette, Investigate, Leadership and even a dot in Pilot since the underlying concept is rich socialite turned vigilante hunter of monsters. They also have a 2 dot ally Virtual Adept who acts Overwatch/Oracle because every good avenger of the night needs one of those (and someone to be a last minute date to various social functions because they're too busy fighting the forces of darkness to have much of a dating life).</div> </div> </div> </div>Why I'm so not surprised?...anyhow this exemplifies the problem I was trying to show you from the beguining, and that you don't see. Your &quot;mages&quot; are perfect superheros, while the &quot;combat specialist&quot; Garou was just a Physical Primary / Brawl master. Do you really not see the unbalance between Batman and a wrestler?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They aren't using military equipment either. Its reinforced clothing and a 9mm semi-automatic semi-automatic pistol. They've just been tricked out to LOOK super hi-tech (mostly by the VA ally. While it is real equipment in that sense, its mostly just really good cosplay so their magic is concealed from the masses (the armored suit didn't actually stop the bullet... that was the &quot;Iron Skin Body Technique,&quot; but the masses will believe the suit must be a lot stronger than it really it is more readily than &quot;magic&quot; made their skin as hard as steel). </div> </div> </div>So they have a Wonder or a Ritual going on. Either more dots of difference between this character and your garou or prep-time. In other words, effort, which was the whole point of you having to go over your head to be there and be useful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nor do they have a mystic firearms skill. They have dots in firearms and martial arts/Do and magic that uses the practice of martial arts to augment any attack they make (their kick can do as much as the gun via magic, but a gun has range that a punch or kick lacks and is more coincidental when it does lethal or aggravated damage). </div> </div> </div>If you use martial arts to &quot;argument any attack they make&quot;, somehow including guns, which implies you're using some sort of bizarre &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_fu\" target=\"_blank\">Gun fu</a>&quot; , with magic, then you have mystic firearms skill: You have firearms, you use magick trough them, you're a mystic because you use mystical Do, <i>how it's that you don't have a mystic firearms skill!</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They can also channel their Chi to heal people, remove diseases and negate toxins. They can see in the dark, hear beyond the range of human hearing, read auras, turn invisible, fly (though they do it more wire-fu style because paradigm matters), perform telekinetic feats (more like Chi-bending), throw things really hard, manipulate fire and electricity and alter their appearance. </div> </div> </div>So, outlandish feats that Batman would need a gadget to perform, you can do with just saying Chi. Oh, the advantages of having a Mystic Paradigm! . And you don't even need to reject technology, as guns are part of your Instruments!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They don't always wear the armor either. They wear what ever's fashionable during those lovely fundraising gallas and corporate dinners, workout clothes when practicing in the gym and swimsuit at the pool. But they do always wear armor when they're expressly going out to hunt down things that prey on people. To do otherwise would be like a soldier going into a known warzone without his armor or weapon.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">In other words, they have a lot of fun and interesting options available to them above and beyond combat... they just also happen to be pretty good at combat as well. If all I cared about was combat munchkin-ism I'd have done Life 3, Time 3 so they'd not only be able to Pump their stats up to stupid levels, but also give themselves extra actions that don't even require rage or blood points to maintain and forego most of the wealth/spy network to cap out relevant combat abilities at 5 dots.<br /> <br /> The true combat munchkin would be ex-military player whose playing a Brujah with Celerity 3 who's an ex-military sniper with certification 5, resources 5, military force 5 and retainers 5 all wrapped up in a Blackwater-like private military contractor business (the 100 soldiers are his front line people, the herd is his office staff, the retainers are his ghoul elite forces w. potence 1 and celerity 1). His preferred method of combat is to blow your head off with a .50 cal sniper rifle from 1000 yards away before you even know he's there. Option two is go in with 10-20+ battle hardened vets in full military gear. He absolutely will not engage unless he's actually got what he feels is sufficient force to make it a curb stomp in his favor (&quot;Fair fights are for losers&quot; is his motto).<br /> <br /> They and the other combat focused vampire (also with Resources 5 and martial arts) are a big part of what pushed my character more in the Batman direction (other than knowing I'd be playing a mage, I didn't even start building my character until everyone else had already shared their concepts. Apparently for this particular crossover we ended up with a &quot;Supernatural Spycraft&quot; type theme.</span></span> </div> </div> </div>Time 3, Life 3, per se, isn't enough to say a character it's min/max, there's various factors, like maxed combat skills as you stated. So does your &quot;inventory&quot; (when everyone has military grade gear regardless of theme). And the Instruments and Practices also have a say. Are you avoiding anything that could be even remotely cumbersome or restrictive?, are your Instuments omnicapable or you use them when it's thematic?.<br /> <br /> And, yes, these vampire characters are more exaggerated than yours (but not by a long shot). It seems that everyone has Resources 4-5 in your city. I would be surprised if the local Garou wasn't owning a massive minigun, a lvl 5 fetish weapon and a body armor crafted to use in Crinos by his successful military company. All the while having a personal Totem and Ancestors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-24-2017, 12:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T13:32:25-07:00","id":1152597,"title":"","content":"The Deadliest Warrior thread aside I'd be almost inclined to run a &quot;Make your starting character, they meet in an alleyway and things go sideways. We begin just before initiative rolls...&quot; and run the combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T13:34:07-07:00","id":1152598,"title":"","content":"Possibly with a &quot;you know you're Probably going to be facing a supernatural, but you only discovered that an hour ago&quot; aspect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-24T17:08:30-07:00","id":1152640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152598#post1152598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Possibly with a &quot;you know you're Probably going to be facing a supernatural, but you only discovered that an hour ago&quot; aspect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, Double-Blind Combat. When a Timber Wolf/Mad Cat ends up face to face with a Hunchback just right...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-25T08:24:02-07:00","id":1152810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Deadliest Warrior thread aside I'd be almost inclined to run a &quot;Make your starting character, they meet in an alleyway and things go sideways. We begin just before initiative rolls...&quot; and run the combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>How sideways?, Isn't fleeing an option?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152598#post1152598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Possibly with a &quot;you know you're Probably going to be facing a supernatural, but you only discovered that an hour ago&quot; aspect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, It's that an hour worth of Rituals (enough to get 10 successes in one casting, or a lot of smaller 5 successes in spells for a Mage) or it's part of that spended going to the alley?<br /> <br /> Can I go there immediately to lay traps?<br /> <br /> What impedes me from bringing my personal mercenary army (or equivalent)?<br /> <br /> Can't I call my whole pack/coterie/cabal?. Because I would if I know I was going to met someone potentially dangerous<br /> <br /> Or it's a white room of fight with prep time and no resources or strategy allowed.<br /> Mind you, 1 hour of prep it's enough for any ritual using splat to cast at least one ritual or equivalent. Thus providing even more variables.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-25T11:50:33-07:00","id":1152855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1152810#post1152810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> How sideways?, Isn't fleeing an option?</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe that the context in that situation would make it highly dependent on how quickly you can run, or if there's a sufficiently fast vehicle nearby. It would probably still be more difficult for some characters than others; I made the post up there that I did because it reminded me of Double-Blind miniatures combat, where movement and position are only known completely to a third party. So, you keep walking forward, until you run into something hostile. It could be a scout unit; it could be a brawler that's extremely dangerous in close quarters. Only the moderator knows.<br /> <br /> Edit: And if you did have a private army with you, you'd need to spend an awful lot of time determining their positions and formation. They might not all be able to get to you, or have a clear shot at whatever enemies you encounter, if the quarters are cramped, hemmed-in, and offering a lot of places to take cover or hide.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 10-25-2017, 02:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-25T13:55:19-07:00","id":1152925,"title":"","content":"Essentially Saur Ops (Although the HBK is going to take a beating no matter how nasty a surprise that AC 20 or Ultra AC 20 is at knife fight ranges). Yep. Proper ST moderated PvP. <br /> <br /> You have an hour to get to where your going. You are aware there will be a supernatural threat there. Each character is say 2-3 miles from the EZ.<br /> <br /> And characters would have to be characters that would make sense starting out in a &quot;generic&quot; chronicle of their relevant gameline.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2767,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","user":"Moirdryd","date":". 10-25-2017, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-31T08:05:22-07:00","id":1154375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1151629#post1151629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The average soldier IS well above the norm in terms of physical ability. You have to run, jump and climb in 80+ pounds of gear, go days on end with 1-2 hours of sleep a night in the field and drill in a number of actions that hone your reflexes and hand-eye coordination.<br /> <br /> 3 dots in your physical abilities would be the minimum to not get washed out of the military (MINIMUM PFT requirements for males age 17-21 are 42 pushups in 2 minutes, 53 sit-us in 2 minutes and 2 miles in 15:54 seconds). 4 dots in Strength wouldn't be unusual... think about how much a man in full body armor weighs. Now imagine having to fireman carry him out of a hot zone because he's been wounded while still in your own gear. Does that sound closer to &quot;Lift 250 lb.&quot; (STR 3) or &quot;Lift 400 lb.&quot; (STR 4) to you?</div> </div> </div> </div>I just want to address that American soldiers carry around about 60 lbs of gear (Google), and probably weigh around 160-170lbs on average. Their lifestyle and diet makes for a lean physique. The description of a soldier carrying his ally plus both of their gear fits within what's reasonably possible with strength 3. The stats in the book are probably over shooting it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-31T10:04:20-07:00","id":1154425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PazuzuAxelf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154375#post1154375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I just want to address that American soldiers carry around about 60 lbs of gear (Google), and probably weigh around 160-170lbs on average. Their lifestyle and diet makes for a lean physique. The description of a soldier carrying his ally plus both of their gear fits within what's reasonably possible with strength 3. The stats in the book are probably over shooting it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on if it means &quot;Lift&quot; as in lift and carry or like doing a single rep on a bench. Soldiers have to do the former reliably to go into the field. Though honestly having to lift a 160-170lb man and both their sets of 60 lb gear reliably out of an active combat situation with haste definitely sounds like strength 4 to me no matter how you slice it. Even if Strength 3 is &quot;lift and carry&quot; 250 lb's, being able to pick up 280 lb and be comfortable enough with it to move it without being to hindered to move swiftly sounds like a bit of a stretch.<br /> <br /> Not to mention those are just normal front line infantry(at least in the US), more elite military divisions like rangers, seals, and paratroopers Will be at top physical condition. To the point of having 2-3 physical stats at 5 and several abilities depending on role as well. I'd also put them at the higher end of willpower 5-7 for standard troops and 8-10 for elite, us military members have to go through sere training on how to resist confinement and interogation of enemy forces, spend long periods of time without seeing friends or family on deployments, and with the afore-mentioned sleep deprevation. Better trained military members Are unshakeable in every sense of the word.<br /> <br /> Average joe-wolf isn't just going to waltz in and take on at team of army rangers unless he plans on walking out missing a limb or two.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-31T13:07:10-07:00","id":1154485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154425#post1154425\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Depends on if it means &quot;Lift&quot; as in lift and carry or like doing a single rep on a bench. Soldiers have to do the former reliably to go into the field. Though honestly having to lift a 160-170lb man and both their sets of 60 lb gear reliably out of an active combat situation with haste definitely sounds like strength 4 to me no matter how you slice it. Even if Strength 3 is &quot;lift and carry&quot; 250 lb's, being able to pick up 280 lb and be comfortable enough with it to move it without being to hindered to move swiftly sounds like a bit of a stretch.<br /> <br /> Not to mention those are just normal front line infantry(at least in the US), more elite military divisions like rangers, seals, and paratroopers Will be at top physical condition. To the point of having 2-3 physical stats at 5 and several abilities depending on role as well. I'd also put them at the higher end of willpower 5-7 for standard troops and 8-10 for elite, us military members have to go through sere training on how to resist confinement and interogation of enemy forces, spend long periods of time without seeing friends or family on deployments, and with the afore-mentioned sleep deprevation. Better trained military members Are unshakeable in every sense of the word.<br /> <br /> Average joe-wolf isn't just going to waltz in and take on at team of army rangers unless he plans on walking out missing a limb or two.</div> </div> </div> </div>The ST system is not meant to be a physics engine. Attempting to make it into one gave us Exalted 2nd edition, i.e. a buggy mistake that falls apart whenever pressure falls on it. Particularly with the feats of strength, it's generally better to go by a textual description than a listen maximum weight, because the core of the system is &quot;roll if something is uncertain, using an Attribute + Ability&quot;, not &quot;you can lift up this exact quantity of coinage&quot;, the latter of which is only really important if you're looting a place and gaining xp for each gold piece you can get back to town. If you aren't playing a character advancement game that revolves around treasure, exact weights are not particularly important to the game's typical operations, and you can eyeball it.<br /> <br /> Auxiliary Note #1: W20 explicitly allows for a variance of Willpower rolls to lift more weight, as well as to potentially carry it for as long as a given feat takes to achieve.<br /> <br /> Auxiliary Note #2: The lifting table seems to assume a military press, since it allows you to throw something, and it's generally much more difficult to do this than to, say, carry around a load of weight on a frame specifically designed to carry gear, like a rugged backpack with a solid framework, or, presumably, most tactical apparel. Also, different muscle groups are involved in these tasks, so a character might get away with a lower Attribute rating than they would need if they were lifting it over their head.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-31T17:20:44-07:00","id":1154554,"title":"","content":"It's worth noting that the Criminal Enforcer stats as listed from V20 by a previous poster are stated as being the reasonably tough and savvy member of the underworld and that regular thugs ect are much less threatening in their stats. So the Criminal Enforcer is like a 'named' thug from an action movie, not the standard guy and yeah, I could easily see Spec Forces having similar stats and they'll still die to an enraged Werewolf.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-31T22:28:46-07:00","id":1154624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154554#post1154554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's worth noting that the Criminal Enforcer stats as listed from V20 by a previous poster are stated as being the reasonably tough and savvy member of the underworld and that regular thugs ect are much less threatening in their stats. So the Criminal Enforcer is like a 'named' thug from an action movie, not the standard guy and yeah, I could easily see Spec Forces having similar stats and they'll still die to an enraged Werewolf.</div> </div> </div> </div>Would have to disagree with a &quot;named&quot; thug being on the same level as a spec ops personel. Said thug could be very knowledgable of the underworld and have survived several life or death scenarios, possibly even every day. I'd imagine someone like that would have higher perception, wits, dex, survival, medicine, alertness, althetics, firearms and streetwise. I'd think standard infantry would have similar stats<br /> <br /> On the other hand we have a special operation personel, who's been trained for years to be at peak physical condition, taut how to almost any combat scenario, trained in the practical application of subjects from medicine to crafts, and is mentally hardened against the best devised interrogation methods. They'd have stamina 5, and likely one or both of their other physical stats at 5, wits and perception of at least 4, they'd have drive, alertness, firearms, survival, athletics, and technology at the 3-4 range, and medicine of 2(or greater if a field medic or doctor).<br /> <br /> Surviving the underworld, while impressive, is no where near the level of formal training. the average thug is no where close to as combat effective as the average infantryman and a hardened thug that's been around the block is still only a step or two above the average and more than a little luck. The spec ops of any branch of service is almost magnitudes above an infantryman, they have the worlds the best training broke down into a science, they have some of the best combat gear money can buy, they're battle harden as part of their job and their dayly lifestyle revolves around the cycle of work-train-action-work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6383,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","user":"Prometheas","date":". 11-01-2017, 01:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-01T06:42:33-07:00","id":1154691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154624#post1154624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Surviving the underworld, while impressive, is no where near the level of formal training. the average thug is no where close to as combat effective as the average infantryman and a hardened thug that's been around the block is still only a step or two above the average and more than a little luck. The spec ops of any branch of service is almost magnitudes above an infantryman, they have the worlds the best training broke down into a science, they have some of the best combat gear money can buy, they're battle harden as part of their job and their dayly lifestyle revolves around the cycle of work-train-action-work.</div> </div> </div> </div>To put it another way, there's a reason almost every story every involving some elite criminal/assassin/hitman throws in a line about how they're at least ex-military or, more often, ex-special forces. Everyone understands the extreme level of competence possessed by the special forces (with Spetsnaz and Mossad being noted for their competence even beyond what the US Special Forces are capable of) so it feels plausible that they could go up against a dozen normal underworld thugs at once and defeat them easily.<br /> <br /> Or to put it another way... The Armed Forces represent the upper 1% of combat ability in the world (less than 1 in 100 people are actually members of any armed forces). Calling them an &quot;average&quot; human is like calling an F-15 an &quot;average&quot; plane.<br /> <br /> Likewise, the special forces are the upper 1% of that upper 1% (to just stay in the US military you need to score 180+ on their regular physical fitness tests... US spec forces need a 229+ score and to be able to swim 50 meters in boots and BDUs just to QUALIFY for the special forces TRAINING program). If the average human is a Cessna Skyhawk or Piper Cherokee and the typical soldier is an F-15, they're the SR-71s.<br /> <br /> And that's just your average special forces members. There are elite units within the special forces that are a step up from even those guys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-01T07:26:39-07:00","id":1154702,"title":"","content":"Absolutely they are above average to the point of being Exceptional. Exceptional is a 4 and for most of them the Delirium is still going to be a problem #1. Four highly trained Best of the Best of the Best style guys (so an A-Team one might say) with 5s all over the place of with automatic weapons getting the drop on an 'average' recently changed Garou Cliath are going to be a threat to that Garou yes. In fact that's why Pentex First teams are considered a problem because they are stats Atts 3-4 with abilities 3-5 carrying the very tools to make them a threat. <br /> <br /> Although it's worth noting that things like a flack jacket are Armour 4, Dex -2 NOT Armour 5 Dex -3 which is full riot gear. The defensive options in combat for Werewolf are also different to Mage (and I think Vampire, need to check) as there is no way to go 'full defensive' so that also twists the matrix a little. However armour also doesn't guarantee a soak roll vs Agg in WTA combat system either unless it's talen or fetish armour (or otherwise specifically constructed) much like a Bullet Proof Vest is really handy vs a Gun but less so vs a Knife, it comes down to the ST call. Also W20 doesn't have some of the combat options that appeared in 2nd ed or revised when it comes to called shots (oh it has called shots +2 diff effects agreed with ST) where 4HLs passed Soak to a limb was limb removed, aka instant death if the head was targeted and you were mortal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-01T18:08:39-07:00","id":1154910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154691#post1154691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To put it another way, there's a reason almost every story every involving some elite criminal/assassin/hitman throws in a line about how they're at least ex-military or, more often, ex-special forces. Everyone understands the extreme level of competence possessed by the special forces (with Spetsnaz and Mossad being noted for their competence even beyond what the US Special Forces are capable of) so it feels plausible that they could go up against a dozen normal underworld thugs at once and defeat them easily.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, also the wank factor. Especially if they're presented as being even more powerful than other special forces operatives, which such characters typically are, being straight-up power fantasies of human capability.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or to put it another way... The Armed Forces represent the upper 1% of combat ability in the world (less than 1 in 100 people are actually members of any armed forces). Calling them an &quot;average&quot; human is like calling an F-15 an &quot;average&quot; plane. </div> </div> </div>That statement is just... mind-boggling, honestly. Humans aren't really comparable to machines coming in a wide array of designs and role purposes, using wildly different power sources. Even training doesn't really make sense as an analogy, since it typically doesn't make you guzzle the nutritional equivalent of jet fuel at every sortie and result in confinement to the hangar for all personnel with these capabilities.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Likewise, the special forces are the upper 1% of that upper 1% (to just stay in the US military you need to score 180+ on their regular physical fitness tests... US spec forces need a 229+ score and to be able to swim 50 meters in boots and BDUs just to QUALIFY for the special forces TRAINING program). If the average human is a Cessna Skyhawk or Piper Cherokee and the typical soldier is an F-15, they're the SR-71s.<br /> <br /> And that's just your average special forces members. There are elite units within the special forces that are a step up from even those guys. </div> </div> </div>But not for very long, if they do manage to push that far. Degradation of performance starts to cut into stats well before middle age sets in, and rotations accordingly start dropping personnel at around age 30, at which point those 5s turn into 4s, and 4s turn into 3s. Garou and Fera don't even start to experience that until they are incredibly old, though, and tend to have more incidences of higher Traits simply because they're monsters with potential beyond most human limits. For those that experience negative effects of age at all, anyway.<br /> <br /> Also, as an interesting comparison between systems and assumed character statistics, GURPS Special Ops 2nd edition templates have exactly one special operations group that has a minimum ST higher than 11, and that would be the SEALs, at ST 12 (SEALS also have the highest default point total at 200 points, or twice what's suggested for most adventurous types; generally, a template will tie up nearly all of a character's points). Neither ST 11 nor ST 12 under the GURPS 3e system assumptions would really get you even to the equivalent of ST system Strength 3, so being a bruiser in that fashion is by no means assumed. Most of the higher point total templates tend to focus on having much higher Dexterity and Intelligence than Strength or Health, and all of them have the Fit advantage, which allows for faster recovery of fatigue points, presumably because most of these people aren't doing these amazing things without exerting themselves in the process. Character point totals are also telling, as the official GURPS conversion of W:tA generally places Garou as having several hundred character points (as in, about 500 or more) over even elite special operatives, even if you add on higher Attributes and skills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-01T20:53:29-07:00","id":1154972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Moirdryd</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1154702#post1154702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Absolutely they are above average to the point of being Exceptional. Exceptional is a 4 and for most of them the Delirium is still going to be a problem #1. Four highly trained Best of the Best of the Best style guys (so an A-Team one might say) with 5s all over the place of with automatic weapons getting the drop on an 'average' recently changed Garou Cliath are going to be a threat to that Garou yes. In fact that's why Pentex First teams are considered a problem because they are stats Atts 3-4 with abilities 3-5 carrying the very tools to make them a threat.<br /> <br /> Although it's worth noting that things like a flack jacket are Armour 4, Dex -2 NOT Armour 5 Dex -3 which is full riot gear. The defensive options in combat for Werewolf are also different to Mage (and I think Vampire, need to check) as there is no way to go 'full defensive' so that also twists the matrix a little. However armour also doesn't guarantee a soak roll vs Agg in WTA combat system either unless it's talen or fetish armour (or otherwise specifically constructed) much like a Bullet Proof Vest is really handy vs a Gun but less so vs a Knife, it comes down to the ST call. Also W20 doesn't have some of the combat options that appeared in 2nd ed or revised when it comes to called shots (oh it has called shots +2 diff effects agreed with ST) where 4HLs passed Soak to a limb was limb removed, aka instant death if the head was targeted and you were mortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, when you actually read the actual requirements off the various US Armed Forces recruitment sites the sheer physical prowess of members of the armed forces relative to those of us in the largely sedentary west is pretty amazing.<br /> <br /> I'm reminded of the Mythbusters episode where they were testing out video game myths and did the whole thing with DOOM and the &quot;hyperspace arsenal.&quot; Jamie and Adam are reasonably fit guys and often times they based their &quot;plausible&quot; or &quot;busted&quot; on whether THEY could do what a soldier or similar was reputed to have done. They were ready to call the whole thing busted because they could barely drag their asses across the finish line with all the gear they had to carry. Then they brought in an actual Marine (since the DOOM character is supposed to be a Space Marine) and he blew through the course with all the gear in less than half their best times without even breaking a sweat.<br /> <br /> And I think there's a LOT of that in a lot of the RPG stats you see in many game systems (but which Mage and Vampire at least seem to have largely avoided)... people who have very little contact with the actual armed forces making a bunch of half-assed judgements of military ability based on what THEY think is physically fit... when their definition of &quot;fit&quot; actually wouldn't even pass basic training.<br /> <br /> -----<br /> <br /> While Werewolf is a bit vague with saying the armor might not stop all aggravated damage, both Mage and Vampire are consistent that armor will stop things like the teeth and claws of supernatural critters even if the damage is aggravated, but will NOT stop fire, electricity, radiation or other similar aggravated damage effects. Two of of three, including the most recent version, say essentially the same thing... Armor stops direct damage (unless you make a called shot), but not AoE's.<br /> <br /> Its also worth noting for crossover purposes that Mage and Werewolf use the same basic armor values while Vampire still uses the older Class 1-5 armor list from earlier editions (largely, I expect because it was the first and they didn't plan for it to be anything but a one-shot nostalgia product until the Kickstarter way over-performed their expectations while the later 20A projects put a little more effort into things). That said, Mage does adds a couple of additional armor categories and has actual descriptions of the armor types including Military Armor (5 armor; -2 Dex) that &quot;reflects a full suit of modern battle armor&quot; while listing the Riot Suit as &quot;police heavy body armor.&quot; (i.e. not quite as effective as military grade body armor).<br /> <br /> My hunch is that, because of the whole &quot;first change in your mid-teens&quot; aspect (along with the rage problem) and because they're mostly fighting spiritual corruption, they didn't feel the need to include things like military grade weapons (beyond the basics like assault rifles) and armor or actual combat rules for IFV's, tanks, attack helicopters and fighter jets (and their armaments) into Werewolf (and they certainly are very unlikely to turn up in a traditional Vampire game).<br /> <br /> But because a Technocracy-based campaign is 100% viable in a way that, say, a Pentex campaign isn't... Mage has a LOT more rules related to actual military hardware, explosives, etc. than either Vampire or Werewolf do by a wide margin. Wanna know how hard it would be for a Crinos to claw through a steel wall or total an automobile? Mage actually has mechanics for that. Wanna know how much damage a .50 caliber machine gun, flamethrower, 30mm Gatling cannon, 120mm cannon would do or how to handle 10 lb. of C4 on a building's foundation? Mage again is the only one of the three that has answers in its core book. Because if you're a Technocracy-based group and have the Requisitions for it, you totally CAN call in SEAL Team Six or tanks, artillery, combat helicopters and fighter jets to assist you.<br /> <br /> -----<br /> <br /> As to Dodging; Mage is the only one of the three that has the &quot;Total Defense&quot; option listed, but as its the most recent of the three 20th Anniversary rule sets, I'd presume the option to use the total defense action would be as available to vampires and werewolves as it would be to mages and mortals. Mage is also useful because its the ONLY one of the three that also deals with both countering the effects of other Nightfolk and Nightfolk being able to counter Magick (ex. Mind Effects normally use the Target's Willpower for difficulty instead of the usual sphere difficulty, but against Nightfolk and other mages the difficulty is Willpower+3). Similarly, <i>&quot;Night-Folk can use the equivalent of basic countermagick. Instead of Arete, such entities use their Wits + Occult as a dice pool. However, that dice pool cannot exceed the Gnosis or Rage (whichever is higher, for werecreatures), Willpower (vampires, spirits, wraiths, demons, hunters, and hedge wizards), Glamour (changelings and other fae), Mystic Shield (Bygones), or True Faith (faithful humans) Trait of the Night-Folk character.&quot;</i> This basic countermagick can also apply if a vampire was trying to counter a werewolf effect or visa-versa so that everyone is on the same footing.<br /> <br /> All of the above are just one of the reasons why, at least for our V/W/M20 crossovers, our group defaults to Mage when there's a difference in the rules... its had the advantage of being written with feedback from the other two available to them and its also the only one of the three that doesn't try to use its own supernatural rules (i.e. disciplines, gifts, or a stripped down version magick) to represent Vampires, Werewolves, Changelings or Wraiths in their antagonists' section and instead makes references to the other settings directly for the rules you should use for them. As such, they don't &quot;cheat&quot; on how to handle the other Night-Folk in the game and had to include rules like those above on how those mechanics can interact. Its also big enough to include the Vampire Creation Myth, Werewolf's Cosmology (objectively true for the Fera, because the Otherworlds always reflect our beliefs, but not all mages see the cosmos as they're wired to do) and Demons' &quot;multiple levels of reality&quot; cosmologies all at the same time (the Dreaming, the Shadowlands/Tempest, the Messengers and Yama Kings also fit without the slightest effort).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-02T04:23:30-07:00","id":1155014,"title":"","content":"I'm more than passingly familiar with Mage and the extent that is has certain rules within its core books and yes I recall the military armour on that list (which is I believe at the very edge of Device hood). <br /> <br /> More importantly I was talking about starting and combat rules parity etc. we are given some examples from various sources because they are written with that sources direct systems in mind as well as the descriptors given for the stats themselves. This is why basic Soldiers tend to come in at physical 3s (or primary for a start out mortal character playing one) and Elite come in with 4s (any one of which is Exceptional, so all 4 of which is pretty damned good) the reason you don't tend to see 5s in the mortal array is because that's human maximum. I've had the privilege of knowing a couple of Royal Marines, one Paratrooper and couple of ex SAS. The stories they've told from active postings plus things I've seen them do is pretty hair raising but none of them were likely to go into something like Worlds Strongest Man or the Iron Man contests, none of them would be becoming a Circue De Soeil performer and of the SAS guys I'm willing to believe one of them had Stamina 5 based on his injury scars (ironically not from in combat). <br /> <br /> When it comes to things like Pentex First Teams there's a lot of variance but they are trained as an Elite Special Ops Force and the ones in like the Amazon War get some seriously grim starting and then add a bunch of Fomori powers and Fetish/Talen armour and gear on top of that. But the point is they are a threat. <br /> <br /> A team of special forces vs a young Garou? If they for some reason don't succumb to the Delirium they are totally a credible threat to that one werewolf and even then there's even odds that team will take casualties or life changing injuries. That's 4-1 we've got the drop on you, going first in full gear advantage vs average stats (for human so that's tertiary priority) Garou with no gifts that effect the immediate situation nor fetish for the same and no real help except for extra WP points from Totem. A group of similarly delirium immune soldiers also pose a threat but will get more badly mauled and literally anyone else is walking arterial spray and little wet chunks of monkey meat. That's against a Rage 2 Garou by the way (as theurge are the Least 'combat' Auspice, ragabash having stealth Gift options and liking things like high Dex). As soon as you put some XP into that character with a focus towards a fight, the Auspice goes to something more aggressive etc the numbers begin to shift. <br /> <br /> The same applies to Vampires and Mages. Forces, Matter or Correspondence with the right Rotes (or paradigm) can render a huge amoun of a SpecForce teams abilities and advantages useless. Vampires suffer far less from bullets and against non Agg damage can get their own good soak values plus there's a whole bunch of disciplines that can even the odds at low levels. However an elite team with the drop on any of these is gong to be a problem. They are supposed to be but against a more focused or experience character they might not be a problem, in themselves anyway, for long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-02T06:23:47-07:00","id":1155034,"title":"","content":"As someone with multiple friends and family members who are either currently active duty army servicemen, or were in the past, I think you guys are grossly overestimating what they're capable of. The descriptions I've seen here are closer to the Army Rangers, or some equivalent military special ops, than a standard infantryman. Sure, they have rigorous personal training in boot camp and have to maintain a similar physical health level, but they're not nearly as exceptional as some of you have made it seem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-02T08:26:00-07:00","id":1155071,"title":"","content":"That's the deal of a system with 5 categories of a certain stat. You necessarily would have to &quot;bag&quot; several levels of expertise under the same rough level. Mechanically, having that contested one extra dice on strenght doesn't do a whole lot in combat, it's just one extra dice on damage/grapple.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-02T10:07:09-07:00","id":1155105,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1149965-game-line-compatibility?p=1150509#post1150509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, you cannot have both Werewolf's cosmology and Mage's cosmology coexist in the same setting. Werewolf gives a hard answer for existence in the form of animism - spirits make reality. Mage ascribes the basis of reality to something else, and puts humanity on more than a little bit of a pedestal in determining what reality is. The two are matter and antimatter, and, fittingly enough, will make your game explode if you try to incorporate both as correct.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see that kind of incompatibility. That kind of animism is the Werewolves' paradigm and they act accordingly, and because they are half-spirit by nature they can't see things any differently. Mage doesn't just drown itself in relativism, it also states that there is some kind of underlying reality that was overwritten through the ages by men's evolution; Werewolf says that the relationship between spirits and humans flows both ways, with spirits both being created by (and feeding off) human minds and affecting their lives to push them toward whatever purpose they may have.<br /> <br /> The issue is not setting compatibility and not even the power level, but the theme and the narrative style. A crossover campaign needs to focus over an aspect and by doing so you need to strip something off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13673-maris-streck","name":"Maris Streck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13673_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-02T13:23:36-07:00","id":1155173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maris Streck</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see that kind of incompatibility. That kind of animism is the Werewolves' paradigm and they act accordingly, and because they are half-spirit by nature they can't see things any differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, speeches like &quot;they can't see different because it's their paradigm&quot; wouldn't even be a thing if weren't for Mage so...I disagree.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">The issue is not setting compatibility and not even the power level, but the theme and the narrative style. A crossover campaign needs to focus over an aspect and by doing so you need to strip something off.</span></span><br /> <br /> </div> </div> </div>I agree, incompatible metaphysics isn't what makes crossover hard. If werewolves or mages are right it's something that will never come up as anything more than a philosophical debate. Sure, the mage may see the totem as a green alien, but everyone knows mages are crazy...<br /> <br /> You may have to touch some things in the Umbra, though, like Mages and Garou having different expectations about where physical outer space its located. But that may never come up, it's not like Garou and Mages are constantly going to the Deep Umbra<br /> <br /> Crosover it's hard because:<br /> A. Rules<br /> B. Themes<br /> <br /> It's worth to note that the themes that these games &quot;are supposed to portray&quot;...are often ignored in single splat games anyway. As long as you agree on a theme that all the players can live with, everything works fine: For instance, vampions with furry superheros can work together as long as werewolves accept to be open minded about vampires. This totally murders the themes of their respective games, but hey, that's not crossover's, people like to play that way.<br /> <br /> And whith the rules: You need to adress many things. Armors, combat techniques and weapons being different in each 20th aniversary book are the simplest part. Powers are the hard part.<br /> <br /> People it's going to play with the expectation that their powers are going to work as intended. You need to understand that with each new splat you introduce new posibilities and new mechanics. A few examples<ul><li>The enigma that could take a vampire a whole story to solve may be solved by a Mage with one Arete roll using one or two of her gazillon senses</li> <li>The enemy that a splat would find &quot;challenging&quot; may be puny from the perspective of another splat. or instance: Standard Hit Marks are a problem for normal mages, but aren't so impresive against werewolves or even vampires. A BSD ambush that would be &quot;challenging&quot; for the average pack may annihilate a group of unprepared vampires or mages .</li> <li>The epic enemy that would require a vampire coterie or werewolf pack a lot of effort to beat may be vulnerable to a power of other splat (and if not, surely a single mage is able to drown it in a pile of ritual successes <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )</li> </ul>You need to prepare having in mind what the characters can and can't do. Doing so otherwise leads to the &quot;evil mages turned my Elder into a chair&quot; type of problems (and it's many variations, for instance, &quot;A werewolf ripped X appart with total ease&quot;) without anyone being a powergamer...perhaps those players merely expected their powers to work as intended. A good resource it's to combine antagonists from all games, I.E: the Correspondence scrying that would've worked so well against the BSD may be countered by a Nephandi.<br /> <br /> To be prepared for this kind of eventuality, you need to be versant on the rules of all games (and supplements) implied.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 11-02-2017, 04:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}